// "Key takeaways" shown at the top of each article (keyed by post slug). 3 short, factual bullets.
module.exports = {
  'wordpress-website-cost-india': ['Most small business WordPress sites in India cost roughly ₹15,000–₹40,000 to build, plus yearly domain and hosting.', 'Unique layouts, custom design, features like payments or bookings, and content work drive the build price more than the number of pages.', 'Budget for yearly costs too (domain, hosting renewals, licences and maintenance), and send a clear brief to get an accurate fixed quote.'],
  'wordpress-vs-wix-vs-shopify': ['WordPress suits most service businesses, content-heavy sites and stores that want low running costs, Indian payment gateways and full ownership.', 'Shopify suits product brands that want a hosted store with hosting and security handled, and accept a monthly plan, app fees and possible extra transaction fees.', 'Wix suits very small DIY sites that will not grow much; it is the easiest to start with but the hardest to leave, and its payment options in India are limited.'],
  'why-is-my-wordpress-site-slow': ['Diagnose before fixing: PageSpeed Insights, browser DevTools and Query Monitor show whether hosting, plugins, the theme, images, scripts or the database is the bottleneck.', 'Slow hosting, missing caching and oversized images are the usual culprits, followed by heavy themes, page builders, plugins and third-party scripts.', 'A slow admin, a slow WooCommerce store or a site that slowed after an update each have specific causes, so back up and test fixes on staging.'],
  'signs-wordpress-site-hacked': ['Check Google Safe Browsing, Search Console\'s Security issues report and a site: search for spam pages first.', 'Unknown admins, PHP files in uploads and redirects that only hit mobile visitors are strong signs of a hack.', 'If hacked: back up, tell your host, change every password from a clean device, then clean before asking Google to review.'],
  'clinic-website-checklist-for-doctors': ['A clinic website earns trust through details patients can check: degrees with university and year, registration number, real clinic photos and genuine reviews.', 'Make booking effortless on a phone with tap-to-call, WhatsApp and a short form that asks only for name, phone and preferred session, never symptoms or reports.', 'Give each major treatment its own page, keep your Google Business Profile consistent with the site, and keep every medical claim factual and within current NMC guidance.'],
  'freelancer-vs-agency-web-developer': ['For most small and medium business websites, an experienced freelancer gives direct communication and lower cost; an agency fits projects that need branding, content, ads and development managed together.', 'The bigger risks are the same either way: vague scope, accounts in someone else\'s name and no plan for support, so get scope, ownership and after-launch terms in writing.', 'Judge any developer by live sites you can open on your phone, who will actually do the work, and how clearly they answer your questions before you pay.'],
  'woocommerce-vs-shopify-india': ['WooCommerce suits Indian stores that want low running costs, only the gateway\'s fee on payments, flexible COD and GST rules, strong content and full ownership, with a developer handling upkeep.', 'Shopify suits stores that want hosting, security and updates handled, and accept a monthly plan, monthly app fees and any Shopify transaction fee that applies to third-party gateways.', 'Both work with Indian gateways, UPI, COD and couriers such as Shiprocket; the real differences are how features are added, how much you can customise and how easily you can leave.'],
  'wordpress-maintenance-checklist': ['Weekly jobs are quick checks: updates after a backup, confirming the backup ran, a test enquiry and a look at uptime alerts.', 'The monthly checklist is the core of maintenance: security scan, user review, Site Health, Search Console, speed, broken links and a database tidy-up.', 'Quarterly and yearly tasks stop slow problems: an untested backup, abandoned plugins, an old PHP version, expired licences and surprise renewals.'],
  'get-more-enquiries-from-your-website': ['First work out whether you have too few visitors, visitors who do not act, or enquiries that are lost or untracked, because each needs a different fix.', 'Say what you do and for whom in the headline, put one clear call to action on every page, and offer WhatsApp, a tap-to-call number and a short form.', 'Real proof, a page for each service, a fast mobile site and tracking every form, call and WhatsApp click turn the same traffic into more enquiries.'],
  'local-seo-guide-small-business-india': ['Google ranks local results on relevance, distance and prominence, so a complete, accurate Business Profile and a steady flow of genuine reviews do most of the early work.', 'Keep your name, address and phone identical everywhere, give each main service its own page, and build area or branch pages only where you genuinely serve or operate.', 'Track calls, direction requests and website visits from your profile, plus enquiries by area, so you know which parts of local SEO actually bring customers.'],
  'b2b-manufacturer-website-guide': ['Plan the site for everyone in the buying process: engineers need specifications and drawings, purchase teams a quick RFQ, quality teams verifiable certificates and approvers a company profile.', 'Applications pages, a technical downloads library and a separate route for dealers are the parts most manufacturer websites miss, and each one answers a different kind of buyer.', 'B2B searches are specific, so match each type of query, from product plus specification to application and comparison, with a page written for it, and show only certifications you currently hold.'],
  'elementor-vs-gutenberg': ['Elementor gives visual drag-and-drop design with popups, forms and theme building in Pro; Gutenberg is built into WordPress, lighter by default and stores content natively.', 'Speed depends more on how a site is built than on the editor: a careful Elementor build can be fast, and a bloated block theme can be slow.', 'Choose Elementor for design-heavy marketing pages edited by non-technical teams, Gutenberg for content sites and long-term flexibility, or combine them on one site.'],
  'choose-wordpress-hosting-india': ['Decide the type of hosting first, shared, managed WordPress, cloud or VPS, based on your traffic, whether you sell online and who will look after the server.', 'For visitors in India, compare server location, server software and caching, PHP version, off-site backups and WordPress-aware support before you compare prices.', 'Work out the three-year cost including renewal and GST, read the limits behind "unlimited", and keep the hosting account in your own business name.'],
  'landing-page-mistakes-google-ads': ['Most wasted ad spend comes from the page, not the ad: a headline that ignores the ad, a slow mobile load, a long form or tracking that counts the wrong thing.', 'Fix tracking first, because without it you cannot tell which change helped, then fix anything broken or against policy, then speed, message match, the form and proof.', 'Change one thing at a time, judge it on real enquiries rather than clicks, and keep a short log so you know what worked and what to try next.'],
  'how-long-to-build-wordpress-website': ['Counted from when content is ready, a landing page usually takes 3–5 working days, a small business site 1–2 weeks and a WooCommerce store 2–4 weeks.', 'Late content, scattered feedback and features added midway cause more delays than the build itself, so prepare text, photos and logins before day one.', 'To hit a launch date, work backwards from it, start third-party approvals in the first week, and launch the essential pages first if time is short.'],
  'redesign-website-without-losing-rankings': ['Benchmark and crawl the old site first, then map every URL that changes to its closest new equivalent with a single 301 redirect, never a blanket redirect to the homepage.', 'Keep the content, titles, headings, internal links and schema of the pages that already rank, and keep staging out of Google with a password rather than relying on noindex.', 'On launch day remove the noindex, test every old URL and check canonicals, then watch Search Console daily for two weeks and fix 404s and drops quickly.'],
  'accept-online-payments-wordpress-india': ['Open an account with an Indian payment gateway in your business name, complete KYC and publish your policy pages, then connect the gateway to WooCommerce, a payment form or payment links.', 'Set up webhooks so orders paid by UPI are marked paid even if the customer never returns to your site, and check the settlement cycle so you know when money reaches your bank.', 'Test in test mode first, including failed, cancelled and refunded payments on a phone, then switch to live keys and confirm with a real low-value order.'],
  'signs-you-need-a-new-website': ['The clearest signs are a site that is hard to use on a phone, slow, dated, impossible to edit, insecure, short of enquiries or no longer describing your business.', 'One or two specific problems can usually be fixed with a refresh; a site that fails on phones, cannot be updated safely or no longer fits the business usually needs rebuilding.', 'Before any redesign, record which pages bring visits and enquiries today, so the new site keeps them and redirects every address that changes.'],
  'wordpress-security-checklist': ['Most WordPress hacks come from automated bots exploiting outdated plugins and weak logins, so updates, strong passwords and 2FA block the majority of attacks.', 'Harden the site itself: turn off file editing, set sensible file permissions, block PHP in uploads and disable XML-RPC if nothing on your site needs it.', 'Run one security plugin, keep tested off-site backups and monitor uptime and Search Console, because hardening reduces risk but never removes it.'],
  'core-web-vitals-explained': ['Core Web Vitals measure loading (LCP, good at 2.5 seconds or less), responsiveness (INP, good at 200 milliseconds or less) and visual stability (CLS, good at 0.1 or less).', 'Google judges them on field data from real Chrome users at the 75th percentile over a rolling 28 days, so fixes take up to four weeks to show in Search Console.', 'Find the worst metric on your most important page type, fix the cause, usually server response, the hero image, scripts or missing image sizes, then confirm with lab tests and wait for field data.'],
  'setup-google-analytics-search-console': ['Search Console shows how people find you in Google; GA4 shows what they do on your site. You need both, and both should be owned by a business Google account.', 'Verify a Domain property in Search Console with a DNS record, add GA4 to WordPress one way only (Site Kit, a plugin or Tag Manager), and check it in Realtime.', 'Link them in GA4 under Admin, Product links, Search Console links, then publish the Search Console reports from the Library and mark enquiries as key events.'],
  'migrate-wix-to-wordpress': ['Wix designs cannot be exported, so a move to WordPress is a rebuild on staging with your text, images, blog posts and products carried across.', 'List every Wix URL before you start, then match it in WordPress permalinks or redirect it with a 301 to its closest new page, never all to the homepage.', 'Keep the Wix site and its plan running until the new site is live and checked, and copy every DNS record, including email, before the domain switches.'],
  'google-business-profile-checklist': ['Get the foundations exactly right first: your real business name, the most specific primary category, an accurate address or service area, hours and a link to the most relevant page.', 'Reviews are the biggest ongoing factor, so ask every happy customer with your direct review link, reply to every review and never buy or incentivise them.', 'Manage the profile like a routine: weekly review replies, monthly photos, posts and detail checks, and a quarterly look at categories, services and performance.'],
  'how-to-write-website-content': ['Plan before you write: decide who each page is for, the one action it should lead to, and the questions customers ask before they buy.', 'Write service pages first, then the homepage, About and contact pages, using plain words, the customer first and a specific fact behind every claim.', 'Make every page scannable on a phone, use the words customers search for naturally, and fill in a short content brief for each page before drafting.'],
  'website-design-mistakes': ['The costliest mistakes are the basic ones: a headline that does not say what you do, pages that are hard to use on a phone, slow loading and contact details visitors cannot find.', 'Most of them can be fixed without a full redesign: rewrite the headline, add clear calls to action, simplify forms, use real photos and show genuine proof.', 'If several mistakes stack up and the site was not built for phones, a redesign on staging with proper redirects usually costs less than patching it.'],
  'wordpress-vs-custom-coded-website': ['WordPress is a ready-made system you build on with themes and plugins; a custom-coded site is written from scratch, so every feature, editing screen and change is built by developers.', 'For most business websites, blogs and online stores, WordPress launches faster, costs less and lets your team edit content; custom code earns its cost for web applications.', 'Neither is automatically faster, more secure or better for SEO: those depend on how well the site is built, hosted and maintained.'],
  'hotel-website-direct-bookings': ['Many guests who find you on an OTA search your name next, so the website has to beat the OTA listing on photos, room detail, trust and how easy it is to book.', 'Give guests a reason to book direct that fits your OTA agreements, such as perks or packages, and check any rate parity terms before advertising a lower price.', 'Treat WhatsApp, your Google listing and returning guests as direct channels, and measure the share of bookings that come without commission each month.'],
  'website-for-lawyers-and-chartered-accountants': ['Clients arrive with a specific problem, so give each practice area a page that describes the situations you handle, the process, the documents needed and the limits of your scope.', 'Show credentials clients can verify, such as enrolment or membership numbers, and keep wording within Bar Council or ICAI rules: factual, with no success claims for advocates.', 'Treat every enquiry as confidential from the first click: ask for an outline only, no documents, run conflict checks before discussing details, and collect as little personal data as you need.'],
  'solar-company-website-guide': ['Give homeowners, housing societies and businesses their own pages, because they ask different questions, decide differently and expect different proof.', 'Keep subsidy and savings information honest: explain the process as you handle it, link to the official portal, date any figures and label every calculator result as an estimate.', 'Make the site survey the main goal, and build trust with dated project pages, plain warranty explanations and only the brands, certifications and empanelment you genuinely hold.'],
  'white-label-wordpress-development-agencies': ['In white-label development, your agency sells and manages the project while a partner builds the WordPress site under your brand, with no credits and no client contact.', 'Agree the basics in writing before the first project: scope and revision rounds, the pricing model, an NDA, who owns accounts and code, and how feedback flows.', 'Quality comes from process: a clear brief, staging builds, the partner\'s QA before your review, one consolidated feedback list per round and a handover note in your branding.'],
  'real-estate-website-must-have-features': ['Build every project page from one template, with configurations, carpet areas, zoomable floor plans, checked location facts, RERA details and a clear price or price-on-request route.', 'Make the next step easy and measurable: site-visit booking with slots, a brochure gated only where it earns a lead, WhatsApp and calls, all landing in one sheet or CRM tagged by source.', 'Builders, plotted developers and brokers need different extras, so launch with the essentials for your business and add calculators, portals and tours only when they solve a real problem.'],
  'school-coaching-website-what-parents-look-for': ['Parents compare several institutes in one sitting, so the website must answer results, faculty, fees, safety, timings and location quickly, with details they can check.', 'Results persuade only when they are verifiable: name the exam, year and course taken, with consent, and leave out anything you cannot back up.', 'Plan the admission enquiry as a sequence, from form to counsellor call, demo or visit, registration and confirmation, so no family is lost between steps.'],
  'temple-ngo-website-online-donations': ['Make a donation form connected to a payment gateway the main route, with a UPI QR code for people at the temple and bank details for large donors.', 'Gateways activate only after checking your registration, PAN, a bank account in the organisation\'s name and the policy pages on your website, so prepare these first.', 'Test every amount, payment method and receipt email in test mode, then make a small real donation before announcing the page.'],
  'startup-website-checklist': ['Before building, put the domain and every account in the company name, write a one-sentence positioning line and choose one primary call to action.', 'Launch with a homepage, product, pricing if you sell self-serve, about, contact and legal pages, with sign-ups or demo requests tracked from the first day.', 'Add investor, careers, customer story and blog pages when you have something real to put on them, on templates your team can reuse without a developer.'],
  'figma-to-wordpress-designer-guide': ['Name colour, type and spacing styles or variables by role, and use auto layout everywhere, because both map straight onto global styles and Flexbox containers in WordPress.', 'Build buttons, cards, fields and navigation as components with every state, and design at least desktop and mobile frames for the homepage and each unique template.', 'Hand over SVG icons, full-resolution photos, web-licensed fonts, real content, documented interactions and a page and template list, so nothing has to be guessed.'],
  'industrial-website-product-catalogue': ['Plan the category tree before any design: product type first, then family and model, with applications and industries as a second way in, named in the terms buyers search.', 'Every product or family page needs a real text spec table, drawings, a downloadable datasheet and a Request a Quote button that carries the product name into the form.', 'WooCommerce works well as a catalogue without prices, giving you attributes for filters, bulk CSV import and a quote basket, with Product and Breadcrumb schema for search.'],
  'domain-hosting-ssl-explained': ['The domain is your address, DNS points it to your hosting and your email, hosting stores and serves the website, and SSL encrypts the connection.', 'Each part renews separately, so put every renewal date in one place, turn on auto-renew and keep payment details current.', 'Register the domain, hosting and email in your business name with your own login, and give your developer access rather than ownership.'],
  'website-maintenance-cost-india': ['Freelance WordPress maintenance plans for small and medium sites in India typically cost about ₹1,500 to ₹6,000 a month, with stores and business-critical sites at the higher end.', 'The yearly cost of running a WordPress site is your domain (₹500 to ₹1,200) and hosting (₹2,500 to ₹15,000) renewals, plus any premium licences and a maintenance plan if you choose one.', 'Compare plans on what is actually done: tested updates, off-site backups with restores, security, uptime monitoring, form and checkout checks, content time and a monthly report.'],
  'landing-page-vs-website': ['A website serves many visitors with many pages; a landing page is one focused page for one audience, one offer and one action, usually for an ad campaign.', 'Sending paid clicks to your homepage usually wastes budget; a landing page or a focused service page that repeats the ad\'s promise converts better.', 'Most businesses running ads need both: the website for credibility and search traffic, landing pages to turn paid clicks into enquiries.'],
  'get-website-indexed-google-faster': ['Link every new page from pages Google already crawls often, such as the homepage, main menu and indexed posts, and keep it in a sitemap with an accurate lastmod.', 'Use Search Console\'s Request indexing only for your most important URLs, because the daily quota is small and repeated requests do not speed anything up.', 'Google indexes selectively on new sites, so fewer, fuller and unique pages on a fast server get indexed sooner than many thin ones; use IndexNow to tell Bing directly.'],
  'how-to-choose-wordpress-theme': ['Decide how the site will be built and edited first, Elementor, the block editor or a custom design, because that decides which kind of theme fits.', 'Check speed on a plain page, recent updates, support replies, reviews, required plugins and lock-in before you look at demo designs.', 'Use free themes from WordPress.org or premium ones from the developer, never nulled copies, and test any theme switch on a staging copy.'],
  'restaurant-website-online-ordering': ['Choose the ordering route your team can actually run: WhatsApp orders for small menus and regulars, a WooCommerce menu with online payment once manual replies start slipping.', 'Plan portions, add-ons, delivery areas, ordering hours and payment before anything is built, because these choices decide how every dish and checkout is set up.', 'Make sure every order is seen and confirmed quickly, by email, app alerts or WhatsApp, and give regulars a simple reason to order direct instead of through an app.'],
  'personal-brand-website-professionals': ['A personal brand website should say in one sentence who you help and with what, then back it up with credentials visitors can check, not adjectives.', 'Professional rules shape the copy: doctors, advocates and CAs should check their regulator\'s current guidance on advertising and testimonials before launch.', 'Give visitors one clear next step, such as a consultation request, a discovery call or a free self-assessment, and make it work on a phone in a couple of taps.'],
  'equipment-rental-website-guide': ['Organise the fleet the way buyers specify it, usually by capacity first, and give each capacity band or equipment type its own page with uses, price factors and FAQs.', 'For SEO, match "on rent" and "hire" searches with capacities and your city, keep your Google Business Profile complete, and only create area pages where you genuinely serve.', 'Make enquiring instant with a call-back button in the header, an enquiry button after every section, click-to-call, page-specific WhatsApp messages and a short form.'],
  'directory-website-wordpress': ['Plan the data first: a listing post type, taxonomies for how people browse, such as category and location, and custom fields for every detail a visitor needs.', 'Decide the search routes before choosing a theme or plugin, then give each route its own archive page with filters, sorting and a map where it helps.', 'Google indexes useful listings, not thousands of near-empty ones, so launch with fewer, complete listings, write real category intros and keep filter URLs out of the index.'],
  'lead-magnets-newsletter-small-business': ['A good lead magnet answers one question your buyers have before they hire you, such as a checklist, price guide or calculator, and is delivered the moment they ask.', 'Build it a short landing page with a clear consent line, deliver it on the thank-you page and by email or WhatsApp, and track sign-ups separately from enquiries.', 'Follow up with a few useful messages that lead naturally to your service, and only keep messaging people who clearly agreed to hear from you.'],
  'woocommerce-store-launch-checklist': ['Start the slow tasks early: payment gateway KYC, courier accounts, GST rates from your CA and policy pages can hold up a launch more than the build itself.', 'Run test orders for every path, including failed and abandoned payments, COD, out-of-state GST, coupons and refunds, before switching the gateway to live keys.', 'On launch day, place and refund one real low-value order, delete test orders, open the store to search engines, then check orders against the gateway daily for two weeks.'],
  'schema-markup-explained': ['Schema markup is structured data, usually JSON-LD, that tells search engines exactly what a page describes; it helps Google understand a page but guarantees neither rankings nor rich results.', 'Most small business sites need Organization or LocalBusiness, Service or Product, Article and BreadcrumbList; FAQ rich results are now limited and self-serving review stars are not shown.', 'Let one SEO plugin generate the core schema, add custom JSON-LD only for gaps, mark up only what is visible on the page, and test every template after changes.'],
  'multilingual-wordpress-website-hindi-english': ['Use a translation plugin such as Polylang, WPML or TranslatePress so each language gets its own URL, usually a folder like /hi/, with a language switcher.', 'Translate everything a visitor touches: menus, forms, confirmation emails, SEO titles and image alt text, and have a fluent speaker write or check the Hindi.', 'Let the plugin add hreflang tags, never redirect visitors by location, and start with the pages that bring enquiries before translating the whole site.'],
  'whatsapp-on-business-website': ['A wa.me click-to-chat link with your number in international format and an optional pre-filled message is free, needs no API and works with the WhatsApp Business app.', 'Use page-specific pre-filled messages so you know what each lead wants, and place WhatsApp beside your main calls to action as well as in a small floating button.', 'Track WhatsApp clicks as a GA4 key event and label chats in WhatsApp Business, because a click is a strong signal but not the same as a conversation.'],
  'image-optimization-wordpress': ['Resize images to the largest size they are shown at, convert photos to WebP or AVIF and compress them with one tool, so no page ships multi-megabyte photos to phones.', 'Treat the main image at the top of the page differently: never lazy-load it, give it fetchpriority="high" and set its width and height, then lazy-load everything below the fold.', 'Fix an existing site by finding the heaviest images first, bulk-optimising with backups of the originals, and checking that sliders, CSS backgrounds and product galleries are covered too.'],
  'website-accessibility-basics': ['Accessibility means people can use your site whatever their eyesight, hearing, movement or device, and WCAG level AA is the usual target.', 'Most problems on small business sites are basic and fixable: low contrast, missing alt text, menus that fail with a keyboard, skipped headings and unlabelled form fields.', 'Automated checkers find only some issues, so add a keyboard test, a zoom test and a quick screen reader test, and build the fixes into your theme rather than patching pages.'],
  'website-brief-template': ['A website brief covers goals, audience, pages, features, content, examples, a budget range, timeline and account access, and one or two pages is enough.', 'Be specific where cost hides: list every page and feature, say who writes the content, and give an honest budget range so developers can suggest the right approach.', 'Send the same brief to every developer, ask each to quote against it line by line, and the quotes become comparable instead of guesses.'],
  'on-page-seo-checklist': ['On-page SEO starts before writing: match the type of page Google already shows for the search, and give each search one page so your own pages never compete.', 'Get the visible basics right on every page: a unique title and description, one clear H1, a direct answer near the top, real experience and a clear next step.', 'Then check the technical layer: internal links in and out, compressed images with alt text, matching schema, a self-referencing canonical, no stray noindex, and fast loading on a phone.'],
  'contact-form-not-getting-enquiries': ['Submit your own form today and check the email arrives; many forms fail silently because WordPress sends unauthenticated email that lands in spam or nowhere.', 'Send form emails through SMTP from an address on your own domain, keep SPF and DKIM in place, and store every submission so nothing depends on email alone.', 'If the form works but nobody uses it, shorten it, put it where visitors decide, offer WhatsApp and phone alongside, and show proof and a reply time next to it.'],
  'essential-wordpress-plugins-business': ['Most business sites need one plugin, or a host feature, for each of SEO, security, backups, caching, images, forms with spam protection, email delivery and analytics.', 'Choose plugins that are recently updated, well supported and do one job well, and never run two plugins that do the same job.', 'The number of plugins matters less than their quality: delete what you do not use, avoid nulled copies, and update with a backup and a test.'],
  'website-traffic-dropped': ['Before changing anything, confirm the drop is real by comparing GA4 with Search Console and with the same weeks last year, since broken tracking and seasonality cause many false alarms.', 'Narrow it down by channel, pages, queries and the exact date, then match the pattern to a cause: a site change, an indexing block, a Google update, a hack or competitors.', 'Check Manual actions, Security issues and the Pages report in Search Console early, and fix the cause you find rather than rewriting or deleting pages in a panic.'],
  'ai-search-optimization-website': ['AI Overviews and AI assistants mostly draw on pages that search engines can crawl and index, so the foundation is the same as good SEO: clear, trustworthy pages that answer questions directly.', 'Write quotable answers, keep your business details identical everywhere, add accurate structured data, and check that robots.txt and security tools don\'t block the crawlers you want.', 'llms.txt is an optional, unofficial proposal with unproven benefit; measure what you can through Search Console, GA4 referrals and asking new enquiries how they found you.'],
  'website-for-interior-designers-architects': ['Make the project page the heart of the site: the brief, the site as found, key decisions, materials, your exact scope and real photos, captioned so visitors can match your work to their taste.', 'Large images can stay sharp and fast if they are exported at sensible sizes, served in modern formats and loaded only when needed.', 'Ask enquiry type, project stage, budget and timeline so you know what each lead needs before you call, and keep design partner and careers enquiries on separate forms.'],
  'website-for-gyms-fitness-studios': ['Members want location, schedules, memberships, trainers and photos of the space.', 'A free trial offer, online payments and class booking turn visitors into members.', 'Local SEO and a complete Google Business Profile bring in nearby searchers.'],
  'website-for-travel-agencies': ['Give every package its own page with a day-by-day itinerary, hotels or hotel category, meals, transfers, inclusions and exclusions, price basis, dates and cancellation terms.', 'Most trips are customised, so make the trip enquiry form the main action: destination, dates, departure city, travellers, hotel category and budget, followed up quickly on WhatsApp.', 'Mention registrations and memberships such as Ministry of Tourism recognition, IATA or TAAI only if genuinely held, and back them with reviews, a real office and clear payment and refund terms.'],
  'website-for-dentists': ['Patients want to know qualifications, treatments, costs, clinic quality and availability.', 'Treatment pages, before-and-after galleries (with consent) and reviews build trust.', 'Address dental anxiety clearly and keep content within professional guidelines.'],
  'portfolio-website-freelancers-creatives': ['A portfolio website you own is where serious clients check you out: show your best 6–12 pieces, explain your thinking in case studies and make the next step obvious.', 'Say who you work with and what you offer, add an about page with a real photo and real testimonials, and ask for project type, budget and timeline on the form.', 'Keep image-heavy portfolios fast with resized WebP images and lazy loading, and target your niche and location so the right clients find you on Google.'],
  'website-for-event-wedding-planners': ['Show real events with short stories, and explain your services and process.', 'Testimonials, vendor partners and a short enquiry form build trust and leads.', 'Compress large galleries and target local wedding and event searches.'],
  'wordpress-backup-restore-guide': ['A complete WordPress backup has two parts, the files (mainly wp-content and wp-config.php) and the database, and you need both to restore a working site.', 'Follow the 3-2-1 rule: host backups plus an automatic plugin or service backup stored off the server, such as UpdraftPlus to Google Drive, BlogVault or Jetpack VaultPress Backup.', 'Restore through your backup tool or hosting panel, or manually with File Manager and phpMyAdmin, and test a restore on staging every few months so you know it works.'],
  'woocommerce-product-page-optimization': ['Answer four questions on the first phone screen: what it is, the full price, when it will arrive and whether the store can be trusted.', 'Use consistent photos, clear variation swatches, specifications as attributes and a one-line delivery, COD and returns summary right beside Add to cart.', 'Keep the page fast, add honest reviews, and use GA4 views, add-to-carts and purchases per product to decide what to change next, one change at a time.'],
  'woocommerce-seo-guide': ['Map one main search term to each category and product, then give category pages real content, because they usually have the best chance of ranking for broader searches.', 'Use one SEO plugin, check that only one set of Product schema appears on each product page, and keep filter, sort and variation URLs canonicalised to the main page.', 'Keep temporarily out-of-stock products live, redirect discontinued ones to the closest alternative, and keep the store fast with caching that excludes cart and checkout.'],
  'get-more-google-reviews': ['Most happy customers will leave a review if they are asked personally, at the right moment, with a direct link, so make asking part of every handover, visit or delivery.', 'Don\'t offer anything in exchange, don\'t ask only the customers you expect to be happy, and never post reviews yourself; Google\'s policies forbid all three, at the time of writing.', 'Reply to every review, good or bad, and show genuine reviews on your website with a link to your profile, so a steady flow of reviews keeps working for you.'],
  'internal-linking-explained': ['Internal links help Google find your pages, understand them from the anchor text and judge which ones matter most, and they move visitors towards enquiring.', 'Organise pages as hubs and spokes, link from your strongest pages to new ones, and rely on descriptive links inside the body text rather than menus and widgets alone.', 'Find opportunities with a site: search, the Search Console Links report and your SEO plugin, and make linking a fixed step every time you publish or change a URL.'],
  'technical-seo-audit-wordpress': ['Start with what Google can see: the Search Console Pages report, URL Inspection, the WordPress visibility setting, robots.txt and a clean XML sitemap.', 'Then make sure every page has one clean version: HTTPS, single-step redirects, correct canonicals, no indexable duplicates and no broken internal links.', 'Prioritise by impact: fix indexing blockers and site-wide template issues first, then key-page speed, structured data, internal links and tidy-up work.'],
  'choose-domain-name-business': ['Pick a short name that matches your business, passes the phone test and works as an email address, on .in or .com depending on where your customers are.', 'Before buying, check trademarks, existing businesses, social handles and the domain\'s history, and avoid hyphens, numbers and spellings you would need to explain.', 'Register it in your business\'s own registrar account with a business email, turn on auto-renew and two-factor login, and compare renewal prices, not first-year offers.'],
  'website-for-export-businesses': ['Overseas buyers need detailed specs, proof of capability, certifications and export terms.', 'Quote forms should capture product, quantity and destination, with fast replies across time zones.', 'Clear English, optional translations, fast global hosting and product-level SEO win international enquiries.'],
  'why-elementor-sites-slow': ['Most slow Elementor sites are slow because of how they were built: nested containers, add-on packs, animations, full-size images and a heavy theme underneath.', 'Turn on Elementor\'s performance settings one at a time, remove add-ons and widgets you do not use, and rebuild only the heaviest sections with flat containers.', 'A slow Elementor editor is a separate problem, usually long pages, low PHP memory, weak hosting or a plugin, and page caching does nothing for it.'],
  'headless-wordpress-small-business': ['Headless WordPress keeps WordPress for managing content but builds the public website as a separate front end that fetches content through an API.', 'It can be fast and flexible, but you pay for two systems, lose most front-end plugins and visual editing, and need a developer for layout changes.', 'Most small businesses get the speed they want from a well-built traditional WordPress site with good hosting, caching and a CDN, or a simple static site.'],
  'measure-website-roi': ['Website ROI is the value of the customers your website brings in, minus what the website costs, divided by what it costs, measured over the same period.', 'The hard part is tracking: mark forms, calls, WhatsApp clicks and orders as GA4 key events, tag campaigns with UTMs and log every enquiry with its source.', 'Lead value is your average customer value multiplied by your close rate, and comparing it with cost per lead shows which channels and pages earn their keep.'],
  'website-for-diagnostic-labs': ['Patients want a searchable test menu with prices, preparation and report times.', 'Home sample collection booking, WhatsApp and clear locations drive bookings.', 'Protect health data, show real accreditations and target local test searches.'],
  'write-about-page-that-builds-trust': ['Open with who you help and what you do for them, then keep the story short and tell visitors who is behind the business, with real photos.', 'State only credentials you can prove, such as registrations, memberships and real projects, and back them with case studies and genuine reviews.', 'Finish with a clear next step, such as a call, WhatsApp or a quote form, because the About page is often visited just before someone decides to enquire.'],
  'write-service-pages-that-convert': ['Give every main service its own page, named the way customers search, because each service is a separate search with its own questions.', 'Answer questions in the order buyers ask them: what it is and who it is for, what is included, how it works, what affects the price, proof, FAQs and how to start.', 'Use the customer\'s words, real proof and one clear call to action repeated down the page, and keep every service page\'s text unique even when the layout is shared.'],
  'collect-display-customer-testimonials': ['Ask happy customers soon after a good outcome, with two or three specific questions, and get written permission for the exact wording, name and photo you will publish.', 'Show testimonials word for word next to the decisions they support, such as service pages, forms and landing pages, rather than only on a testimonials page.', 'Never invent, buy or reword testimonials to change their meaning, and do not mark them up as review stars, which Google does not show for reviews of your own business.'],
  'write-case-studies-business-website': ['Tell each story in a fixed order: the client, the problem, your approach, what you delivered and the outcome, then a quote and a clear next step.', 'State results only as far as you can verify them, with the client agreeing to publish; if there are no numbers, describe what visibly changed.', 'Give every case study its own page with a descriptive title, link it to the service and industry pages it supports, and get permission before naming anyone.'],
  'update-wordpress-safely': ['Before updating, take a backup you know how to restore, read the changelog for major versions and test big updates such as WooCommerce, page builders and core on staging.', 'Update one thing at a time at a quiet hour, usually plugins, then the theme, then core, and test pages, forms and checkout while logged out after each important update.', 'If an update breaks something, use recovery mode or rename the plugin folder to get back in, roll back that one update or restore the backup, and pause its auto-updates.'],
  'staging-sites-explained': ['A staging site is a private copy of your website for testing updates, redesigns and new features before visitors see them.', 'Your host\'s one-click staging (Hostinger, SiteGround, Softaculous on cPanel hosts) is the easiest option; the WP Staging plugin works almost anywhere.', 'Keep staging password-protected and out of Google, stop it emailing customers, and never push a staging database over a live WooCommerce store without a plan for new orders.'],
  'website-for-logistics-transport-companies': ['Business clients want services by mode, coverage, fleet, industries served and fast quotes.', 'Show certifications, insurance, safety practices and genuine client proof.', 'Target service and lane searches, and create location pages only where you truly operate.'],
  'website-for-salons-spas': ['Show a service menu with prices, real work photos and stylist profiles.', 'Online or WhatsApp booking, first-visit offers and packages drive appointments.', 'A strong Google Business Profile and regular reviews win local searches.'],
  'website-for-construction-companies': ['Clients look for similar completed projects, capacity, safety and reliability.', 'Project pages with photos, scope and duration plus a company profile support tenders.', 'Target service-and-city searches and keep photo-heavy pages fast.'],
  'website-for-furniture-businesses': ['Choose an online store, an enquiry catalogue or a hybrid based on what you sell.', 'Product pages need room photos, exact dimensions, materials, customisation and delivery details.', 'Showroom visits and a trade section for bulk buyers bring high-value orders.'],
  'website-for-jewellers': ['Show the price breakdown buyers expect: metal weight, purity, the rate used, making charges, stone charges and tax, with the rate coming from a source your store controls and a visible update time.', 'Sell lightweight and everyday pieces online, and use reserve-to-try, store appointments and WhatsApp video calls for bridal and high-value collections.', 'Build trust with accurate photos, purity and hallmark details stated in hedged, checkable terms, clear exchange and buyback policies, insured delivery and a secure checkout.'],
  'website-for-it-software-companies': ['Clear positioning (who you help, with what) beats generic buzzwords.', 'Service or product pages, case studies and industries pages are the core.', 'Demo or consultation calls to action, lead magnets and specific SEO bring leads.'],
  'website-for-d2c-food-brands': ['A food buyer cannot taste before ordering, so product pages must answer every label question: ingredients, nutrition, allergens, shelf life, storage, net quantity and your FSSAI details.', 'Ship what survives the journey: check serviceable pin codes and transit times, pack for heat and breakage, and keep short-shelf-life products to zones you can reach quickly.', 'Repeat orders are where a D2C food brand makes its margin, so plan subscriptions or reorder reminders, trial packs, genuine reviews and festival gifting from the start.'],
  'website-for-fashion-boutiques': ['Answer the fit question on every product page with a size chart in centimetres and inches, garment measurements, fit notes and the model\'s height and size worn.', 'Set up sizes and colours as proper WooCommerce variations with stock per size, and keep lookbooks and collections linked to products so a styled photo turns into an order.', 'Use Instagram for discovery and WhatsApp for styling questions, but send both to product pages, and keep COD, exchange and returns rules short, clear and the same everywhere.'],
  'website-for-pharmacies': ['Show locations, hours, services and fast phone and WhatsApp contact.', 'Online medicine sales are regulated, so confirm current requirements before selling online.', 'Protect prescription data and win "near me" searches with a strong Google Business Profile.'],
  'website-for-car-dealers-workshops': ['Dealers need detailed vehicle listings with photos, specs, price and test-drive buttons.', 'Workshops need service menus, pricing ranges and online booking with pickup options.', 'Tap-to-call, WhatsApp and local SEO matter because searches are often urgent.'],
  'website-for-printing-packaging-companies': ['Build the site as a catalogue: one page per product type with real sample photos, styles, materials, print processes, finishes, MOQ and applications, plus pages that prove your machinery and capacity.', 'Make quoting easy with an RFQ form that asks for size, quantity, material, finish and delivery city, accepts artwork files safely, and lets buyers request samples.', 'Publish artwork guidelines and dielines, state MOQs and proof approval rules plainly, and claim certifications or sustainability only where you genuinely hold them.'],
  'website-for-security-facility-management': ['Buyers look for services, sectors served, licences, training and scale.', 'Company profile downloads, real team photos and case studies support procurement.', 'A careers page helps recruitment, and location pages should reflect genuine operations.'],
  'wordpress-vs-webflow': ['Webflow suits design-led marketing sites where a team wants hosting, updates and security handled for a subscription, and can live within its CMS and plan limits.', 'WordPress suits sites that need ownership, a choice of hosting, Indian payment gateways, lots of content or specific features, and it has a far larger pool of developers in India.', 'Both can rank well and load fast; the bigger differences are how you pay, how easily you can move later and who will look after the site.'],
  'website-ready-for-google-ads': ['Before launch, give every ad group a matching landing page that loads fast on a mid-range phone, and test it on mobile data.', 'Set up and test conversion tracking for forms, calls and WhatsApp clicks with real test enquiries, so no click is counted twice or missed.', 'Check that forms deliver, policy pages and business details are in place, and someone is ready to answer leads during the hours your ads run.'],
  'website-copywriting-mistakes': ['Most weak website copy fails the same way: it is vague, talks about the business instead of the customer, and leaves the next step unclear.', 'Replace claims with specifics you can back up, answer the questions that stop people buying, and write in the words customers use when they search.', 'Test your copy by reading it aloud, checking it on a phone and asking whether a competitor could paste it onto their site unchanged.'],
  'woocommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery': ['WooCommerce has no built-in cart recovery: a plugin or email platform captures checkout details and sends reminders with a link that restores the cart.', 'Treat failed and pending payments separately from abandoned carts, and fix checkout friction first, because reminders cannot rescue a broken checkout.', 'Send two or three helpful reminders, keep discounts for the last one if at all, get opt-in for WhatsApp, and tag reminder links so you can measure recovered sales.'],
  'woocommerce-shipping-setup-india': ['Enter packed weights and box dimensions for every product, because couriers charge the higher of actual and volumetric weight and missing weights break rate rules.', 'Order zones from most specific to least, such as local pin codes, your state, remote regions and the rest of India, since WooCommerce uses the first zone that matches.', 'Allow COD only on the shipping methods and pin codes your courier supports, connect an aggregator or courier for labels and tracking, and test real pin codes before launch.'],
  'local-landing-pages-without-doorway-pages': ['A city or area page is fine when it covers a place you genuinely serve and says things true only of that place; near-identical pages with the city name swapped are doorway pages under Google\'s spam policies.', 'Each page needs real local substance: the branch or team that covers it, local jobs and reviews, coverage and response times, and answers to questions customers there actually ask.', 'If you have nothing local to say, one strong service page with a clear list of areas beats a thin page per city, and existing doorway pages are best merged and redirected.'],
  'privacy-policy-cookie-basics-india': ['If your site has a form, takes orders or runs analytics, publish a plain-language privacy policy that matches what you actually collect, share and keep.', 'India has no cookie-specific law, but EU and UK visitors generally need opt-in consent, and Google expects Consent Mode v2 signals for them if you use its ad features.', 'Ask separately for marketing consent on forms, block tracking scripts until visitors agree where consent applies, and have a lawyer or CA review the final wording.'],
  'wordpress-user-roles-explained': ['WordPress roles are bundles of capabilities: Administrators control everything, Editors manage all content, Authors publish their own posts, Contributors submit drafts and Subscribers only manage their profile.', 'Give each person the lowest role that fits, remember that Authors cannot edit pages, and use Shop Manager rather than Administrator for WooCommerce staff.', 'Keep administrators to the owner and the current developer, protect Editor and Shop Manager accounts with 2FA, and remove access the day someone leaves.'],
  'uptime-monitoring-explained': ['An uptime monitor checks your site from outside every few minutes and alerts you by email, SMS or app when it stops responding properly.', 'A basic status check misses many failures, so add a keyword check on a key page, plus SSL certificate and domain expiry alerts.', 'Free tools such as UptimeRobot, Better Stack and Jetpack Monitor cover most small business sites; shorter intervals and SMS alerts suit stores and sites running ads.'],
  'website-speed-indian-mobile-networks': ['Indian visitors often browse on budget Android phones over 4G connections that vary by hour and place, so latency, JavaScript and page weight matter more than an office Wi-Fi test suggests.', 'Test the way customers browse: PageSpeed field data, Chrome DevTools with Slow 4G and CPU throttling, and a real mid-range phone on mobile data in a private tab.', 'Set a page weight budget, host close to your visitors, cut heavy scripts and fonts, and make calls, WhatsApp, forms and checkout work even on a weak connection.'],
  'logo-favicon-brand-basics-website': ['Get your logo as an SVG plus PNG versions, in horizontal, stacked, icon-only and reversed forms, and keep the original design files in your business name.', 'One simple square icon of at least 512 pixels covers the favicon and phone home-screen icons; WordPress generates the smaller sizes from it.', 'Set a 1200 by 630 social share image and an organisation logo in your SEO plugin, and use the same name, logo and colours everywhere customers see you.'],
  'website-accessibility-older-users': ['Older visitors need larger body text, strong contrast and big, well-spaced buttons, and the site must respect the bigger text size many of them set on their phones.', 'Use plain, familiar words, a short menu and short forms, and always offer a tap-to-call number and WhatsApp next to the form.', 'Remove auto-playing media, rotating sliders and pop-ups, show the details that build trust, and test the site by watching an older relative complete real tasks.'],
  'regain-website-access-old-developer': ['Start with the domain: whoever controls the registrar account controls DNS, and with it the website, the email and Search Console verification.', 'Accounts in your business name can be recovered from the provider with proof of ownership; accounts in the developer\'s name need their cooperation, so ask politely and in writing first.', 'Once you are back in, change every password, remove old administrators and keep a record of each login in your name, so the next handover takes minutes.'],
  'stop-contact-form-spam': ['Identify the kind of spam first: bots fall to honeypots, time checks and Turnstile, while pitches typed by real people need Akismet or keyword filters.', 'Layer light defences that are verified on the server, and avoid puzzles, country blocks and silent filters that turn real customers away.', 'Store form entries so nothing filtered is lost, strip visitor text from auto-replies, and send a test enquiry after every change.'],
  'business-email-deliverability-spf-dkim-dmarc': ['SPF lists who may send for your domain, DKIM signs each message, and DMARC tells receivers what to do when a message fails, so you need all three.', 'Keep one SPF record per domain, copy exact values from each provider, and start DMARC at p=none, moving to quarantine only when every real sender passes.', 'Send website form emails through an authenticated SMTP login, such as a domain mailbox or a Gmail app password, rather than the server\'s PHP mail function.'],
  'faq-page-seo': ['Pick questions from Search Console, People Also Ask and real sales calls, then answer each one in the first sentence.', 'FAQs work best on the page they relate to: service, sales, product and location pages, tailored to each.', 'Since 2023 Google shows FAQ rich results only for well-known government and health sites, so FAQ schema mainly adds clarity.'],
  'website-for-insurance-financial-advisors': ['Show credentials, registrations, services and how you\'re paid.', 'Financial content is regulated: avoid promised returns and include required disclosures.', 'Educational guides, privacy care and local SEO build trust and leads.'],
  'website-for-real-estate-agents-brokers': ['Serve both buyers/tenants (listings, filters, WhatsApp) and owners (list-your-property).', 'Area guides for localities you genuinely cover show local expertise.', 'Keep listings current and build trust with registration details and reviews.'],
  'common-wordpress-errors-fixes': ['Most WordPress errors come from a handful of causes: plugin or theme code, server limits such as memory and PHP version, the database, configuration files and SSL or DNS.', 'For any error, stop making changes, note what changed last, take a backup, then read the recovery mode email or the error log before guessing.', 'Use the quick-reference table to match the message to its usual cause, then follow the dedicated guide for that error.'],
  'ssl-certificate-errors-fix': ['Most SSL errors come down to five causes: no certificate, an expired one, one that does not cover the domain, an untrusted one, or pages loading files over plain http.', 'Read the exact error code first, check the certificate details and test from another device, so you know whether the problem is the site or the visitor\'s device.', 'Prevent repeats with auto-renewing certificates, expiry monitoring, one preferred domain version with redirects, and an SSL check after every migration or redesign.'],
  'what-is-a-cdn': ['A CDN is a network of servers in many cities that keeps copies of your site\'s files and serves each visitor from the nearest one, while your hosting stays the origin.', 'It helps most when visitors are far from your server or spread across regions, and it does nothing for a slow admin, a heavy theme or uncached pages.', 'Set it up with DNS records copied carefully, cart, checkout and admin pages bypassed, and caches purged after updates, then test forms and checkout.'],
  'google-maps-on-website': ['For most business sites, the free Share and Embed iframe is enough; the Maps JavaScript API needs an API key and a billing account, so use it only for custom maps or many locations.', 'Embedded maps are heavy, so put one only where it helps, usually the contact page, and lazy-load it or show a click-to-load preview instead.', 'Write the address in text, add a Get directions link and LocalBusiness schema, and keep every detail identical to your Google Business Profile.'],
  'website-analytics-metrics-that-matter': ['Start with key events, such as enquiries, calls, WhatsApp clicks and orders, then look at which sources and landing pages produce them.', 'Use Search Console for what people searched and which pages earn clicks, and treat page views, time on site and follower counts as context, not results.', 'A 30-minute monthly routine comparing key events, sources, landing pages and search queries with last month and last year beats a detailed report nobody reads.'],
  'mobile-first-design-explained': ['Mobile-first design means planning the phone layout first and adding to it for bigger screens, because that is where many visitors decide whether to call or leave.', 'Google mainly uses the mobile version of your pages for indexing, so content, links and structured data must be on mobile, not only on desktop.', 'Big tap targets, short forms with the right keyboards, a sticky call and WhatsApp bar and a light page on a mid-range phone over 4G matter more than desktop polish.'],
  'website-for-home-services': ['Make calling instant with tap-to-call, WhatsApp, hours and emergency info.', 'Create a page per service, list areas served and give pricing guidance.', 'Reviews, photos of work and a strong Google Business Profile win local searches.'],
  'website-for-cleaning-services': ['Show packages with checklists, duration and prices by home size.', 'Offer online booking with WhatsApp confirmation.', 'Staff verification, guarantees and before-and-after photos build trust.'],
  'website-for-packers-movers': ['Explain services and what affects price, with no hidden charges.', 'Use a detailed quote form and accept photos or video on WhatsApp.', 'Prove you\'re genuine with registration, insurance, real photos and reviews.'],
  'website-for-hospitals': ['Put emergency contact and directions on every page.', 'Create department and doctor pages with timings and booking.', 'Add insurance, packages and patient guides, and keep medical content accurate.'],
  'website-for-physiotherapy-clinics': ['Create pages for the conditions people search for.', 'Explain your approach, first session and home visits, with therapist profiles.', 'Easy booking, patient education and local SEO bring in patients.'],
  'website-for-eye-clinics-opticians': ['Clinics need treatment pages, surgeon profiles and insurance information.', 'Optical stores need eye test booking, frames catalogue and lens explanations.', 'High-contrast, accessible design matters especially for eye care visitors.'],
  'website-for-veterinary-pet-clinics': ['Show services, species treated, emergency contact and vet profiles.', 'Easy booking, WhatsApp and grooming/boarding pages with prices win clients.', 'Pet owner guides, reviews and local SEO build trust.'],
  'website-for-bakeries-cake-shops': ['Separate ready-to-order cakes, which can be bought straight from the menu, from custom cakes, which start with a detailed form, a quote and an advance payment.', 'Make the delivery date, time slot, pin code and daily capacity part of the order itself, so the website never accepts a cake your kitchen cannot bake or deliver on time.', 'Label eggless options, allergens and FSSAI details clearly, show real photos of your own cakes, and plan festival pre-orders before the rush starts.'],
  'website-for-florists-gift-shops': ['The person paying is rarely the person receiving, so checkout must collect both sets of details, the card message and a delivery slot, and keep prices out of the parcel.', 'Build pages around occasions and recipients, show cut-off times and the earliest delivery slot on every product, and state your substitution policy for seasonal flowers.', 'Plan Valentine\'s Day, Mother\'s Day, Rakhi and Diwali weeks ahead, with pre-orders, slot limits and campaign landing pages, and give corporate buyers their own quote route.'],
  'website-for-wedding-venues-banquet-halls': ['Show each space with capacity, photos, videos and setups.', 'Share package or per-plate guidance and amenities.', 'Enquiry forms, site-visit booking, reviews and local SEO fill dates.'],
  'website-for-coworking-spaces': ['Show plans and prices clearly, from day passes to private cabins.', 'Each location needs amenities, photos and access details.', 'Tour booking, community stories and location pages win members.'],
  'website-for-hostels-pg-accommodation': ['List room types, rent, deposit, food and amenities clearly.', 'Explain safety measures, since families care most about them.', 'Real photos, distance to colleges or offices and visit booking fill beds.'],
  'website-for-driving-schools': ['Show packages, fees, car types and what each session includes.', 'Explain licence assistance, instructors, safety, timings and pickup areas.', 'Easy enrolment, reviews and local SEO bring learners.'],
  'website-for-music-dance-academies': ['List courses by style, age and level, with teacher profiles.', 'Share schedules and fees and offer trial class booking.', 'Photos and videos of classes and performances show your academy\'s energy.'],
  'website-for-home-tutors-online-teachers': ['Parents check three things before booking a tutor: whether you teach their child\'s subject and board, whether they can trust you, and how classes, timings and fees work.', 'Make a demo class the main action, with a short booking form for class, board, subject, mode and preferred time, and a clear note on whether the demo is free.', 'State qualifications and results precisely and only with consent, explain how home and online sessions keep children safe, and target searches by subject, class and area.'],
  'website-for-handicraft-artisan-brands': ['Tell the story of the craft and the people who make it with their consent and in specific, checkable terms, and use GI names, handloom marks or fair trade claims only where they are genuinely held.', 'Explain what varies between handmade pieces and what does not, photograph texture and scale clearly, and give exact materials, dimensions, care and lead times on every product.', 'Treat retail, overseas and trade buyers as separate routes: a store for single pieces, clear shipping and breakage terms for fragile items, and a wholesale page with MOQs and a quote form.'],
  'website-for-agriculture-businesses': ['Serve farmers, dealers and bulk buyers with clear paths for each.', 'Catalogues need crop suitability, usage guidance and a dealer locator.', 'Regional languages, helpful crop content and light pages widen reach.'],
  'website-for-chemical-pharma-manufacturers': ['List products with CAS numbers, grades, specifications and available documents.', 'Show genuine certifications, quality systems and manufacturing capability.', 'Offer quote, document and sample requests, and avoid regulated claims.'],
  'website-for-textile-manufacturers': ['Catalogues need GSM, count, composition, finishes, MOQs and lead times.', 'Show capacity, quality control and genuine compliance certifications.', 'Offer quote, sample and tech pack upload with fast replies.'],
  'website-for-hardware-building-materials': ['Show product categories, authorised brands and showroom details.', 'Give contractors bulk quote forms, delivery areas and trade terms.', 'Buying guides and local SEO win homeowner and contractor searches.'],
  'google-search-console-reports-explained': ['Performance, Pages, URL Inspection and Sitemaps do most of the work for a small business; Manual actions and Security issues should simply stay empty.', 'Each report answers a different question: how you appear in search, whether pages are indexed, how Google sees one URL, and whether speed, HTTPS or structured data need fixing.', 'A short monthly routine across the reports catches indexing problems, lost pages and new opportunities long before they show up as lost enquiries.'],
  'ai-tools-website-content-responsibly': ['Google\'s guidance, at the time of writing, judges content by how helpful it is rather than how it was made, but mass-producing pages mainly to rank breaks its spam policies whatever the method.', 'Use AI for outlines, first drafts from your own notes and tidy rewrites, then add what only you know: real examples, photos, local detail and the questions customers actually ask.', 'Check every fact, price, rule and number before publishing, never let AI write reviews, testimonials or case studies, and keep customer data out of AI tools.'],
  'website-maintenance-vs-management': ['Website maintenance keeps a site secure, backed up, updated and working; website management uses the site to grow the business through content, SEO, campaigns and conversion improvements.', 'Every business site needs maintenance; management makes sense when the website is a main source of leads or sales and someone has time and budget to improve it.', 'Providers use the words loosely, so judge any plan by its list of tasks, the hours or requests included and the monthly report, not by its name.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-when-needed': ['WordPress Multisite runs many sites from one installation and suits one team managing many similar sites, such as franchise outlets, branches or departments.', 'For a few unrelated sites, a multilingual site or several locations of one business, a single site or separate installations is usually simpler and safer.', 'Google treats every site in a network as a separate site, so each one needs its own SEO settings, sitemap, Search Console checks and genuinely unique content.'],
  'website-security-headers-explained': ['Five headers are quick, low-risk wins: X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options set to SAMEORIGIN, Referrer-Policy, a simple Permissions-Policy and HSTS once HTTPS works everywhere.', 'Content-Security-Policy is the most powerful and the easiest to get wrong on WordPress, so run it in report-only mode and fix every violation before enforcing it.', 'Add headers in one place, test them with securityheaders.com and your browser, and remember they harden a clean site but never replace updates, 2FA and backups.'],
  'conversion-rate-optimization-basics': ['Conversion rate optimization means getting more enquiries, calls, WhatsApp messages or orders from the visitors you already have, starting with tracking those actions properly in GA4.', 'Find the leaks before changing anything: GA4 shows which pages and devices underperform, and Microsoft Clarity\'s heatmaps and recordings show what visitors actually do there.', 'On a low-traffic site, make clearly better changes and compare before and after rather than A/B testing small tweaks, and judge results by lead quality as well as numbers.'],
  'redesign-website-tight-budget': ['Spend a small redesign budget on the pages that bring enquiries, usually the homepage, top service pages and contact page, and phase everything else.', 'Keep the platform and URLs where you can, adapt a quality lightweight theme, cut the number of unique templates and prepare content yourself to reduce cost without cutting quality.', 'Never save money on redirects, backups, mobile testing, form testing, licensed software or owning your domain and hosting; those cuts cost far more later.'],
  'seasonal-festival-campaigns-website': ['Give each festival one permanent page, such as a Diwali gifts page, and update it every year instead of creating a new URL that has to start from zero each season.', 'Prepare weeks ahead: offers and coupons scheduled, order-by dates on every page, gift guides by budget and recipient, and the site tested for speed and checkout before traffic rises.', 'Tag every campaign link and track enquiries, WhatsApp clicks and orders, then review what worked while it is fresh so next year starts from evidence, not memory.'],
  'website-content-calendar': ['Pick three to five themes tied to the services you sell, then place topics in the months when customers actually search for them, including festivals and seasons.', 'Choose a publishing pace you can keep for a whole year, and keep a slot each month for updating an older post as well as writing new ones.', 'Run the calendar from one spreadsheet with the topic, target service page, owner, dates and status, and review it each month against Search Console.'],
  'video-on-business-website': ['Video helps most where seeing beats reading: short explainers, customer testimonials, product demos and tours of your premises, placed beside the text they support.', 'Host videos on YouTube or Vimeo rather than your web hosting, and embed them with a click-to-play preview image so the player loads only when someone taps it.', 'Add captions and a short written summary, keep autoplaying background videos off key pages, and use a phone, good light and a clip-on mic to film on a budget.'],
  'website-for-taxi-car-rental': ['Show fleet, capacity, fare structure and what\'s included.', 'Cover airport, outstation and package services, with route pages only for real routes.', 'Instant call, WhatsApp and short booking forms plus reviews win bookings.'],
  'website-for-immigration-visa-consultants': ['Organise services by country and visa type, linking to official sources.', 'Never guarantee outcomes; keep content dated, accurate and reviewed.', 'Transparent fees, credentials, scam warnings and privacy build trust.'],
  'website-for-medical-equipment-suppliers': ['Catalogues need specifications, models, brochures and authorised brands.', 'Show genuine certifications and strong after-sales service support.', 'Offer quote, demo and dealer enquiries with fast responses.'],
  'website-for-authors-content-creators': ['Social platforms and bookstores control your reach and your customer data; your own website and email list are the parts of your audience you keep.', 'Give readers, brands, event organisers and journalists separate routes: book or content pages, a newsletter sign-up, a media kit with a collaboration form, and a speaker page.', 'Sell signed copies, merchandise and digital products directly where it makes sense, and keep links to bookstores and platforms for everything else.'],
  'migrate-blogger-to-wordpress': ['Export your Blogger content as a backup file, import it into WordPress on staging, then pull every image into the media library so nothing still loads from Blogger.', 'Matching the Blogger post URL pattern in WordPress permalinks avoids most redirects; pages, labels and anything else that changes need 301 redirects to the closest new page.', 'A custom domain moves cleanly with a DNS change, while a blogspot.com address cannot send server redirects, so keep the old blog live and plan for a slower handover.'],
  'migrate-shopify-to-woocommerce': ['Export everything from Shopify first, rebuild the store on WooCommerce staging, and check products, variants, customers and orders in samples before going further.', 'Map every Shopify URL, especially /products/ and /collections/, to its WooCommerce equivalent with 301 redirects, or match the old URL bases in WooCommerce permalinks.', 'Set up and test an Indian payment gateway, shipping, COD and GST invoices on staging, then switch in a quiet period and keep Shopify until everything is verified.'],
  'xml-sitemaps-explained': ['An XML sitemap lists the URLs you want search engines to crawl; it helps Google find pages, but it does not make Google index or rank them.', 'List only canonical, indexable pages that return a 200 status, keep lastmod honest, and let one plugin generate the sitemap rather than two.', 'Submit the sitemap index once in Search Console, then use the Pages report filtered to submitted pages to see which listed URLs Google has left out and why.'],
  'robots-txt-explained': ['Robots.txt controls which URLs crawlers may visit, not which pages appear in Google; to keep a page out of search results, use noindex and leave it crawlable.', 'Most WordPress sites need only a short file that blocks the admin area, allows admin-ajax.php and lists the sitemap, without blocking CSS, JavaScript or uploads.', 'Check the live file after every launch or migration, test important URLs with URL Inspection, and decide on AI crawlers deliberately rather than by accident.'],
  'canonical-tags-explained': ['A canonical tag tells Google which of several near-identical URLs you prefer, but it is a strong hint rather than an order, so redirects, internal links and your sitemap must agree with it.', 'Most pages should have a self-referencing canonical, which WordPress and SEO plugins add automatically; paginated pages, language versions and genuinely different filter pages should point to themselves.', 'Check "Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user" in Search Console and fix common mistakes such as canonicals pointing to an old domain, the homepage, a redirected URL or page 1.'],
  'duplicate-content-explained': ['Duplicate content is the same or very similar content at more than one URL; Google says it is generally not penalised unless the intent is to deceive or manipulate results.', 'The real costs are split ranking signals, Google showing the wrong URL and wasted crawling, mostly caused by archives, URL parameters, site versions and staging copies.', 'Fix it with 301 redirects, canonical tags, noindex for low-value pages and by merging near-identical pages, and only link to and list the preferred URLs.'],
  '301-vs-302-redirects': ['A 301 says a page has moved for good, so Google moves its ranking signals to the new URL; a 302 says the move is temporary, so Google keeps showing the old URL.', 'Use a 301 for almost every real change, point each old URL straight at its closest equivalent in one hop, and keep the redirect for at least a year.', 'Manage page redirects in one place, such as the Redirection plugin or Rank Math, keep site-wide rules at server level, and test in a private window because browsers cache 301s.'],
  'keep-visitors-engaged-website': ['Visitors stay when the first screen confirms they are in the right place, the page loads quickly on a phone, and the content is easy to scan.', 'Every page needs an obvious next step that fits what the visitor wants next, such as a related guide, a case study, a calculator or WhatsApp, linked from inside the content.', 'Measure engaged sessions and key events by landing page in GA4 rather than chasing bounce rate or time on site, because the goal is enquiries, not longer visits.'],
  'how-long-does-seo-take': ['Nobody can honestly give you a date: site age, competition, authority, content and technical health all change how quickly Google responds.', 'Progress usually arrives in order: pages get indexed, impressions grow, positions improve for specific searches, then clicks and enquiries follow, with broad terms last.', 'Judge SEO by indexed pages, impressions, long-tail positions and enquiries over months, not by checking one keyword every morning.'],
  'seo-vs-google-ads': ['Google Ads buys visibility from the day a campaign starts and stops when spending stops; SEO earns free clicks but takes months to build.', 'Start with Ads when you need enquiries soon or want to test demand; prioritise SEO when you can invest for the medium term and buyers research first.', 'Most small businesses do best with both: a technically sound site with strong service pages, focused ads on your most profitable services, and enquiries tracked by channel.'],
  'seo-red-flags-scams': ['Guaranteed rankings, bulk backlink packages, secret methods and providers who keep your accounts in their name are the clearest signs of a bad SEO service.', 'A fair monthly retainer names the work it covers, starts with an audit, reports Search Console and enquiry data, and can be stopped on reasonable notice.', 'You should always own your website, domain, Search Console, Analytics and Google Business Profile, so you can change provider without losing anything.'],
  'ethical-link-building-small-business': ['The links worth having come from websites with a real reason to mention you: suppliers, partners, associations, clients, local news and people who found your resources useful.', 'Start with relationships you already have, reclaim unlinked mentions and broken links, use directories in moderation and write guest articles only for publications your customers read.', 'Paid links, private blog networks, link exchanges and link packages go against the spam policies Google publishes; a handful of relevant earned links beats hundreds of bought ones.'],
  'business-directories-citations-india': ['A small set of listings matters: maps platforms, a few general local directories, the platforms for your industry, and the associations you belong to.', 'Write one master version of your name, address and phone before listing anywhere, claim existing listings before creating new ones, and clean up duplicates and old details.', 'Skip packages that submit you to hundreds of directories and invoices for search engine listing; judge paid listings only on the enquiries they bring, tracked in one sheet.'],
  'handle-negative-reviews': ['Reply to every negative review calmly and briefly: thank, acknowledge, apologise where it is fair, and move the conversation to a phone call or email.', 'Only reviews that break Google\'s policies, such as fake, off-topic, abusive or conflict-of-interest reviews, can be flagged; an honest bad experience stays, so your reply is what counts.', 'Balance the occasional bad review with a steady flow of genuine ones from customers you ask, never paid for, filtered or written for you.'],
  'website-for-property-management-companies': ['Explain owner services, fee structures and areas covered clearly.', 'Speak directly to NRI owners with remote reporting and communication.', 'Give tenants listings and maintenance requests, and build trust with sample reports.'],
  'write-product-descriptions-that-sell': ['Open with who the product is for and the main benefit, then give scannable details, a specifications table, care and delivery notes, and answers to common doubts.', 'Write your own copy instead of the manufacturer\'s text, use the words customers search with once or twice naturally, and keep facts accurate.', 'Put specifications into WooCommerce attributes so they show as a table and power filters, and start with your best sellers when the catalogue is large.'],
  'business-email-options': ['Google Workspace suits teams who live in Gmail and Google Docs, Microsoft 365 suits Outlook and desktop Office users, and Zoho Mail is the budget choice with a small free plan.', 'Email that comes with web hosting is cheap but tied to your hosting account, so a hosting problem or migration can take your email down with it.', 'Whichever you pick, set up MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC records properly, use aliases or groups for info@ and sales@, and keep the account in the business name.'],
  'website-speed-test-tools-explained': ['PageSpeed Insights shows two things: real-user field data from the Chrome UX Report at the top and a simulated Lighthouse lab test below; decisions should rest on the field data.', 'Scores vary between runs and tools because of server response, caching, test location, device simulation and third-party scripts, so run tests several times and compare like with like.', 'Use Search Console for site-wide Core Web Vitals, GTmetrix or WebPageTest waterfalls to find the slow file, and aim to pass Core Web Vitals rather than to score 100.'],
  'does-a-local-shop-need-a-website': ['For most local shops, a complete Google Business Profile, WhatsApp Business and genuine reviews come first, because that is where nearby customers look.', 'A website earns its place when you want to be found for specific products or brands, take delivery or bulk orders, sell to businesses, or look more established than competitors.', 'A simple site of one to a few pages, with WhatsApp, click-to-call, a map and details that match your Google profile, is usually enough to start.'],
  'domain-seo-scam-emails': ['Only the registrar you actually pay can renew your domain; an invoice from any other company is a sales trick or a scam, however official it looks.', 'Look for the giveaways: a company you have never used, urgency, a payment link, small print saying "this is not a bill" and senders on free email addresses.', 'Never click login or payment links in these emails; go to your registrar, host or Google account directly, and if you paid, call your bank and report it on 1930.'],
  'website-for-dermatology-skin-clinics': ['Create treatment pages covering suitability, sessions, downtime and FAQs.', 'Use before-and-after photos only with consent, and note that results vary.', 'Avoid guaranteed or exaggerated claims; share pricing guidance and doctor credentials.'],
  'website-for-ayurveda-wellness-centres': ['Explain therapies and programmes simply, with practitioner credentials.', 'Residential centres need room, diet, schedule and travel details.', 'Avoid cure claims and keep design calm, fast and multilingual where needed.'],
  'website-for-fertility-clinics': ['Explain treatments clearly and compassionately, encouraging consultation.', 'Avoid guarantees or misleading statistics and follow applicable regulations.', 'Protect privacy with minimal, secure forms and discreet contact options.'],
  'website-for-pest-control-companies': ['Create pages for each pest with signs, treatment and aftercare.', 'Explain safety for children and pets and offer residential and commercial plans.', 'Quick booking, WhatsApp photos, pricing guidance and local SEO win urgent enquiries.'],
  'website-for-ev-dealers': ['First-time EV buyers need explanations more than brochures: give every model a page that explains range, charging time and battery warranty in plain words, with the test basis stated.', 'Never publish on-road prices or subsidy amounts you cannot keep current; use an on-road price form, link subsidy information to official sources and date every figure you show.', 'Turn the site into a sales and service tool: test ride booking, a charging guide, a running cost calculator with visible assumptions, and service and battery health bookings.'],
  'website-for-mobile-laptop-repair': ['List devices, brands and common repairs with starting prices and turnaround.', 'Build trust with repair warranties, honest parts info and data privacy.', 'WhatsApp photo quotes, pickup options and local SEO bring customers.'],
  'website-for-laundry-dry-cleaning': ['Show services with a clear price list by item or weight.', 'Make pickup booking easy with areas, slots and turnaround times.', 'Subscriptions, business contracts, care policies and reviews build steady revenue.'],
  'website-for-catering-services': ['Show menus, packages and per-plate starting prices by event type.', 'Build trust with licences, hygiene, event photos and tasting sessions.', 'Detailed enquiry forms and corporate catering pages bring larger orders.'],
  'website-for-tiffin-meal-subscriptions': ['Start with a trial meal and prepaid weekly or monthly plans sold as normal products, and add automatic renewals only once your volume justifies the extra setup.', 'Publish this week\'s menu as a web page, list delivery areas and times you can actually keep, and make pausing and skipping simple with a clear cut-off.', 'Show your FSSAI details, real kitchen photos and honest food claims, and use WhatsApp for menus and delivery updates only with customers who have opted in.'],
  'website-for-trekking-adventure-operators': ['Trip pages need itineraries, difficulty, season, inclusions and real photos.', 'Offer departure calendars with online deposits.', 'Safety information and preparation guides build trust and attract searches.'],
  'website-for-overseas-education-consultants': ['Create accurate, dated destination and course guides linked to official sources.', 'Explain services, fees and counsellor experience; never guarantee admissions or visas.', 'Offer free counselling and profile evaluations to generate leads.'],
  'website-for-recruitment-agencies': ['Give employers and candidates separate journeys from the homepage, each with its own pages, forms and calls to action, so hiring enquiries never get lost among CVs.', 'Publish every live job as its own page with JobPosting structured data and a closing date, and take filled roles down promptly so candidates and Google only see real openings.', 'Treat CVs as sensitive personal data: collect them through a secure form with a clear consent line, limit who can open them, delete them on a schedule and warn candidates about fake offers.'],
  'website-for-event-rental-businesses': ['Show equipment with photos, capacities and starting rates.', 'Offer event packages and quick availability enquiries with clear terms.', 'Real event galleries, safety practices and local SEO win bookings.'],
  'website-for-sports-academies': ['List programmes by sport, age and level with coach profiles.', 'Show facilities, safety, timings, fees and trial booking.', 'Share achievements with consent and keep parents updated.'],
  'website-for-preschools-daycare': ['Lead with what parents check first: safety, who looks after the children, facilities, daily routine and timings, described as you actually run them rather than with vague claims.', 'Make the main action a school visit: a short enquiry form with the child\'s age, programme and a preferred visit slot, followed up quickly on WhatsApp.', 'Publish children\'s photos only with written parental consent, avoid pairing names with faces, and keep daily photos and CCTV in private parent channels, never on public pages.'],
  'website-for-tailoring-services': ['List services with starting prices and extras.', 'Explain measurement, turnaround, trials and pickup options.', 'Galleries, WhatsApp design sharing and seasonal cut-offs drive orders.'],
  'news-magazine-websites-wordpress': ['Design the category structure before the homepage, give every section its own archive, and show bylines, dates and categories on every article.', 'Speed at scale comes from good hosting, page and object caching, sized images and ad slots that reserve their space, measured as the archive grows.', 'Google decides automatically which sites appear in Google News, so focus on original reporting, clear authorship and dates, and correct article schema.'],
  'one-page-vs-multi-page-website': ['A one-page website suits one offer, one audience and one action, such as an event, a single service, a launch or a campaign, or a quick start on a small budget.', 'Choose a multi-page website when you offer several services, serve several areas or audiences, or want to be found on Google for more than your name, because each page can target its own search.', 'You can start with one page and grow: plan sections that can become their own pages later, and keep the homepage URL and section links working when you do.'],
  'first-90-days-after-website-launch': ['Spend the first week proving everything works and is measured, then the first month getting key pages indexed and fixing what Search Console reports.', 'Months two and three are for trust and content: your Google Business Profile, genuine reviews, a few useful posts and improvements based on real search data.', 'Keep backups, updates and monitoring running from day one, and expect impressions to grow before clicks and enquiries do.'],
  'write-meta-titles-descriptions': ['Aim for meta titles of roughly 50 to 60 characters that start with the main search term, and descriptions of roughly 120 to 155 characters that give a specific reason to click.', 'For online stores, hand-write the homepage, top categories, best sellers and sale pages, and use Yoast or Rank Math templates for everything else.', 'Keep one permanent sale URL, avoid hard deadlines like "ends tonight" in the meta tags, and switch to an evergreen version between sales.'],
  'website-navigation-structure': ['Plan the page hierarchy around what visitors come to do, then build a short main menu of five to seven plain labels with one clear call to action.', 'Keep important pages within two or three clicks of the homepage, use the footer and breadcrumbs for secondary routes, and make the mobile menu work with a thumb.', 'Menus are only part of navigation: clean URLs that follow the structure and contextual links between related pages help visitors and Google find everything.'],
  'breadcrumbs-explained': ['Breadcrumbs are the trail of links near the top of a page that shows where it sits on your site and lets visitors move up a level in one tap.', 'They add internal links to your section pages, make your structure clear to Google and, with matching BreadcrumbList schema, can appear in search results in place of the URL.', 'On WordPress, switch them on in one source only, such as Rank Math, Yoast or WooCommerce, place them above the page heading, and test the markup after any change.'],
  'helpful-404-pages': ['A helpful 404 page says plainly that the page is missing, keeps your normal menu, and offers search, links to popular pages and a way to contact you.', 'It must return a real 404 or 410 status code; showing an error page with a 200 status, or redirecting every missing URL to the homepage, creates soft 404s.', 'Find 404s in Search Console, logs and a crawl, then redirect moved pages with a 301, fix broken internal links and leave genuinely dead URLs as 404s.'],
  'wordpress-comments-enable-or-disable': ['Most business websites are better with comments off; keep them only if your articles attract genuine questions and someone will moderate and reply within a day or two.', 'The Discussion settings screen only controls new posts, so close comments on existing posts and pages with bulk edit, and turn off pingbacks and trackbacks either way.', 'If you keep comments, hold first comments for approval, add one spam filter and close comments on older posts; if you remove them site-wide, make sure WooCommerce product reviews still work.'],
  'add-blog-to-existing-website': ['Put the blog in a subfolder such as yourdomain.com/blog/ on the same platform as your site where you can; it keeps everything one site to manage, measure and link.', 'Match the design, set up a few categories that mirror your services, and handle sitemaps, Search Console and SEO settings before the first post goes live.', 'Start with a handful of posts that answer the questions customers ask before they buy, and link every post to the service page it relates to.'],
  'repurpose-website-content-social-media': ['Break each article into parts first, such as the core idea, a checklist, tips, a myth and a FAQ answer, then turn each part into the format that suits a platform.', 'Make every piece useful on its own, and link back to the full article with UTM-tagged links so GA4 shows which platforms bring visitors and enquiries.', 'Follow a simple weekly routine for one article, reshare evergreen pieces every few months, and drop formats that never bring anyone back to the website.'],
  'choose-website-colours-fonts': ['Build a small palette by role, with a primary, an accent for buttons, and neutrals for text and backgrounds, starting from your logo rather than colour psychology.', 'Check every text and button colour against WCAG contrast ratios; a brand colour that fails with white text needs a darker shade or dark text instead.', 'Use one or two font families with only the weights you need, body text of at least 16 pixels, and set colours and fonts once as global styles.'],
  'website-launch-checklist': ['Test the things that cost you enquiries first: every form, call and WhatsApp link on a real phone, with the email arriving where someone will answer it.', 'Most launch disasters are settings, not design: a ticked "discourage search engines" box, leftover noindex tags, staging URLs, missing redirects or no backup.', 'Launch early in the week, test forms again on the live address, submit the sitemap, then watch enquiries, Search Console and 404 errors closely for the first week.'],
  'prepare-photos-for-website': ['Shoot real photos in soft natural light at the shape they will be used, landscape for banners and square for products, and take more than you think you need.', 'Resize each photo to roughly the largest size it is shown at, save it as WebP or a compressed JPEG, and give it a descriptive file name and alt text.', 'Get consent from anyone recognisable, then send full-quality files to your developer in folders by page, with a short sheet of captions and notes.'],
  'hire-developer-vs-diy-website': ['DIY suits simple, low-stakes sites when you have time to learn; hiring pays off when the website brings enquiries or sales, needs real features or has rankings to protect.', 'The biggest DIY cost is your time and the problems you cannot see, such as forms that fail silently, slow pages and missing SEO basics, not the subscription.', 'A common middle path is a developer-built foundation you edit yourself, with help hired only for technical work, and every account kept in your name either way.'],
  'write-homepage-that-converts': ['The first screen must say what you do, who it is for and what to do next, with one primary call to action, and it must pass that test on a phone.', 'A reliable order is hero, quick proof, services, why you, process, work, testimonials, FAQs and a final call to action, each linking to a deeper page.', 'Repeat one primary action through the page, use only proof you can back up, and judge changes by enquiries rather than opinions.'],
  'show-prices-on-website': ['Most service businesses benefit from some price guidance, such as a range, a starting-from price or packages, even when every job is quoted individually.', 'A price needs context next to it: what is included, what is not, whether GST is extra and what moves the cost up or down.', 'If you are unsure, add price guidance to one page or landing page and compare the number and quality of enquiries over a couple of months.'],
  'diy-website-audit': ['With a phone, a laptop and free tools you can check first impressions, mobile use, speed, contact paths, Google visibility, security, content, trust and analytics in under an hour.', 'Score each area from 0 to 2 as you go; anything that stops enquiries, such as a broken form or a wrong phone number, gets fixed first, whatever the total.', 'A few low scores usually mean targeted fixes or a refresh; low scores across mobile, speed, trust and Google visibility usually point to a redesign.'],
  'ga4-events-explained': ['GA4 records every interaction as an event; key events are the few you mark as business outcomes, such as enquiries, calls, WhatsApp clicks and purchases.', 'Enhanced measurement covers page views, scrolls, outbound clicks and downloads, but enquiries need their own events, best fired on a successful submission or thank-you page.', 'Use clear, consistent event names, mark only real outcomes as key events, and test every event in DebugView and Realtime before trusting the reports.'],
  'utm-tags-explained': ['UTM tags are labels added to a link so Google Analytics knows which source, medium and campaign sent a visitor, which matters most for WhatsApp, email, QR codes and social links.', 'Use lowercase, consistent names and medium values GA4 recognises, keep a shared spreadsheet, and never tag links between pages of your own website.', 'In GA4, switch Traffic acquisition to session source, medium or campaign and add key events, so you compare campaigns by enquiries rather than visits.'],
  'thank-you-pages-forms': ['A thank-you page confirms the enquiry arrived, says honestly what happens next, offers a faster route such as WhatsApp, and gives you a reliable conversion to track.', 'Redirect to it only after a successful submission, keep it out of Google with noindex rather than robots.txt, and give each important form its own thank-you URL.', 'Count each enquiry once: use a generate_lead key event set to once per session in GA4, set Google Ads to count one conversion, and test with a real submission.'],
  'wordpress-caching-explained': ['Page caching serves ready-made HTML and is the biggest win for most sites; browser and CDN caching speed up files and repeat visits, object caching (Redis or Memcached) helps dynamic sites, and OPcache is usually handled by your host.', 'Use your host\'s server-level cache where it exists (for example LiteSpeed with the LiteSpeed Cache plugin) or one reputable caching plugin, never two, and always exclude cart, checkout, My Account and logged-in users.', 'Clear caches in order (plugin, server, CDN, browser) after changes, and test that caching works by checking response headers in a private window, then testing forms and the cart.'],
  'woocommerce-gst-invoices-india': ['Enable taxes, calculate tax on the shipping address, create a tax class per GST rate, and add CGST and SGST for your home state with IGST for every other state.', 'WooCommerce has no HSN field, GSTIN checkout field or GST invoice by default, so add them with a maintained invoice plugin or custom fields and test the PDF.', 'Rates, HSN codes, invoice format, shipping tax and e-invoicing depend on your products and turnover, so confirm every setting with your CA before launch.'],
  'woocommerce-cash-on-delivery-india': ['Enable COD under WooCommerce payments, limit it to the shipping methods your courier supports, and add rules for order value, pin code or product with a plugin or a little code.', 'Confirm COD orders by WhatsApp, SMS or a call before dispatch, hold unconfirmed ones, and use a COD fee, a prepaid incentive or a partial advance to filter non-serious buyers.', 'Act quickly on failed delivery reports, reconcile courier COD remittances against your orders every week, and tighten rules where refusals keep happening.'],
  'website-ownership-checklist': ['Your business should own the domain, DNS, hosting, WordPress administrator account, email, Google accounts, premium licences and payment gateway, each under your own email.', 'Owning an account means it is registered to you and you can add or remove other users; simply knowing a password is access, not ownership.', 'Give developers their own user accounts with the role they need, keep a written record of every login and renewal date, and remove access when the work ends.'],
  'website-for-nutritionists-dietitians': ['Show qualifications, programme pages, how consultations work and pricing.', 'Offer online booking, payment and WhatsApp, with a short intake form.', 'Share results responsibly, protect health data and publish practical recipes and guides.'],
  'website-for-psychologists-counsellors': ['Answer credentials, approaches, formats, fees and what happens in the first session.', 'Use calm design, gentle language and a simple contact form without sensitive fields.', 'Add crisis information, follow ethical rules on testimonials and protect privacy.'],
  'update-php-version-wordpress': ['Old PHP versions stop getting security fixes and newer plugins drop support for them, so plan an upgrade rather than waiting for your host to force one.', 'Before switching, take a backup, update WordPress, plugins and theme, replace abandoned plugins and test the new version on staging; PHP Compatibility Checker is only a first pass.', 'Switch in hPanel or cPanel, check that extensions such as mysqli, curl, mbstring, intl, zip and gd are enabled for the new version, test key pages and forms, and switch back if anything breaks.'],
  'wordpress-database-optimization': ['Always take a full backup first, then remove revisions, auto-drafts, trash, spam and expired transients, which are safe to delete on almost every site.', 'Large autoloaded options and leftover plugin data cause more slowness than revisions, but delete them only once you know which plugin created them.', 'Limit revisions and trash days in wp-config.php, trim plugin logs and never delete WooCommerce orders, then repeat the clean-up every few months.'],
  'website-design-contract-checklist': ['A website contract or proposal should name every page and feature, list what is excluded, and say who supplies content and how many revision rounds are included.', 'Payment milestones, change requests, ownership of the site and accounts, the support period and how either side can end the project cause most disputes, so write them down.', 'It does not need to be long, but it should be in writing and accepted by both sides; have a lawyer review anything high-value or unusual.'],
  'wordpress-form-plugins-compared': ['All the common form plugins handle a standard contact or quote form; the differences are stored entries, spam tools, conditional logic, file uploads, multi-step forms and integrations, and which of those are free.', 'No form plugin fixes email delivery on its own, so pair any of them with SMTP, stored entries and spam protection, then send a test enquiry from your phone.', 'Choose by the forms you actually need and who will maintain them, and check the current plans of each plugin, because what is free and what is paid changes.'],
  'website-for-astrologers-vastu-consultants': ['Give each service its own page with formats, fees and languages.', 'Offer online booking and payment, and protect birth details and personal data.', 'Avoid guaranteed or fear-based claims; publish helpful articles for search traffic.'],
  'website-for-photographers': ['Organise the portfolio by specialism and show complete stories, but export images at display size, serve WebP and lazy-load everything except the first image so galleries stay fast on phones.', 'Deliver work through private proofing galleries with favourites, downloads and an expiry date, kept out of search and away from your main hosting if storage is tight.', 'Use an enquiry form that asks for date, city, venue, events and coverage, and support it with image SEO: descriptive file names, alt text, captions and a story for every shoot.'],
  'website-for-coaches-consultants': ['Lead with a clear niche: who you help and the problem you solve.', 'Give each programme a page with format, investment and FAQs, and make booking a call easy.', 'Use honest proof, avoid income or outcome guarantees and grow a list with a free resource.'],
  'website-for-wholesalers-distributors': ['Organise the catalogue by category and brand with SKUs, pack sizes and case quantities, and claim authorised distributor status or use brand logos only where you genuinely can.', 'Decide how prices are shown: price on request for most trade catalogues, or a dealer login with role-based prices, so retailers see their rates and the public does not.', 'Give bulk buyers an enquiry basket or quick RFQ with WhatsApp, keep dealer registration on its own page, and add WooCommerce B2B ordering once repeat customers want to reorder online.'],
  'website-for-colleges-universities': ['Give every programme its own page with eligibility, duration, curriculum, fees or fee structure, careers and an enquiry button, because programme pages match what students actually search for.', 'Build admissions as a funnel, from programme page to enquiry, counsellor follow-up, campus visit, application and fee payment, with dates staff can update in one place.', 'State approvals, affiliations, accreditations and placement figures only exactly as held and verifiable, and keep mandatory disclosure, anti-ragging and grievance pages current.'],
  'website-for-elder-care-home-nursing': ['Families want services, trained and verified staff, emergency procedures and pricing.', 'Use real photos and family testimonials, and get consent before showing residents.', 'Offer phone, WhatsApp and video calls, accessible design and local SEO.'],
  'website-for-sweet-shops-mithai': ['List menu prices, gift boxes, corporate orders and delivery areas.', 'Use WooCommerce with delivery slots, pin code zones and gift messages.', 'Plan festival pre-orders early and show FSSAI, allergen and shelf-life information.'],
  'website-chatbot-worth-it': ['For most small Indian businesses, a WhatsApp button plus clear pages and FAQs does more than a chatbot, at lower cost and with less upkeep.', 'A chatbot earns its place with many repetitive questions, out-of-hours visitors or a busy store, and only if someone keeps its answers accurate and offers a route to a person.', 'Chat widgets can slow every page and collect personal data, so load them only when needed, update your privacy policy and measure enquiries before and after.'],
  'keyword-research-small-business': ['Start with the words customers use on calls and WhatsApp, then expand them with Search Console, Google autocomplete, People also ask and Keyword Planner ranges.', 'Collect local modifiers, city spellings and Hinglish or Marathi variants, map every keyword group to one page by intent, and prioritise searches close to a buying decision that you can realistically win.', 'Keep a simple keyword map so two pages never target the same search, and revisit it every few months with fresh Search Console data.'],
  'search-intent-explained': ['Search intent is the goal behind a search, and Google ranks the type of page that best meets it, so a page of the wrong type rarely ranks however well it is written.', 'Read intent by searching the phrase yourself on a phone and noting which page types and features Google shows, such as guides, service pages, products or a map.', 'Give each intent one page, match its type, format and opening to what searchers want, and link guides to services so readers can take the next step.'],
  'topic-clusters-pillar-pages': ['A topic cluster is one broad pillar page that covers a subject and links to detailed articles on each question, with every article linking back; for a business, the pillar is usually a service page.', 'Plan clusters from the questions customers ask before buying, give each page one distinct job, and decide whether a question deserves its own article or a section of the pillar.', 'In WordPress, contextual links in the body text do most of the work; categories, breadcrumbs and related-post lists help, but they do not replace deliberate linking.'],
  'update-old-blog-posts': ['Start with posts that are losing clicks, sit just off page one or get impressions without clicks in Search Console, and give priority to posts that bring enquiries.', 'Decide whether each post should be updated, merged, redirected or removed, then refresh it in place: answer first, correct the facts, cover current questions and add internal links.', 'Keep the URL, show an honest updated date, request indexing, and compare the same length of time before and after so you know whether the refresh worked.'],
  'google-algorithm-updates-small-business': ['Core updates reassess how helpful and relevant pages are across the web, while spam updates target sites breaking Google\'s spam policies, so each needs a different response.', 'Before blaming an update, match your Search Console dates to the Search Status Dashboard, wait for the rollout to finish and rule out tracking, site changes and seasonality.', 'Recovery comes from genuinely better pages and removing spam tactics, usually over months and often not until a later update, so nobody can promise a date.'],
  'write-blog-posts-that-rank': ['Start from a question customers actually search, check what Google already ranks for it, and outline a post that answers it more clearly and more specifically than those pages.', 'Add what only your business knows, such as real examples, local detail and photos of your work, then handle headings, internal links, images and the title before publishing.', 'Publishing is the midpoint: share the post, link to it from related pages, and update it once Search Console shows which searches it appears for.'],
  'woocommerce-returns-refunds-policy': ['A good returns policy states what can be returned or exchanged, the window from delivery, the condition required, how to ask, who pays return shipping, and how and when refunds are paid.', 'Issue refunds from the WooCommerce order screen so stock, totals and emails stay correct; refunds go back automatically only if your gateway plugin supports it.', 'For COD orders, collect bank or UPI details through a secure form, record the transaction reference, and treat RTO parcels separately from customer returns.'],
  'woocommerce-product-variations': ['Attributes (size, colour) and their terms combine into variations, and each variation can have its own price, SKU, stock and image; use global attributes for anything shoppers filter by.', 'Generate only the combinations you sell, use bulk actions or CSV import for large catalogues, and add swatches and size charts so shoppers pick the right option first time.', 'Above WooCommerce\'s AJAX variation threshold (30 by default at the time of writing) unavailable combinations are no longer greyed out in advance, so keep variation sets lean and keep one URL per product for SEO.'],
  'woocommerce-inventory-management': ['WooCommerce has solid built-in inventory management: turn it on under Settings, Products, Inventory, then set quantity, SKU and thresholds on each product or variation.', 'Most stock problems come from setup: manage stock left unticked on variations, orders stuck in pending payment, or backorders allowed by accident.', 'If you also sell in a shop, on marketplaces or keep stock in Tally, pick one system as the master, use identical SKUs everywhere and keep a small buffer for sync delays.'],
  'woocommerce-coupons-discounts': ['WooCommerce has three built-in coupon discount types: percentage discount, fixed cart discount and fixed product discount, and any of them can also unlock free shipping.', 'Usage restrictions and limits (minimum spend, products, sale items, individual use, allowed emails and per-customer limits) protect your margins and stop codes leaking.', 'Automatic discounts, coupon links and buy-one-get-one offers need an extension; for festival sales, set expiry dates in the right timezone, test at checkout and measure profit, not just orders.'],
  'google-merchant-center-woocommerce': ['Connect WooCommerce to Google Merchant Center with the free Google for WooCommerce extension or a feed plugin, then set shipping and returns to match your site.', 'Most disapprovals come from prices, GST or stock that do not match the product page, missing shipping details, poor images or missing contact and policy pages.', 'Free listings cost nothing, and the same clean product data later powers Shopping and Performance Max ads once conversion tracking is in place.'],
  'own-website-vs-marketplaces': ['Large marketplaces bring ready buyers but take fees on every sale, set the rules and keep most of the customer relationship.', 'Your own store gives you margin, customer data, brand control and search visibility, but you have to bring the traffic and run payments, shipping and support.', 'Many brands use both: marketplaces for discovery and volume, their own site for the full range, bundles and repeat customers, with stock kept in sync.'],
  'website-down-what-to-do': ['First confirm the site is down for everyone, not just your network, by testing on mobile data and with an online down checker.', 'The error message tells you which layer failed: DNS errors point to the domain, timeouts and suspension pages to hosting, certificate warnings to SSL, and PHP or database errors to the site itself.', 'Check in this order: domain and DNS, hosting status and resource limits, SSL, then recent changes to the site; restore a backup only once you know the domain and hosting are fine.'],
  'domain-expired-what-to-do': ['An expired domain is rarely lost straight away: most registrars give a grace period to renew at the normal price, then a redemption period where the owner can restore it for an extra fee.', 'When a domain expires, the website and the email on that domain stop working, and SSL, payment callbacks and password-reset emails can fail with them.', 'Turn on auto-renew, keep the registrar account in your business name with a recovery email on a different domain, and check that renewals actually went through.'],
  'transfer-domain-to-another-registrar': ['A transfer changes the company you pay to register the domain; your website and email stay online as long as the DNS records keep working throughout.', 'Copy every DNS record and prepare it first, then unlock the domain, get the EPP code and start the transfer at the new registrar.', 'Check eligibility before you start: registrars can refuse a transfer soon after registration, a previous transfer or an owner change, and close to expiry.'],
  'wordpress-admin-slow': ['The dashboard is never page-cached, so every admin screen runs plugins and database queries live, which is why it can crawl while the public site feels fast.', 'Measure before changing anything: Query Monitor shows slow queries, slow requests to outside servers and which plugin causes them.', 'The usual fixes are removing or replacing heavy plugins, slowing Heartbeat, cleaning autoloaded data, adding object caching and moving to hosting with enough resources.'],
  'deceptive-site-ahead-warning-fix': ['The red "Deceptive site ahead" screen comes from Google Safe Browsing and almost always means someone has hidden phishing pages on your site, often in a folder or subdomain you never look at.', 'Open the Security issues report in Search Console, find and delete every phishing page, close the way the attacker got in, and only then request a review that explains what you fixed.', 'Google has said phishing reviews usually take about a day, but a site flagged again after a review can face a longer wait, so clean thoroughly the first time and keep it maintained.'],
  'secure-wordpress-login': ['Two-factor authentication for every administrator, editor and shop manager, plus unique passwords from a password manager, stops most login attacks even when a password leaks.', 'Limit login attempts and add Cloudflare Turnstile to every login form, including WooCommerce\'s account page, and make sure your security plugin sees real visitor IPs if you use a proxy such as Cloudflare.', 'Changing the login URL cuts bot noise but isn\'t real protection, and passkeys are the strongest option where your plugins support them.'],
  'compare-website-quotes': ['Website quotes differ mainly because they price different work, so send everyone the same written brief and compare scope line by line, not the headline number.', 'A good quotation lists pages and templates, features, revision rounds, content responsibilities, exclusions, timeline, payment terms, GST and whose name the accounts are in.', 'Compare the three-year cost, including hosting, licences, maintenance and likely changes, and weigh live work, the questions asked and ownership before price.'],
  'fixed-price-vs-hourly-website-projects': ['Fixed price suits most website projects with a clear scope, such as a new site, a redesign or a landing page, because you know the total before work starts.', 'Hourly billing suits work nobody can scope in advance, such as diagnosing a slow or broken site, but always agree an estimate and a cap.', 'A common, fair setup is a fixed quote for the build and a monthly plan or fixed-price batches for changes after launch, all written into the agreement.'],
  'give-website-feedback-developer': ['Send one consolidated list from one decision-maker, where each point names the page, the device and browser, what you see and what you expected, with a screenshot.', 'Explain the problem rather than only the fix, mark each point as must-fix, should-fix or later, and keep bugs separate from new requests.', 'Review on your phone as well as a computer, and send everything for a round together by the agreed date so each revision round counts.'],
  'website-bug-vs-change-request': ['A bug is something that does not work as agreed or as it reasonably should; a change request is anything new or different from the agreed scope.', 'Bugs in the original work are normally fixed without charge during the support period set out in your agreement; changes are quoted or covered by a maintenance plan\'s allowance.', 'Report one issue at a time with the page URL, steps to reproduce, what you expected, your device and browser, and a screenshot or screen recording.'],
  'outsource-wordpress-development-india': ['India offers skilled WordPress developers, competitive rates and useful time zone overlap.', 'Choose partners by live work, process and communication, starting with a small test.', 'Agree scope, milestones, payments, NDA and ownership in writing, with clear quality standards.'],
  'web-designer-vs-web-developer': ['A web designer decides how a site looks and how visitors move through it; a web developer makes it work; a WordPress developer is a developer who specialises in WordPress.', 'For a small business site, one person who designs and builds well is often enough; for fixes, speed, security, WooCommerce or custom features you need a developer.', 'Judge either by their live work: designers by how polished and on-brand it looks, developers by how fast, mobile-friendly and reliable the sites are.'],
  'what-does-wordpress-developer-do': ['A WordPress developer builds, customises and fixes WordPress sites; some specialise in themes or plugins, others in complete business websites and stores.', 'A web designer decides how a site looks and works for visitors; a WordPress developer turns that into a working, fast, secure site, and many people do both.', 'Hire one when your site drives enquiries or sales, needs features you can\'t add yourself, or is slow, broken or hacked, and judge them on similar live work and a clear scope.'],
  'elementor-pro-worth-it': ['Elementor Pro is worth it when your site needs custom headers, footers and post templates, WooCommerce layouts, dynamic listings, or forms and popups in one tool.', 'For a simple brochure site, free Elementor on a theme with a header and footer builder, plus a form plugin, is often enough.', 'Pro is a yearly licence: buy it in your business name, budget for renewals, and remember that removing it later means rebuilding anything made with Pro features.'],
  'best-theme-for-elementor': ['The best theme for Elementor is a lightweight one that matches who draws the header, footer, blog and shop templates: the theme, or Elementor Pro\'s Theme Builder.', 'With Elementor Pro designing everything, Hello Elementor keeps things minimal; with free Elementor, Astra, GeneratePress, Kadence or Blocksy handle the templates Elementor can\'t.', 'Avoid multipurpose themes that bundle other builders and many required plugins, use a child theme only for custom code, and switch themes on a staging copy first.'],
  'figma-to-wordpress-approaches': ['Elementor suits marketing sites that clients want to edit visually, a block theme with custom blocks suits lighter and more structured sites, and a custom theme suits complex or locked-down builds.', 'Bricks and similar builders give developers cleaner output but have smaller ecosystems, and automatic Figma export tools usually produce heavy, hard-to-edit layouts.', 'Choose by who edits the site after launch, how strict the speed targets are, budget and timeline, and who will maintain it later, then insist on the same design review, staging and pixel QA whichever you pick.'],
  'website-for-saas-startups': ['Lead with a specific outcome, product visuals and one primary call to action.', 'Include features, use cases, pricing, customers, integrations, security and docs.', 'Use WordPress for the marketing site, keep the app separate and track the funnel.'],
  'restaurant-menu-on-website': ['Build the menu as a real web page with dish names, prices and markers as text, and keep a PDF only as a printable extra.', 'Give every dish the same details in the same order, with dietary and allergen labels supplied by your kitchen, and photos only for the dishes that sell the restaurant.', 'Point QR codes, your Google Business Profile and every outlet to stable menu addresses, and give one person the job of keeping prices current everywhere.'],
  'website-for-cloud-kitchens': ['Keep delivery apps for discovery, and use your own website and WhatsApp to turn repeat customers into direct orders you keep more of, with a clear reason to order direct.', 'Choose the ordering flow that matches your team: WhatsApp orders for small menus, WooCommerce ordering with payment, delivery areas and kitchen hours for higher volumes.', 'Put every brand menu on real web pages with prices, veg markers and allergens, show your FSSAI details, and check Google rules before creating profiles for virtual brands.'],
  'real-estate-project-microsite': ['A project microsite should answer a buyer\'s first questions fast: configurations with carpet area, floor plans, location, price or price on request, RERA details and how to book a visit.', 'Most projects work best as a page on the developer\'s main site plus focused ad landing pages; a separate microsite earns its place for a large launch with its own budget.', 'Capture leads with short forms, a gated brochure, site-visit booking, tap-to-call and WhatsApp, and track every one by campaign so you know which ads bring visits.'],
  'hotel-booking-engine-channel-manager': ['A booking engine takes direct bookings on your website; a channel manager keeps rates and availability in sync across OTAs; a PMS runs the property.', 'Check real two-way sync, the OTAs you actually use, Indian payment options, PMS integration and how sync failures are handled.', 'Most overbookings come from editing OTA extranets directly, wrong room mapping or unrecorded walk-in and phone bookings.'],
  'ngo-website-donations-80g': ['Donors give when the website answers their doubts: who you are legally, what their money will do, whether a tax benefit applies and what happened after they gave.', 'Mention 80G, 12A, FCRA or CSR registration only if the organisation genuinely holds it, collect PAN only from donors who want 80G, and confirm receipts and foreign donation rules with your CA.', 'Transparency pages, simple volunteer forms and impact stories shared with written consent build the trust that brings repeat and monthly giving.'],
  'solar-company-lead-generation': ['Match each lead source to the right page: search ads and Google Maps bring buyers who are ready to compare, while social ads and articles bring people who need more convincing.', 'Qualify with a few questions that change your proposal, such as property type, PIN code, roof type and bill range, and judge campaigns by surveys booked rather than form fills.', 'Speed of follow-up decides many solar sales, so reply instantly, call as soon as you can, use WhatsApp for documents and track every lead through to survey and order.'],
  'website-for-advocates-bar-council-rules': ['Bar Council rules prohibit solicitation but permit limited factual website information.', 'Share name, contact, enrolment, qualifications and practice areas.', 'Avoid success claims, testimonials and promotional words; add a disclaimer.'],
  'website-for-homestays-bnbs': ['Show real photos, rooms, rates, location, amenities, rules and reviews.', 'Take direct bookings with a calendar synced to Airbnb and OTAs via iCal.', 'Offer direct perks, display registration and write local area guides.'],
  'online-appointment-booking-website': ['Pick the booking method by how you work: a request form or WhatsApp for a single practice, an embedded scheduler for consultants, a WordPress booking plugin for staff and services, or your clinic or salon software\'s own widget.', 'Set services, durations, buffers, holidays and booking windows carefully, and keep phone and walk-in bookings in the same calendar to avoid double bookings.', 'Reminders with an easy reschedule link, plus deposits for first visits or long appointments, do more to cut no-shows than anything else.'],
  'remove-malware-wordpress-step-by-step': ['Back up the infected site, reset every password and key, then replace core, plugins and themes with clean copies.', 'Check uploads, mu-plugins, .htaccess, wp-config.php, the database and cron jobs, and close the entry point.', 'Only request a Google review once the site is fully clean, then remove spam URLs and keep monitoring.'],
  'fix-wordpress-redirect-hack': ['Redirect malware usually targets mobile, logged-out visitors from Google, so test on mobile data in a private window.', 'Check .htaccess, wp-config.php, theme header and footer files, JS files, the database, fake plugins and mu-plugins.', 'Clean every location, purge all caches and close the entry point, or the redirect will come back.'],
  'fix-japanese-keyword-seo-spam-hack': ['The Japanese keyword hack creates cloaked spam pages that only Google sees, so confirm it with site: searches and the URL Inspection live test rather than by visiting your site.', 'Remove any Search Console owner or sitemap the hacker added, including their verification token, then delete the spam generator and every backdoor at the same time.', 'Let spam URLs return 410 or 404, never redirect or block them, use the Removals tool for brand searches, and expect a large hack to take weeks or months to clear.'],
  'find-remove-wordpress-backdoors': ['A backdoor is hidden code or access that lets an attacker back in after a cleanup, and a missed one is the usual reason malware returns days later.', 'Check core and plugin files against official checksums, then look where backdoors hide: PHP in uploads, mu-plugins, fake plugins, wp-config includes, .htaccess and .user.ini, cron jobs, rogue admins and the database.', 'Remove every backdoor in one session, replace files rather than editing them, then change all passwords and salts, or the attacker simply uses the one you missed.'],
  'wordpress-site-sending-spam-emails': ['Spam from a hosting account usually comes from a malicious mailer script, a stolen mailbox or SMTP password, or an abused form, and the mail logs and message headers tell you which.', 'Ask your host to pause outgoing mail, keep a sample of the spam, fix the cause and change every mailbox password before you request delisting from Spamhaus or any other blacklist.', 'Send website email through authenticated SMTP with SPF, DKIM and DMARC in place, so abuse is easier to spot and spoofed mail is easier for receivers to reject.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-skimmer-malware': ['A checkout skimmer is injected JavaScript that copies what customers type or shows a fake card form, and it can affect stores that use a hosted payment page too.', 'Inspect the checkout as a logged-out customer with the Network tab open, list every script domain, and search the database and JavaScript files, because skimmers often live in settings rather than PHP files.', 'If you find one, pause card payments, keep evidence, tell your payment gateway and take legal advice on reporting and customer notices, then clean the store and limit checkout scripts with a Content Security Policy.'],
  'why-wordpress-sites-get-hacked': ['WordPress core is rarely the way in; most hacks come through vulnerable or abandoned plugins and themes, stolen or reused passwords, pirated software and neglected sites in the same hosting account.', 'Attacks are automated: bots scan for known weaknesses soon after a vulnerability is published, so small business sites are used for their search reputation, server and visitors, not because anyone picked them.', 'Prompt updates, unique passwords with two-factor login, no nulled software, a tidy hosting account and monitoring close the common routes, and logs can show which one was used after a hack.'],
  'nulled-themes-plugins-risks': ['Nulled WordPress themes and plugins are pirated premium copies that often hide backdoors, spam links or redirects, and they never receive official security updates.', 'Check every premium product against a genuine licence and an official copy, search for obfuscated code and unknown admin users, and see what Google is indexing for your domain.', 'Deleting the nulled plugin is not enough: install clean copies, look for backdoors elsewhere, reset passwords and keys, and keep a licence register in the business\'s name.'],
  'wordpress-malware-scanners-compared': ['Remote scanners such as Sucuri SiteCheck only see public pages, plugin scanners such as Wordfence check files and the database from inside WordPress, and server-side scanners see the whole hosting account.', 'No scanner catches everything: cloaked spam, database injections, new malware and premium plugin files are common blind spots, so trust symptoms and Google\'s reports over a clean result.', 'For most business sites, one plugin or host scanner on a schedule, an occasional remote check and Search Console alerts are enough, as long as someone acts on the results.'],
  'hosting-suspended-malware': ['A malware suspension protects the host\'s other customers; most hosts reactivate the account once every site in it is clean and the way in has been closed.', 'Ask the host for the scan report and temporary file access, back up the infected copy, then clean every site and folder in the account, not just the main website.', 'Send the host a short summary of what you removed and changed, ask for a re-scan, then check Google, email blacklists and the files again over the next few weeks.'],
  'this-site-may-be-hacked-google': ['The label means Google believes a third party changed pages on your site or added spam, so confirm it in Search Console\'s Security issues report and check for rogue owners and spam sitemaps.', 'Clean the site completely, including backdoors and the entry point, make spam URLs return 404 or 410 (never block them in robots.txt), then request a review that explains what you found and fixed.', 'Reviews and recrawling take time, often longer for spam hacks, so focus on prevention afterwards: updates, no nulled software, two-factor login, a firewall, off-site backups and Search Console alerts.'],
  'wordpress-file-permissions': ['For most WordPress sites, folders should be 755, files 644 and wp-config.php tighter at 600 or 440 where your host allows it; 777 is never the right answer.', 'Ownership matters as much as the numbers: if WordPress asks for FTP details or cannot create folders, the files usually belong to the wrong user, which chmod 777 does not fix.', 'You can check and reset permissions in cPanel File Manager, an SFTP client such as FileZilla or over SSH, and you should recheck them after a hack or a migration.'],
  'wordpress-firewall-waf-explained': ['A plugin firewall such as Wordfence runs on your server and understands WordPress, while a DNS-level firewall such as Cloudflare or Sucuri filters traffic before it reaches your hosting.', 'The rules that matter most on WordPress protect the login, XML-RPC, checkout and forms, and rate limiting on those few URLs stops most bot floods without affecting real visitors.', 'Every firewall blocks something legitimate sooner or later, so find the exact rule in the logs and add a narrow exception rather than switching the firewall off.'],
  'harden-wp-config-php': ['Add DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT, keep debugging off on the live site, force HTTPS for the admin area and refresh the salts after a hack or when someone with access leaves.', 'DISALLOW_FILE_MODS also blocks dashboard updates and automatic updates, so use it only when updates are handled by WP-CLI, deployments or a maintenance routine.', 'Changing the table prefix or moving wp-config.php up a folder adds little real protection; deleting leftover backup copies of the file and setting tight permissions matter more.'],
  'recover-rankings-after-hack': ['Rankings usually recover once the site is genuinely clean, warnings and manual actions are lifted, spam URLs drop out and your real pages are back to normal, but nobody can promise how quickly.', 'Check for SEO sabotage as well as malware: noindex tags, robots.txt changes, altered titles, canonicals and redirects can keep rankings down after the infection is gone.', 'Disavowing links is rarely needed after a hack, because spam links usually point at pages that now return 404 or 410, and Google ignores most spammy links anyway.'],
  'hostinger-wordpress-setup-guide': ['Choose web, WordPress or cloud hosting rather than the Website Builder, check the renewal price, and keep the account and domain in your business name.', 'After installing WordPress in hPanel, switch on SSL, set a current PHP version and configure LiteSpeed Cache before you build a single page.', 'Keep your own off-site backup alongside the host\'s, set up email records carefully, and use staging for changes once the site is live.'],
  'hostinger-website-slow-fix': ['A slow WordPress site on Hostinger is usually hitting its plan\'s CPU, memory or process limits, or not being served from the LiteSpeed cache, so check hPanel\'s resource usage and caching before blaming the host.', 'Use the LiteSpeed Cache plugin with the server cache, one CDN and a current PHP version, clean up the database and heavy plugins, and change optimisation settings one at a time, testing after each.', 'If the server responds quickly but pages are still slow, the cause is the page itself (images, page builder bloat, third-party scripts), and upgrading the plan won\'t fix it.'],
  'migrate-website-to-hostinger': ['Copy the site to Hostinger first, using hPanel\'s migration tool, a plugin such as All-in-One WP Migration, or a manual move, and test it before the domain points there.', 'Recreate every email DNS record (MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC) before changing nameservers, or switch only the A record, so email keeps working through the move.', 'After the switch, check SSL, forms, checkout and caching, remove the old host\'s plugins, and keep the old hosting for a week or two as a fallback.'],
  'hosting-renewal-price-increase': ['The advertised hosting price is usually an introductory discount for the first term, often tied to paying for several years upfront; renewal is at the regular price, plus 18% GST.', 'Before renewing, remove add-ons you do not use, check whether a smaller plan fits, ask for a retention offer and compare the total cost over three to five years.', 'Moving hosts can save money, but the next host will renew at its regular price too; move for better speed and support, and never let the old plan lapse mid-move.'],
  'monthly-website-maintenance-plan': ['A good monthly plan covers updates applied after a backup and tested afterwards, off-site backups, security scans, uptime monitoring, speed, form and link checks, small content changes and a report.', 'The difference between plans is in how the work is done: whether updates are tested, backups are stored off-site and restorable, and someone checks forms and checkout.', 'New features, redesigns, large content projects, hosting fees and cleaning an already-hacked site are usually quoted separately.'],
  'domain-hosting-renewal-checklist': ['List every renewal the website depends on, from the domain and hosting to email, licences and smaller services, with its date, cost and login.', 'Switch on auto-renew with a payment method that will not expire, send renewal emails to an address the business checks, and set reminders well ahead.', 'Keep every account and bill in the business name, so renewals never depend on a developer or a former employee remembering to pay.'],
  'hostinger-business-email-setup': ['Create the mailbox in hPanel, then make sure MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC records point at Hostinger, copying the exact values hPanel shows for your domain.', 'Use IMAP, not POP, on phones and Outlook so every device sees the same mailbox, and keep one SPF record that lists every service sending mail for your domain.', 'Send WordPress form emails through your Hostinger mailbox with an SMTP plugin, using the same address as the sender, then test that enquiries reach the inbox, not spam.'],
  'fix-wordpress-critical-error': ['The critical error means PHP hit a fatal error, usually from a plugin, theme, PHP version change or memory limit; your content is almost always safe in the database.', 'Start with the recovery mode email WordPress sends to the admin address; if it never arrives, turn on WP_DEBUG_LOG and read the error in wp-content/debug.log.', 'No log access? Rename the plugins folder in File Manager or SFTP to find the culprit, switch to a default theme, then check PHP version and memory before restoring a backup.'],
  'wordpress-changes-not-showing': ['Most changes that do not show are held back by a cache, so check the page as a visitor would, then clear the plugin, server, CDN and browser caches in that order.', 'If clearing caches does not help, check you edited the right thing: a template, a synced pattern, a translation, a child theme or a staging copy instead of the live site.', 'Changes that show only on desktop, only for you or only for a while each point to a different layer, which tells you where to look first.'],
  'elementor-not-loading-fix': ['When Elementor is stuck on loading, the cause is usually around Elementor rather than in it: low PHP memory, a plugin conflict, a server firewall rule, or caching and CDN scripts.', 'Diagnose before changing things: check the browser console and Network tab, Elementor\'s System Info, and whether the editor opens in Safe Mode.', 'If only one page won\'t load, suspect that page\'s data and restore a revision or backup; if every page fails, work through memory, plugins, server rules, caching and PHP version in order.'],
  'elementor-layout-broken-after-update': ['Most layouts that break after an Elementor update are fixed by regenerating Elementor\'s CSS and data, clearing every cache and making sure Elementor and Elementor Pro are on matching versions.', 'If that does not fix it, look for an add-on, theme or custom CSS that has not caught up, and check whether containers, features or global settings changed with the update.', 'Rolling back is a temporary fix; test major Elementor updates on staging with a backup first so the live site never breaks.'],
  'edit-website-content-wordpress': ['Find out whether each page uses the block editor or Elementor first, because that decides where and how you edit it.', 'Edit content from your own Editor account, preview before you save, and use revisions or Elementor History to undo mistakes.', 'Leave global styles, permalinks, theme files, plugin updates and new layouts alone, or try them on a staging copy first.'],
  'premium-wordpress-themes-guide': ['A premium theme licence buys you updates and support for a period, not just the files, so buy it from the developer or an established marketplace in your business name.', 'Check what the demo installs, which bundled plugins it depends on and how much of your content would be locked into the theme before you pay.', 'If you would spend weeks fighting a theme to match your design, a lightweight theme with Elementor or a custom build is usually the better investment.'],
  'update-premium-theme-safely': ['Premium themes only update automatically once the licence or purchase code is activated; otherwise download the latest ZIP from the vendor and upload it with "Replace current with uploaded".', 'Before updating, take a full backup and check whether anyone edited the theme files directly, because those edits are overwritten; move them into a child theme first.', 'Update the theme, then its bundled plugins, then test key pages, forms and checkout, ideally on staging first when the version jump is large.'],
  'custom-wordpress-theme-development': ['A custom theme is written for one website and contains only what that site needs, so it suits unusual designs, strict speed targets and locked-down editing.', 'Most business sites are better served by a lightweight theme with Elementor or the block editor, because they are quicker to build and easier to change yourself.', 'If you do go custom, keep content structures in a plugin, insist on documentation and version control, and make sure you own the code.'],
  'custom-wordpress-plugin-development': ['Build a custom plugin for what is unique to your business, such as pricing rules, calculators or integrations, and use established plugins for common jobs like SEO, forms and backups.', 'A well-built plugin sanitises input, escapes output, checks permissions, loads code only where needed and is tested on staging before it touches the live site.', 'Cost depends on features, integrations, edge cases and testing, so get a written scope, and make sure you own the code and receive it in version control.'],
  'wordpress-child-theme-explained': ['A child theme inherits everything from its parent theme and holds only your changes, so the parent can be updated without wiping out custom code or template edits.', 'It needs just a folder, a style.css file whose Template line names the parent folder, and a functions.php that loads the stylesheets; WP-CLI or the theme vendor can generate one for you.', 'Use a child theme for theme templates and theme-specific code, not for small CSS tweaks or site-wide features, and test the switch on staging because Customizer settings may not carry over.'],
  'block-themes-full-site-editing': ['A block theme builds the whole site, including headers, footers and templates, from blocks, with design settings in theme.json and editing in the Site Editor.', 'Changes made in the Site Editor are saved in the database, not the theme files, so agencies need a clear rule for where template changes live.', 'Choose a block theme for lean, structured sites with a stable design system; choose Elementor when non-technical clients must rearrange layouts themselves.'],
  'custom-gutenberg-blocks': ['Start with block patterns built from core blocks; move to custom blocks only when you need structured fields, dynamic data or markup editors must not change.', 'ACF blocks are the quickest route for PHP-focused teams, while native blocks built with create-block and block.json give the richest editing experience with no plugin dependency.', 'Lock the design with theme.json settings, template locks and allowed blocks, keep custom blocks in a plugin rather than the theme, and document them at handover.'],
  'custom-post-types-fields': ['Custom post types add new kinds of content, such as properties, courses, doctors or projects, and custom fields store the details each item needs.', 'Plan the content model first: anything visitors filter by should be a taxonomy or a structured field, not a sentence in the description.', 'Register post types in a plugin rather than the theme, give each one proper templates, and decide which archives Google should index.'],
  'plugin-vs-custom-code-wordpress': ['Use a well-maintained plugin for common jobs, a small snippet for tiny tweaks, and a custom plugin for features specific to your business.', 'Judge every option on who will maintain it, what it loads, how secure it is and what happens to your data if you remove it.', 'Custom code belongs in a site plugin, a child theme or a carefully managed snippets plugin, never in the parent theme\'s files.'],
  'website-for-farm-stays-agritourism': ['Farm stays sell experiences, so give each activity its own section with timings, seasons, suitable ages and real photos.', 'Make day visits, overnight stays, school trips and group outings easy to compare, and be clear about what each includes.', 'A detailed "Getting here" page, a seasonal calendar and WhatsApp booking prevent lost guests and set the right expectations.'],
  'website-for-landscaping-gardening-services': ['A project gallery organised by space type, with before, after and "one year later" photos, does most of the selling.', 'Give each service its own page and explain maintenance contracts clearly, including what is included and what costs extra.', 'Local SEO, climate-specific plant guides and compressed images help landscapers and nurseries get found and stay fast.'],
  'website-for-water-purifier-ro-service': ['Service customers are in a hurry, so offer tap-to-call, WhatsApp and a short booking form with the visiting charge stated upfront.', 'Spell out every AMC plan: visits included, parts covered, brands serviced, response time and renewal terms.', 'Be transparent about whether you are an authorised dealer or an independent service business, and never pose as the brand.'],
  'website-for-modular-kitchen-companies': ['Organise galleries by layout and style, and label real installations separately from 3D renders.', 'Explain carcass materials, shutter finishes and hardware clearly to answer buyers\' questions and show expertise.', 'Make a design consultation or showroom visit the main call to action, and be open about what drives the price.'],
  'website-for-glass-aluminium-fabricators': ['Give homeowners and builders separate paths: product pages and site measurement booking for homes, project details and RFQ forms for builders.', 'Explain uPVC vs aluminium and the main glass types simply, and keep performance claims general or backed by manufacturer data.', 'Show real installations by product and project type, spell out warranty and after-sales service, and target product-plus-city searches.'],
  'website-for-steel-fabrication-companies': ['A fabrication website sells capability, so give each process its own page with honest material, thickness and size limits.', 'Keep an up-to-date machinery list and show only the certifications you currently hold, with scope, certifying body and expiry.', 'Build an RFQ form that accepts drawings and asks for material, quantity, scope and delivery date, and handle uploaded files confidentially.'],
  'website-for-painting-contractors': ['A well-captioned before-and-after gallery, organised by job type, does more selling than any paragraph.', 'A short estimate form plus WhatsApp for photos, with a clear explanation of what happens next, turns visitors into site visits.', 'Spell out your preparation process, paint handling and warranty to answer customers\' worries about mess, delays and quality.'],
  'website-for-waterproofing-companies': ['Build service pages around the problems customers see, such as terrace leaks, bathroom seepage and damp walls, not product names.', 'Explain your methods and warranty terms in plain words, including what is not covered, so quotes are easy to compare and trust.', 'Make inspection booking simple with photos via WhatsApp, and run a pre-monsoon campaign before enquiries peak.'],
  'website-for-ac-repair-services': ['Give each AC service its own page, from repairs and gas leak fixes to installation and AMCs, and only claim brand authorisation you actually hold.', 'A short booking form, honest emergency hours and a clear list of areas served turn urgent searches into confirmed visits.', 'Spell out AMC inclusions clearly and change your website with the seasons, from pre-summer servicing to off-season renewals.'],
  'website-for-cctv-security-installers': ['Organise solution pages by customer type (homes, societies, shops, offices, factories) as well as by system.', 'Make booking a site survey the main call to action, with a short form and WhatsApp for site photos.', 'A clear AMC page, honest brand claims and privacy-conscious project photos build the trust security buyers need.'],
  'website-for-it-hardware-computer-dealers': ['Give retail buyers and business buyers separate paths, because they need different pages and calls to action.', 'Make the B2B quote request the main conversion, capturing items, quantities, GSTIN, delivery details and a BOQ upload.', 'Solution pages, clear after-sales service and honest reseller claims help win bigger corporate contracts.'],
  'website-for-signage-companies': ['Organise your portfolio by sign type and sector, with day and night photos of real installations.', 'Quote forms should capture size, indoor or outdoor, lit or non-lit, location and an artwork upload, with WhatsApp for site photos.', 'Pages showing rollout capacity, brand consistency and after-sales support help win retail chains and corporate clients.'],
  'website-for-housing-societies-rwas': ['Decide early which pages are public and which sit behind a member login, and keep minutes, accounts and dues private.', 'An online notice board, maintenance payments with automatic receipts and a ticketed complaint form save the committee the most time.', 'Register the website in the society\'s name, manage member accounts carefully and never publish residents\' personal details.'],
  'website-for-pharma-franchise-companies': ['A searchable product list with composition, dosage form, pack size and packshots is what franchise partners judge you on first.', 'Clear franchise pages and a lead form that asks for district, drug licence status and interests bring better-qualified enquiries.', 'Avoid cure claims, public carts for prescription drugs and certifications you do not hold, and have claims reviewed before launch.'],
  'website-for-yoga-studios-teachers': ['Give each yoga style its own page with a clear level, what a class feels like and who should check with a doctor first, plus a "Your first class" page for beginners.', 'Treat online batches, workshops, retreats and teacher training as separate offers with their own pages, dates, inclusions and refund rules, and send class links only after booking.', 'Describe certifications exactly as held, never claim yoga cures a condition, and keep the timetable as editable text rather than an image or PDF.'],
  'woocommerce-maintenance-checklist': ['Place a test order every week and look for orders stuck in pending payment, which often means a gateway webhook is not reaching your site.', 'Apply WooCommerce, gateway and theme updates on staging first, and watch the System Status report for outdated template overrides.', 'Store backups need special care: restoring an old database wipes out recent orders, so back up often and never overwrite live orders.'],
  'woocommerce-speed-optimization': ['Cart, checkout and My Account pages are built fresh for every shopper, so store speed depends on hosting, an object cache and lean extensions, not just a caching plugin.', 'Cache shop, category and product pages with the right exclusions, then fix the store-specific drags: cart fragments, heavy galleries, slow filters and site search.', 'Keep the database tidy (sessions, transients, scheduled actions, order storage), control third-party scripts and test every change with a real order on staging.'],
  'woocommerce-security-checklist': ['Start with general WordPress hardening, then add the store layer: a checkout where card details are entered with the gateway, not on your server, and two-factor login for every admin and shop manager.', 'Card-testing bots, skimmers and fake orders target the checkout, so add bot protection there, verify every payment in the gateway dashboard and check the checkout page for scripts you did not add.', 'Every plugin that touches checkout, orders or payments runs on your most sensitive page, so keep that list short, buy only from official sources and update with a test order afterwards.'],
  'website-for-car-wash-detailing': ['Present services as clear packages by car size, with separate pages for ceramic coating and PPF.', 'Make booking a slot easy, from a WhatsApp button to live slot booking with deposits for longer jobs.', 'Real before-and-after photos, honest warranty terms and a complete Google Business Profile win local searches.'],
  'website-for-pet-shops-grooming': ['Give the shop, grooming and boarding their own sections, each with one clear action.', 'Grooming forms should capture breed, size, coat condition, temperament and vaccinations so slots and prices are realistic.', 'Boarding pages must answer anxious owners with real photos, daily routines, supervision details and clear policies.'],
  'website-for-toy-stores': ['Organise toys by age, type, interest, price band and occasion, because gift-givers rarely know product names.', 'Product pages should show age range, contents, size, batteries and safety warnings, with certification shown honestly.', 'Gift wrapping, messages, delivery dates and return gift packs turn birthday and festival shoppers into buyers.'],
  'website-for-beauty-cosmetics-brands': ['Beauty shoppers want full ingredient lists, suitability by skin type or shade, and honest swatches before buying from a new brand.', 'Keep claims careful: avoid medical or guaranteed-result claims, and only say "dermatologically tested" or "cruelty-free" when you have the evidence.', 'Bundles, trial sizes, refill reminders and a clear returns policy for opened products help turn first orders into repeat customers.'],
  'website-for-makeup-artists': ['Organise your portfolio by occasion and look, label real brides separately from styled shoots, and credit photographers.', 'Spell out every package in writing, including trials, hairstyling, draping, travel charges, the advance and date-change terms.', 'An enquiry form that collects the date, functions, venue and headcount, plus quick WhatsApp replies, turns Instagram interest into booked dates.'],
  'website-for-tattoo-studios': ['Let visitors filter the portfolio by style and artist, and show healed work alongside fresh photos.', 'A plain-language hygiene and safety page, with clear age and health policies, reassures nervous first-timers.', 'A consultation-first booking flow with deposits, plus a shareable aftercare page, saves time for artists and clients alike.'],
  'website-for-video-production-companies': ['Put a short showreel near the top of the homepage and give key projects their own pages with the brief, approach and deliverables.', 'Host videos on YouTube or Vimeo and use click-to-play previews so work pages stay fast, especially on mobile.', 'Explain your process and what drives cost, and use an enquiry form that asks about video type, deadline and budget.'],
  'website-for-djs-live-bands': ['Show real performance clips organised by occasion, from sangeet and baraat to corporate nights, with a line of context under each.', 'Spell out each package, including hours, sound and lights, performers, travel and overtime, so clients can compare without calling.', 'Ask for date, venue, event type and guest count in the enquiry form, add WhatsApp, and explain how a date is confirmed.'],
  'website-for-artists-art-galleries': ['Give every artwork its own page with medium, dimensions, status, price or "price on request" and detail photos.', 'Choose a selling model that fits: enquiry only, online checkout with a stock of one per original, or prints and editions.', 'Galleries need exhibition, artist and visit pages, and every art site needs descriptive text and optimised images to be found.'],
  'website-for-book-publishers': ['A publisher\'s site serves readers, aspiring authors, booksellers, media and rights buyers, so give each a clear route from the main menu.', 'Every book needs its own page with formats, ISBN, samples and buying options, and every author needs a profile that links their titles.', 'A submissions page with a form, current open or closed status and an honest response time saves hours of inbox sorting and builds trust.'],
  'website-for-corporate-gifting-companies': ['Organise the catalogue by occasion, budget per piece and product type, and show minimum order quantity, branding methods and lead time on each product.', 'A quote form asking for quantity, budget, logo, deadline and delivery addresses, plus an enquiry cart and WhatsApp, turns visitors into bulk orders.', 'Publish Diwali and year-end gifting pages early on a permanent URL, with a clear last date to order for branded gifts.'],
  'website-for-home-decor-stores': ['Let shoppers browse by room, category, style and colour, and use shop-the-look photos and collections to turn browsing into a basket.', 'Natural-light photos, exact dimensions, size guides and honest notes on colour variation help prevent returns over colour and size.', 'Plan shipping and packing for bulky and fragile items, and give showroom visits and measurement services their own pages for local searches.'],
  'website-for-corporate-training-companies': ['Build each programme page like a short proposal, with objectives, formats, customisation and a downloadable outline.', 'Show trainer profiles, client logos and case studies only with permission, and never estimate ROI figures for clients.', 'Use a proposal form that asks for topic, group size, location and timeline so you can reply with a relevant quote quickly.'],
  'website-for-software-training-institutes': ['Give every course its own page with syllabus, projects, format, fees and a clear note on what the certificate is.', 'Describe exactly what placement support includes and show placed students only with written consent, never as a guarantee.', 'Make demo classes easy to book with real slots, WhatsApp confirmations and reminders, and keep the batch calendar current.'],
  'website-for-ielts-spoken-english-institutes': ['Separate test preparation and spoken English courses so each learner finds pages written for their goal and level.', 'Offer bookable mock tests and free level checks, and make clear that mock scores are estimates, not predictions.', 'Share band scores only with consent and without personal details, and never guarantee a score, a visa or a job.'],
  'featured-snippets-how-to-win': ['Featured snippets and People Also Ask answers are usually taken from pages that already rank well, so start with questions you rank on page one for.', 'Put the question in a heading with a direct two-to-three sentence answer right below it, and use real lists and tables where they fit.', 'No special markup creates a snippet; clear, accurate, well-structured content does, and snippets need regular review because Google changes them.'],
  'long-tail-keywords-explained': ['Long-tail keywords are specific, lower-volume searches with clear intent, and together they bring many small businesses their best enquiries.', 'Find them in customer conversations, Google suggestions, People Also Ask and your Search Console queries rather than relying on tool volumes.', 'Group related phrases onto one strong page instead of creating a thin, near-identical page for every variation.'],
  'seo-friendly-urls': ['Use the Post name permalink structure and short, readable, lowercase slugs with hyphens, and settle them before launch.', 'Words in URLs are only a minor ranking signal, so don\'t rewrite well-performing URLs without a clear reason.', 'When you do change URLs, map old to new, add 301 redirects, update internal and external links you control, and monitor 404 errors.'],
  'image-seo-guide': ['Original photos with descriptive file names, natural alt text and relevant nearby text are far more likely to appear in Google Images and Lens than stock images.', 'Use real image elements in modern formats, compressed and sized responsively with width and height set, and never lazy-load the main image at the top of the page.', 'Add images to your sitemap through an SEO plugin, set a strong featured image for Discover and link previews, and track image clicks in Search Console.'],
  'voice-search-local-seo': ['Voice searches are longer, question-based and usually local, and most local answers come from map listings such as your Google Business Profile.', 'Accurate categories, hours, phone numbers and map pins on Google, Apple Maps and Bing decide whether assistants can recommend and connect people to you.', 'Answer real customer questions near the top of your service pages in everyday language, and keep pages fast with easy call and WhatsApp buttons.'],
  'google-business-profile-posts': ['Posts, offers and fresh photos mainly help convert people already comparing profiles; Google has not said posting improves map rankings.', 'Use updates for news, offers for dated deals and events for dated happenings, each with a real photo, one message and one clear button linking to the right page.', 'A short weekly routine of replying to reviews, posting once and adding photos keeps a profile active, and UTM-tagged links show what it brings in.'],
  'eeat-explained-small-business': ['E-E-A-T is Google\'s shorthand for experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust; it is not a single score or something a plugin can add.', 'Real photos, named authors with genuine qualifications, case studies and honest reviews show E-E-A-T far better than claims or badges.', 'Health, finance and legal businesses are held to a higher standard, so qualified review and accurate, up-to-date content matter most there.'],
  'website-trust-signals-checklist': ['Show a full address, an answered phone number, WhatsApp and an email on your own domain so visitors know you are a real business.', 'Real team photos, specific testimonials and verifiable credentials build far more trust than stock images or vague claims.', 'Outdated offers, broken forms, missing policies and security warnings quietly undo the trust the rest of your website builds.'],
  'backlink-audit-disavow': ['For most small businesses, the Links report in Google Search Console is enough for a sensible backlink audit.', '"Toxic link" scores come from third-party tools, not Google, and Google generally ignores spammy links it does not trust.', 'Use the disavow tool only for unnatural links that caused, or are likely to cause, a manual action, and never for genuine links.'],
  'competitor-seo-analysis': ['Find your real search competitors by searching your key phrases from your own area, then focus on the 3 to 5 businesses that keep appearing.', 'Compare their Business Profiles, site structure and the specific pages that rank, looking for content gaps rather than design differences.', 'Never copy their text, fake addresses, fake reviews or bought links; turn the gaps into a short action plan grouped by effort.'],
  'service-area-business-seo': ['If customers never visit you, hide your address on your Google Business Profile and set realistic service areas instead of borrowing an address.', 'A hidden address still counts for distance, so strong service pages are what help you rank in areas beyond your base.', 'Build separate area pages only where you have real jobs, reviews and local detail; otherwise list your areas on one strong page.'],
  'multi-location-business-website': ['For most businesses with a few branches, one website with a genuinely unique page per branch works better than separate sites.', 'Give each branch its own Google Business Profile linked to its own branch page, with matching name, address, phone and hours.', 'Use a location template, LocalBusiness schema on each branch page and a regular check to keep pages and profiles in sync.'],
  'search-console-page-indexing-errors': ['The number of not-indexed pages is not a score; what matters is whether your service pages, products and key posts are indexed.', '"Crawled" and "Discovered – currently not indexed" usually point to thin content, weak internal links or slow hosting, not a technical block.', 'Fix the cause first, test the live URL, then use Validate fix and give Google days to weeks to recheck.'],
  'orphan-pages-fix': ['An orphan page has no internal links pointing to it, so visitors cannot reach it and Google may treat it as unimportant.', 'Find orphans by comparing everything in your sitemap and analytics with the pages a crawler can reach from your homepage.', 'Link useful orphans from their natural parent and related pages, redirect or merge overlapping ones, and delete pages with no value.'],
  'seo-reporting-what-to-track': ['A monthly SEO report should start with enquiries and sales from organic search, not traffic charts or keyword counts.', 'Track Search Console clicks, impressions and non-branded searches, plus Google Business Profile calls and direction requests for local businesses.', 'Question vanity metrics such as total keywords, third-party authority scores and backlink counts, and always compare with the same month last year.'],
  'respond-to-website-enquiries-fast': ['The first business to reply clearly often wins the enquiry, so a dependable routine matters more than instant replies.', 'Make sure every form, WhatsApp, call and social message reaches a named owner, with a backup person and a realistic response target.', 'Prepared reply templates, prompt call-backs, polite follow-ups and a simple enquiry log stop leads slipping away.'],
  'connect-website-forms-to-crm': ['Treat form emails as an alert, and also send every enquiry to a structured place such as a Google Sheet or CRM.', 'Form plugin integrations, connector tools and webhooks all work, but integrations fail quietly, so keep a backup and test after changes.', 'Map fields carefully, lock down access, collect only what you need and ask separately before adding anyone to marketing emails.'],
  'multi-step-forms-lead-qualification': ['Multi-step and conditional forms suit enquiries that need details before you can quote, not simple or urgent requests.', 'Start with an easy choice, ask only questions that change your reply, offer ranges and a "Not sure yet" option, and ask for contact details last.', 'Use the answers to route and prioritise leads, and track where people drop off so you can simplify the weakest step.'],
  'whatsapp-marketing-small-business': ['Most small businesses should start with the free WhatsApp Business app and consider the API only for automated or high-volume messages.', 'Use the catalogue, labels, occasional relevant broadcasts and Status updates to stay in touch, and only message people who have opted in.', 'Link WhatsApp messages to specific pages on your website, tag them with UTM parameters and remove anyone who asks to stop straight away.'],
  'exit-intent-popups': ['Exit-intent pop-ups help when they offer something genuinely useful that matches the page, not when they appear instantly on every visit.', 'On mobile, avoid full-screen pop-ups that hide content; Google treats intrusive interstitials as a poor experience, so use small banners or inline forms.', 'Keep pop-ups off contact, booking and checkout pages, show them once per visitor, and judge them by total enquiries rather than sign-ups alone.'],
  'google-reviews-on-website': ['Showing Google reviews on your site puts verifiable proof in front of visitors who arrive from ads, social media or WhatsApp links.', 'Review widgets can slow pages, so prefer server-side HTML, reserve space, load them only where needed and test speed before and after.', 'Copy reviews word for word, show your real rating, and don\'t expect review stars in Google results for reviews you display about your own business.'],
  'payment-gateways-india-compared': ['Start from how your customers pay, your typical order values and whether you sell overseas, then shortlist gateways that fit.', 'Compare payment methods, fee structure, settlement cycles and KYC requirements, checking each provider\'s current terms rather than headline rates.', 'On WordPress, look for a maintained official WooCommerce plugin with webhooks, refunds and block checkout support, and test in sandbox mode first.'],
  'woocommerce-subscriptions': ['WooCommerce needs a subscription plugin for recurring products, and the gateway integration must support automatic renewals with that plugin, so confirm both together before you build.', 'In India, automatic charges generally run on a mandate the customer approves once, so many stores combine automatic renewals with prepaid plans or emailed renewal links.', 'Easy pausing, honest renewal reminders, a clear cancel option and a GST invoice for every renewal keep subscribers longer and cause fewer payment disputes.'],
  'sell-digital-products-wordpress': ['WooCommerce downloadable products suit a few files or a mix of digital and physical products, while dedicated download plugins suit software with licence keys and LMS plugins suit courses.', 'Serve files through the store rather than public links, require login where it makes sense, set download limits, and make sure delivery emails reach the inbox.', 'Digital products can be taxed and refunded differently from physical goods, so agree GST treatment with your CA and publish a clear refund policy before launch.'],
  'call-tracking-small-business': ['Start by tracking taps on call and WhatsApp buttons as GA4 key events, then add pre-filled WhatsApp messages to tag chats by page or campaign.', 'Call tracking numbers (static or dynamic) show which channels make the phone ring, but add cost and must be managed carefully to protect local SEO.', 'Keep a simple lead log with the source and outcome of every enquiry so clicks turn into a real return-on-investment figure.'],
  'google-ads-conversion-tracking-setup': ['Track only actions that mean business (forms, calls, WhatsApp clicks and purchases) and mark the most valuable as primary conversions.', 'Import GA4 key events for simplicity or use the Google Ads tag for precision, but never count the same action twice.', 'Test every conversion before spending, pass value and order ID for WooCommerce sales, and cover tracking and consent in your privacy policy.'],
  'meta-pixel-conversions-api': ['The Meta Pixel tracks actions in the browser, while the Conversions API sends events from your server and is less affected by ad blockers.', 'Use standard events such as Lead, Contact and Purchase, install the Pixel only once, and deduplicate browser and server events with a shared event ID.', 'Respect consent, never send sensitive details in events or URLs, and remember that WhatsApp and lead form ads need tracking outside your website.'],
  'woocommerce-product-reviews': ['Enable reviews and required star ratings under WooCommerce settings, show the verified owner label, and check how verified-only reviews work with guest checkout.', 'Ask for a review after delivery, not after payment, with a one-tap link by email, WhatsApp (with opt-in) or a parcel card, and send one reminder at most.', 'Publish honest negative reviews with a calm reply, show ratings near the price and in category listings, and only mark up reviews that genuinely appear on the page.'],
  'customize-woocommerce-emails': ['Start with the built-in email settings: your own-domain sender, logo, colours, specific subjects and a helpful footer.', 'Each email should answer the next question: dispatch time, COD amount, tracking link, GST invoice and how to get help.', 'Send through SMTP with SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and add opted-in WhatsApp or DLT-registered SMS updates for key moments.'],
  'woocommerce-multi-vendor-marketplace': ['WooCommerce plus a multi-vendor plugin such as Dokan, WCFM Marketplace, MultiVendorX or WC Vendors can run a marketplace, but decide the commission, payout and tax model before you pick the plugin.', 'Hold vendor earnings until the return window closes, verify each vendor (PAN, GSTIN where applicable, bank details) before the first payout, and ask your CA about GST registration, TCS and invoicing early.', 'Marketplaces put more load on the server than a normal store, so start on scalable hosting with object caching and a staging site, launch with a small group of vetted vendors and grow from there.'],
  'lazy-loading-explained': ['Lazy-load images, maps, video embeds and widgets further down the page so the browser can focus on what visitors see first.', 'Never lazy-load the hero image, first slide, logo or main product photo, because delaying your LCP image makes the page feel slower.', 'Use one lazy-loading method, set image dimensions to avoid layout jumps, and check your LCP element on mobile in PageSpeed Insights.'],
  'web-fonts-performance': ['Every font family, weight and style is a separate download, so limit your site to one or two families and two or three weights.', 'Self-host fonts in WOFF2 format and use font-display so text stays visible while fonts load, with a similar-sized fallback to reduce layout shift.', 'Preload only the one or two fonts used above the fold, replace icon fonts with SVG icons, and stop the theme and builder loading fonts twice.'],
  'third-party-scripts-slow-website': ['Chat widgets, pixels, embeds and ad tags load code from other servers, and together they can slow loading, taps and layout stability.', 'List every script from plugins, header code and Tag Manager, then measure each one in PageSpeed Insights and by blocking it in Chrome DevTools.', 'Remove unused tools, load embeds and widgets only where and when needed, and re-test conversion tracking after delaying any scripts.'],
  'landing-page-ab-testing': ['An A/B test splits visitors at random between two versions running at the same time; comparing this month with last month is not a test.', 'On low-traffic pages, test big changes or simply fix clear problems, because a handful of conversions cannot reliably pick a winner.', 'Decide the goal and duration in advance, don\'t stop early, and judge a winner on lead quality as well as form counts.'],
  'website-heatmaps-session-recordings': ['Heatmaps and session recordings show why visitors leave a page that analytics shows is underperforming.', 'Review recordings with one clear question and filters, and act on patterns seen across many sessions rather than single visits.', 'Keep form inputs masked, skip sensitive pages, mention the tool in your privacy policy and handle consent where required.'],
  'referral-program-website': ['A two-sided reward, given only after the referred customer\'s purchase is complete, feels fair and limits abuse.', 'Use unique codes or links plus a "referred by" field, and make sharing a one-tap WhatsApp message.', 'Block self-referrals, keep review requests separate from rewards, and check professional rules and tax treatment before launching.'],
  'fix-lcp-largest-contentful-paint': ['Find your actual LCP element in PageSpeed Insights first; it differs between pages and between mobile and desktop.', 'Split LCP into server response, load delay, load duration and render delay, because each part points to a different fix.', 'Never lazy-load the hero image; give it high priority, avoid CSS background heroes and cut render-blocking CSS, fonts and animations.'],
  'fix-cls-layout-shift': ['Most CLS on WordPress comes from content without reserved space: images, embeds, ads and sliders need set dimensions or an aspect ratio before they load.', 'Fonts, cookie banners, sticky headers and delayed scripts cause most of the rest: preload the main font with a matched fallback, show notices as overlays, and keep headers a fixed height.', 'Find the exact shifting elements with PageSpeed Insights and the Chrome DevTools Performance panel, fix the shared template, then validate the fix in Search Console.'],
  'fix-inp-interaction-to-next-paint': ['INP reports roughly the slowest tap, click or key press, so test real interactions in DevTools with CPU throttling and on a mid-range phone.', 'Third-party scripts, heavy plugins and delayed JavaScript that all runs on the first tap are the usual causes on WordPress sites.', 'Show instant visual feedback, simplify menus and sliders, and keep page builder layouts lean so phones can respond quickly.'],
  'choose-wordpress-maintenance-provider': ['Ask how updates are tested, where backups are stored and what happens if the site is hacked before you sign.', 'A useful monthly report names the updates, backups, uptime incidents, form tests and next steps, not just "all good".', 'Agree response times for critical, high and routine issues in writing, and watch for lock-in contracts or shared admin logins.'],
  'wordpress-cron-explained': ['WP-Cron runs scheduled tasks only when someone visits, so low traffic and page caching can delay posts, emails and backups.', 'Site Health, WP Crontrol and WooCommerce Scheduled Actions show whether scheduled events are late or failing.', 'Disabling WP-Cron and adding a real server cron job every 5 or 15 minutes makes scheduled tasks run on time.'],
  'wordpress-memory-limit-errors': ['"Allowed memory size exhausted" means a page needed more PHP memory than allowed; the file named is a clue, not always the cause.', 'Check your PHP memory limit and WP_MEMORY_LIMIT in Site Health, then raise them in wp-config.php and your hosting panel.', 'If pages need far more than 256M, find the heavy plugin, import or oversized image instead of raising the limit again.'],
  'wordpress-vs-squarespace': ['Squarespace is a polished all-in-one subscription, but the site only runs on Squarespace; self-hosted WordPress can move to any host or developer.', 'For Indian stores, WordPress with WooCommerce supports Indian gateways, UPI, COD and GST invoices, while Squarespace offers a limited set of payment processors.', 'Compare three to five years of total cost, including your own time, and choose WordPress if your site must generate leads, take payments or grow.'],
  'wordpress-vs-google-sites': ['Google Sites is free, simple and reliable, which makes it fine for event pages, internal sites, quick tests and stopgaps.', 'It has no blog, payments or bookings and very little SEO control, so it struggles once your website needs to bring in enquiries from Google.', 'If you start on Google Sites, connect your own domain from day one, so you can move to WordPress later without losing your address.'],
  'in-vs-com-domain': ['Choose .in if your customers are mainly in India and .com if you sell abroad or plan to go global; prefer .in over .co.in for a new business, and choose a clean name over an awkward .com.', 'Google treats .in as a signal of relevance to India, while a .com relies on content, addresses, links and your Business Profile now that the Search Console country setting has been retired.', 'Where possible, register both in your own name, use one for your website and email, 301-redirect the other with a working SSL certificate, and protect the unused domain from email spoofing.'],
  'mixed-content-warnings-fix': ['Mixed content means an https page still loads some images, scripts or styles over http, which removes the padlock or breaks the layout.', 'Find the insecure files in the browser console, then fix them at the source with a serialisation-safe search-replace and edits to hardcoded theme and script links.', 'Check CDN settings, purge every cache, and treat SSL plugin fixers and the upgrade-insecure-requests header as a stopgap, not the cure.'],
  'too-many-redirects-error-fix': ['ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS is a redirect loop, usually two rules disagreeing about https or www, and it can normally be fixed without losing content.', 'Check the WordPress site URLs, set Cloudflare or similar proxies to Full (strict) SSL instead of Flexible, and keep each redirect in one place only.', 'Test in a private window, trace the loop in the Network tab, rule out plugins and .htaccess rules, then clear every cache layer.'],
  'wordpress-not-sending-emails-smtp': ['WordPress sends email through the server\'s basic mail function by default, so password resets, order emails and form alerts often land in spam or vanish.', 'Send through an authenticated SMTP mailbox or a transactional email service, with SPF, DKIM and DMARC set up for your domain.', 'Use a From address on your own domain, put visitors in Reply-To, keep an email log and test forms and orders regularly.'],
  'ecommerce-website-cost-india': ['The platform, catalogue complexity, design and checkout needs drive most of an online store\'s build cost.', 'Payments, shipping rules and integrations add development time, and some add monthly or yearly fees.', 'Budget for running costs such as hosting, licences, gateway fees and maintenance, not just the build.'],
  'cheap-website-risks': ['A very cheap website is usually cheap because planning, mobile testing, speed, SEO basics, security, licensed software or ownership have been skipped.', 'The savings tend to come back as high renewals in someone else\'s account, paid changes, malware clean-ups, a rebuild and enquiries you never receive.', 'Before paying, open the developer\'s live sites on your phone, confirm the domain, hosting and admin login will be yours, and ask which theme and plugins are used and whether they are licensed.'],
  'website-refresh-vs-redesign': ['A refresh improves the site you have (content, photos, buttons, speed) while the platform, structure and URLs stay; a redesign changes the design, structure and often the theme or platform.', 'Choose a refresh when the site works on phones, updates safely and still matches what you sell; choose a redesign when it fails on phones, runs on an abandoned theme or no longer fits the business.', 'Whichever you choose, record a baseline, work on staging, keep URLs where possible and redirect any that change, so the pages that already rank keep their place.'],
  'dpdp-act-website-basics': ['The DPDP Act, 2023 applies to personal data your website collects through forms, orders, bookings and tracking tools, and its obligations are being phased in, so check the current rules.', 'Consent generally needs a clear notice, a clear action and an easy way to withdraw, and data should only be used for the purpose you stated.', 'Map your data, tidy up forms and tools, secure the site and have a lawyer review your approach rather than relying on templates.'],
  'website-terms-and-conditions': ['Terms of use set the rules for your website, while terms of sale cover orders, payments, delivery and cancellations for stores and bookings.', 'Copied templates often cite the wrong laws, contradict your other policies or include one-sided clauses that may not hold up.', 'Prepare the facts of how your business works, have a lawyer draft or review the wording, and make terms easy to find and accept at checkout.'],
  'website-handover-checklist': ['A proper handover gives you working access to every account, your own Administrator login, a launch backup you control, documentation and training.', 'Log in to each account yourself, change shared passwords and confirm every licence is in your business name before you sign off.', 'Agree the handover list and support terms in writing before work starts, so launch day is a checklist rather than a negotiation.'],
  'stock-photos-vs-real-photos': ['Use real photos wherever a visitor could think "that\'s them" or "that\'s what I\'ll get", such as team, premises, work and products.', 'Stock images are fine for abstract banners, blog posts and hard-to-photograph concepts, but always check the licence terms first.', 'A phone, daylight, a shot list and one organised day can give most businesses usable photos, as long as they are resized and compressed.'],
  'google-tag-manager-basics': ['Google Tag Manager is a free container for tracking code, most useful once you run several tools such as GA4, Google Ads and the Meta Pixel.', 'Tags send data, triggers decide when they fire and variables supply details; use one install method per tool to avoid double counting.', 'Test every change in Preview mode with real actions, and review the container regularly to remove tags that slow the site.'],
  'change-domain-name-without-losing-seo': ['Plan the move by listing every URL, recording benchmarks and keeping the old domain registered, ideally for good.', 'Redirect each old URL one-to-one with a 301 to its new equivalent, then use Search Console\'s Change of Address tool.', 'Update your Google Business Profile, social profiles, directories, ads and printed material, and monitor traffic and enquiries for months.'],
  'website-for-furniture-appliance-rental': ['Show the monthly rent for each tenure, the refundable deposit and any delivery or installation charges on every product page.', 'Collect KYC documents through a secure upload step with online agreement acceptance, and treat ID documents as personal data.', 'Make maintenance requests, relocation, extensions and end-of-tenure pickup easy to find, and target local "on rent" searches.'],
  'website-for-auto-parts-dealers': ['A make, model and year vehicle selector plus part number search is the most useful feature on a parts website.', 'Label every part clearly as genuine, OEM or aftermarket, and only claim authorised status where you actually hold it.', 'Give garages trade accounts with login pricing and quick reorders, and support WhatsApp orders with pre-filled product details.'],
  'website-for-dairy-milk-delivery': ['Let subscribers choose frequency and quantity, see a clear cut-off time, and pause and resume deliveries without calling you.', 'Tell an honest farm story with real photos and testing details, and avoid organic or A2 claims you cannot back up.', 'Show your FSSAI number and delivery areas clearly, and use a trial-offer landing page plus Google Business Profile for local growth.'],
  'website-for-digital-marketing-agencies': ['State a clear niche and give each core service its own page explaining deliverables, reporting, timelines and how pricing works.', 'Honest, permission-based case studies with context persuade sceptical buyers far more than logo walls or unverified ROI claims.', 'Offer a clear first step like an audit or proposal request, qualify it with a short form, and keep your own site fast.'],
  'website-for-study-centres-libraries': ['Define your seat plans, shifts, timings and facilities clearly, and keep seat availability honest and up to date.', 'List every fee upfront, including deposits and locker charges, and make booking a visit or trial day the main call to action.', 'Win "library near me" searches with a complete Google Business Profile, real photos, honest reviews and genuine branch pages.'],
  'website-for-marriage-bureaus': ['Explain exactly how you verify profiles, including what you can\'t check, instead of claiming every profile is 100% verified.', 'Never publish member profiles publicly; share them only with consent, collect data carefully and let members delete their profile.', 'Build trust with a real office, team, clear fees and safety advice, not success-rate counters or guaranteed results.'],
  'website-for-speech-therapy-child-development': ['Worried parents want to see who will work with their child, so show each therapist\'s qualifications, registration, focus areas and languages.', 'Explain assessments and online sessions in plain language, and keep booking short with WhatsApp and phone options.', 'Protect children\'s data, never publish a child\'s photo or story without written consent, and avoid promising outcomes.'],
  'website-for-3d-visualisation-studios': ['Organise the portfolio by what clients buy, such as exteriors, interiors, aerials and walkthroughs, and explain key projects.', 'Host walkthroughs on YouTube or Vimeo with click-to-play previews, and serve compressed web versions of large renders.', 'Spell out your process, revisions and turnaround, and add time zones, NDAs and call booking for overseas clients.'],
  'website-for-grocery-delivery-stores': ['Large grocery catalogues need predictable categories, consistent product names, bulk imports and search that understands local names.', 'Show pin code checks, delivery slots, minimum order values and substitution choices before customers fill their carts.', 'Build trust with sourcing and packing details, your FSSAI number and careful organic claims, and support WhatsApp and repeat orders.'],
  'test-structured-data-rich-results': ['Use the Rich Results Test to see what Google can use for rich results, the Schema Markup Validator to check any schema.org code, and Search Console to watch the whole site.', 'Fix errors before warnings, and never fill a warning with invented data; most errors trace back to a missing field, a badly formatted value, wrong nesting or two plugins outputting the same type.', 'Test one page of every template after launch and after every SEO plugin, theme or WooCommerce update, purge caches first, then use Validate fix in Search Console.'],
  'email-newsletter-small-business': ['Use a dedicated email marketing tool rather than your inbox or hosting server, and place short, specific sign-up forms where interested visitors already are.', 'Only email people who chose to join, keep consent boxes unticked, make unsubscribing easy and authenticate your sending domain.', 'Send useful content consistently, often monthly, and judge success by clicks, replies and enquiries rather than open rates.'],
  'linkedin-b2b-website-traffic': ['A clear personal profile, a complete company page and useful posts give B2B buyers a reason to visit your website.', 'Publish full articles on your own site first and link from LinkedIn to the specific case study or product page a post is about.', 'Track LinkedIn visits with UTM tags and an enquiry source field, and judge results over months rather than weeks.'],
  'category-tag-pages-seo': ['Index category archives that collect several posts and give them a unique intro; noindex thin tag, author and date archives.', 'Clean up tag sprawl by merging duplicates, removing one-off tags and redirecting any archive that still gets traffic.', 'Give each paginated page its own canonical, show the intro only on page one and use plain, crawlable pagination links.'],
  'improve-wordpress-site-search': ['Default WordPress search ignores custom fields, product attributes and typos, which hurts stores and content-heavy sites most.', 'A search plugin plus a synonym list for Hinglish terms, abbreviations and misspellings makes results far more relevant.', 'Track searches in GA4 or your plugin log, and treat zero-result searches as a monthly to-do list.'],
  'hreflang-multilingual-seo-india': ['Give each language its own URL, usually a subfolder like /hi/, and use valid codes such as hi, en-IN, hi-IN and x-default; a country code on its own, like IN, is not valid.', 'Hreflang must be reciprocal, point to canonical, indexable URLs and use one method (page head or XML sitemap); WPML, Polylang, TranslatePress and Weglot add it once translations are linked.', 'Human-reviewed translation and keyword research in Hindi, regional scripts and Hinglish matter more than the tags, and crawler checks now replace Search Console\'s retired International Targeting report.'],
  'website-for-cafes-coffee-shops': ['Answer the pre-visit questions first: opening hours, menu, price range, vibe, location and whether laptops are welcome.', 'Build a cafe-specific menu page with drink options, milk alternatives, eggless markers and seasonal specials, and link table QR codes to it.', 'Use real photos, a current events page and a light Instagram gallery, and keep Google Business Profile details consistent for local searches.'],
  'website-for-hearing-aid-centres': ['Give the hearing test its own page explaining what happens, and offer booking by form, WhatsApp and email, since phone calls can be hard.', 'Explain hearing aid styles, features, trials and what the price includes in plain words, and make clear the right device depends on the test.', 'Show real audiologist qualifications, avoid promises such as perfect hearing, caption every video and keep hearing reports private.'],
  'website-for-homeopathy-clinics': ['Show each practitioner\'s qualifications and registration, and explain how the first consultation, medicines and follow-ups work.', 'Avoid cure promises, "no side effects" claims and outcome-based testimonials, and have your wording reviewed against current advertising rules.', 'Keep enquiry forms short, collect detailed case histories securely, and explain clearly how online consultations and medicines work.'],
  'youtube-video-seo-small-business': ['Clear titles, useful first lines in the description, chapters and accurate captions help videos get found on YouTube and Google.', 'Use a click-to-play preview instead of loading the full YouTube player so embedded videos don\'t slow your pages.', 'Add VideoObject schema and give key videos their own page where the video is the main content.'],
  'instagram-to-website-enquiries': ['Send Instagram visitors to a page that matches the post, not your homepage, with WhatsApp and a short form near the top, and test it inside the Instagram app.', 'Use your bio link, Story link stickers, Highlights, pinned posts and captions as signposts to specific pages, and move serious DM conversations to WhatsApp politely.', 'Tag every link you share with UTM parameters and track form submissions, WhatsApp clicks and calls, so you know which posts bring enquiries and customers, not just likes.'],
  'google-ads-budget-small-business': ['Work out an affordable cost per enquiry from profit per customer, close rate and the share of profit you are willing to spend.', 'Start with one or two profitable services in the area you serve, with tight keywords, negative keywords and working conversion tracking.', 'Increase spend only while cost per enquiry stays below your limit; stop or rethink if a fair test stays well above it.'],
  'website-disaster-recovery-plan': ['A one or two page plan written in advance turns a website emergency into a routine job.', 'Test restores regularly and keep a contact and access list so the right people can act quickly.', 'Prepare customer messages for Google Business Profile, WhatsApp and phone staff before you need them.'],
  'wordpress-activity-logs': ['WordPress doesn\'t keep a change log of logins, users, plugins or settings by default; an activity log plugin records who did what and when, which helps with security, teamwork and troubleshooting.', 'An activity log is different from an error log (PHP errors) and a server log (every request to the server); each answers a different question, and you often need more than one.', 'Log what matters, give everyone their own login, review alerts and a short monthly summary, and set a retention period, because logs contain personal data.'],
  'website-accessibility-audit-checklist': ['Run an automated scan first, then test key pages by hand, because tools catch only some problems.', 'Check keyboard use, contrast, zoom, alt text, forms, headings and a quick screen reader pass.', 'Fix anything that blocks a task first, such as forms or pop-ups keyboard users can\'t get past.'],
  'pincode-delivery-checker-woocommerce': ['A pin code checker answers "can you deliver here, when and with COD?" before shoppers reach checkout.', 'Delivery data can come from your own pin code list, a courier or aggregator API, or WooCommerce shipping zones.', 'Run the check in the background, show date ranges honestly and still block unserviceable pin codes at checkout.'],
  'woocommerce-wholesale-dealer-pricing': ['Agree your customer groups, price rules and approval process on paper before configuring anything.', 'Dealer roles, role-based or quantity-based prices and minimum quantities handle most wholesale needs.', 'Test logged out and as each role so trade prices never leak through caching, feeds or invoices.'],
  'woocommerce-order-management-workflow': ['Make sure the whole team reads WooCommerce order statuses the same way, and add only custom statuses with a clear owner.', 'A fixed packing and dispatch routine with COD confirmation and tracking updates prevents most order mistakes.', 'Give staff their own limited logins and review dispatch times, returns and RTOs every week.'],
  'membership-website-wordpress': ['A membership site sells ongoing access, so choose a plugin that fits your levels, content rules and the Indian gateways you need for renewals.', 'Gate content by rule, show public teasers for SEO, protect downloadable files and plan fixed-term or manual renewals as a fallback to automatic billing.', 'Keep joining, login and cancellation simple, secure member accounts and plan hosting for logged-in users before adding extra features.'],
  'online-course-website-wordpress': ['Compare LMS plugins against your real needs, such as quizzes, certificates, drip scheduling and payments, and total up the add-ons you will pay for.', 'Host course videos on a dedicated service with domain restrictions and adaptive streaming rather than in the WordPress media library.', 'Plan payments, GST, refunds and student support before launch, and use better hosting because logged-in students bypass page caching.'],
  'woocommerce-product-bundles-upsells': ['Upsells suggest a better alternative on the product page, cross-sells suggest complementary items in the cart, and bundles sell several items as one.', 'WooCommerce linked products cover simple suggestions, while bundle plugins handle combined pricing, per-item stock and build-your-own boxes.', 'Price bundles honestly with no inflated savings or pre-ticked add-ons, and measure average order value, attach rate and profit per order.'],
  'website-for-electrical-contractors': ['Give each type of electrical work its own service page and state your licence class, number and validity exactly.', 'Project pages with real scope, scale, constraints and photos prove capacity better than claims about quality.', 'An RFQ form with BOQ and drawing uploads, a safety page and a clear AMC offer turn visits into business.'],
  'website-for-cold-storage-warehousing': ['Storage buyers compare facilities on specifics, so publish real capacity, chamber details and temperature set points.', 'Give each facility its own location page with access details, and list only registrations and certifications you hold.', 'A space enquiry form that captures commodity, quantity, temperature and duration lets your team quote in one go.'],
  'website-for-tempo-traveller-bus-rental': ['Organise fleet pages by seating capacity, with real photos and honest details of comfort features.', 'Build route and trip pages only for trips you run, and explain every extra charge so organisers can compare fairly.', 'Short quote forms, WhatsApp booking and clear driver and safety information win the trust of group organisers.'],
  'website-for-fire-safety-companies': ['Organise the site into products, systems, services and property types so societies, factories and offices find what they need quickly.', 'Give installation, refilling and AMC their own pages, and word compliance and fire NOC support carefully without promising outcomes.', 'Make urgent breakdown contact easy, but state clearly that in a fire people should call 101 or 112 first.'],
  'website-for-recycling-ewaste-companies': ['A clear page of materials accepted, plus a short pickup form and WhatsApp option, drives most household and small business pickups.', 'Show only the registrations and authorisations you actually hold, with your real role in the recycling chain.', 'Corporate clients want a documented process and paperwork, and any data destruction claims must match exactly what you do.'],
  'wordpress-backup-plugins-compared': ['Host backups, backup plugins and external backup services each store and restore data differently, so many sites use two layers.', 'Tools like UpdraftPlus, BlogVault, Jetpack VaultPress Backup and Duplicator suit different needs, so check current plans before choosing.', 'Off-site copies, failure alerts and regular test restores matter more than which brand you pick.'],
  'website-for-ambulance-services': ['Put a large 24/7 tap-to-call button on every page and make sure the number is always answered.', 'List only the services, vehicle types, equipment and staff you actually have, with real photos of your fleet.', 'Explain what happens when someone calls and how charges work, without promising fixed response times.'],
  'website-for-specialist-doctors': ['Show specific, verifiable credentials such as degrees, registration number and fellowships instead of vague "best doctor" claims.', 'Give each major condition and procedure its own plain-language page, reviewed and dated by the doctor.', 'Add a second opinion page and a clear list of the clinics and hospitals where you consult and operate.'],
  'website-for-kids-activity-classes': ['Organise programme pages by activity and age group, and keep batch timetables and camp dates up to date.', 'Make booking a trial class the main action, with quick confirmation, a reminder and a follow-up.', 'Explain supervision and pick-up rules, and publish children\'s photos or details only with written parental consent.'],
  'website-for-sports-bicycle-stores': ['Organise the catalogue by sport, player and level, and be honest about stock if it isn\'t synced with your billing.', 'Fitting guides based on each brand\'s size chart, plus servicing bookings, reduce returns and bring customers back.', 'A team and school order page, clear returns rules and a strong Google Business Profile win local business.'],
  'website-for-electronics-appliance-stores': ['Build the catalogue around the specs buyers compare, with exact model numbers, dimensions and warranty terms.', 'Word EMI, cashback and exchange offers precisely, with lenders, dates and conditions, and remove expired offers.', 'Clear installation, delivery and branch details plus WhatsApp price checks turn online research into store sales.'],
  'website-for-perfume-fragrance-brands': ['Describe each scent by family, notes, mood and occasion, and avoid trademark comparisons without legal advice.', 'Discovery sets, trial sizes and gift options lower the risk of buying a fragrance you can\'t smell first.', 'Keep claims honest and confirm with your courier in writing how perfumes and attars can be shipped.'],
  'website-for-translation-services': ['Organise pages by language pair, service and document type, the way clients search, without mass-produced doorway pages.', 'Describe exactly what your certification includes and never guarantee acceptance, as the receiving authority decides.', 'Build a quote form around secure document uploads, and make sure file handling matches your confidentiality promises.'],
  'website-for-civil-engineering-consultants': ['Give each discipline, from structural design to surveys and soil testing, its own detailed service page.', 'Show your exact role, scale and challenges on project pages, and state registrations and empanelments precisely.', 'Use separate enquiry forms for design, structural audits and surveys so you collect the details needed to quote.'],
  'website-for-lift-elevator-companies': ['Organise products by use and give homeowners and commercial buyers separate paths with the details each needs.', 'Explain comprehensive and non-comprehensive AMCs side by side, and describe safety and compliance only in terms you can back up.', 'Keep a breakdown number or button visible on every page, separate from sales, with honest support hours.'],
  'search-console-content-ideas': ['Queries with many impressions but few clicks point to weak titles, mismatched pages or missing content.', 'Pages ranking around positions 8 to 20 are often the quickest wins when you strengthen the existing page.', 'Filter queries for question words to find FAQ entries and new article topics people already search for.'],
  'rank-for-your-business-name': ['Search your name on mobile in a private window to see what customers really find.', 'Put your business name in titles, visible text and schema, and keep it identical across every listing.', 'A complete Google Business Profile, social profiles and a clear About page help you own branded searches.'],
  'wordpress-seo-plugins-compared': ['Yoast SEO, Rank Math and All in One SEO all handle the basics; configuration matters more than the choice.', 'Never run two SEO plugins at once, as duplicate titles, schema and sitemaps can confuse Google.', 'Switch plugins with a backup, an import, a check of key pages and redirects, and close monitoring afterwards.'],
  'website-for-uniform-manufacturers': ['Organise the catalogue by sector, such as schools, offices, hospitals and hotels, with real photos, fabrics, size charts and customisation options.', 'Explain sampling, bulk ordering, lead times and shade consistency step by step, and use a form that captures quantities, logos and delivery dates.', 'Offer a parent ordering shop and name schools only when the tie-up is real and you have permission.'],
  'website-for-borewell-drilling': ['Give each service its own page and explain how charges are worked out, even if you don\'t publish exact rates.', 'Describe your survey method, process and real equipment honestly, and never promise that a borewell will find water.', 'Word permit information carefully, make calls and WhatsApp location sharing effortless, and list only the areas you actually serve.'],
  'website-for-franchise-brands': ['Give prospective partners a dedicated franchise section with a page per format covering space, role, staffing, territory and agreement basics.', 'Break down investment and support honestly, label figures as indicative and avoid any promise of returns or payback.', 'Use a qualifying application form, an accurate outlet locator and clear disclosures, including a warning about fake franchise offers.'],
  'website-for-tiles-sanitaryware-showrooms': ['Organise the catalogue by room, look, size and finish, with box coverage and honest notes about batch shade variation.', 'List only the brands you are authorised to sell, and write your own notes instead of copying brand catalogues.', 'Make booking a showroom visit the main action, and give builders and architects a separate project enquiry route.'],
  'website-for-mattress-sleep-brands': ['Describe feel consistently with your own firmness scale, layer details and honest "may not suit" notes.', 'Spell out trial, return and warranty conditions before purchase, not when a customer asks to return.', 'Only make health, certification or performance claims you can back up, and never promise to cure back pain.'],
  'digital-pr-small-business': ['Digital PR earns coverage by giving journalists genuine stories, your own accurate data and useful expert comment.', 'Pitch local news, trade publications and podcasts with short, personal emails, and never demand a link.', 'Avoid paid link packages, undisclosed paid articles, made-up surveys and pay-to-enter award lists.'],
  'website-for-tour-guides-heritage-walks': ['Give every walk or experience its own page with duration, meeting point, languages, group size, inclusions and a clear booking button.', 'Offer UPI for Indian guests and check international card support for foreign visitors, with clear cancellation and weather rules.', 'Mention licences only if you hold them, show genuine reviews and target specific searches like "heritage walk in {city}".'],
  'website-microcopy-that-converts': ['Button labels should start with a verb and say what the visitor gets, such as "Get my free quote" instead of "Submit".', 'Error messages should explain how to fix the problem next to the field, without clearing what the visitor has typed.', 'Confirmations and empty states should say what happens next or point somewhere useful, and every promise must be true.'],
  'rfq-forms-b2b-websites': ['Ask for product, quantity, unit, delivery location and contact details, and leave out anything you will not use to quote.', 'Allow drawing and specification uploads, pre-fill the product on product pages and route each RFQ to the right salesperson.', 'Confirm receipt instantly, reply personally fast and use invisible spam protection that does not block genuine overseas buyers.'],
  'website-for-cooking-baking-classes': ['Give every workshop its own page with the menu, level, fee, seats left and what to bring, and keep the calendar current.', 'Let students book and pay for a seat online, with seat limits, reminders and a clear cancellation policy.', 'Give corporate, kids and private sessions their own pages and use free recipes and videos to get found on Google.'],
  'website-for-recording-studios': ['Show real photos of each room and list only the equipment that is actually in the studio and working.', 'Share sample work only with written permission, credit your role honestly and keep audio players light.', 'Explain booking rules and what affects cost so clients can request the right slot without a fixed rate card.'],
  'lead-scoring-small-business': ['Score each enquiry on a few fit and intent signals, such as a clear requirement, timeline, location and budget.', 'A simple Google Sheet with 0 to 2 points per signal and three follow-up bands is enough to start.', 'Use scores to prioritise, never to ignore: every lead gets a reply, and small leads can grow into big ones.'],
  'website-for-baby-products-brands': ['Organise the store by age, stage and need, using the manufacturer\'s age guidance consistently.', 'Show full ingredients, materials and label warnings, and make claims like "hypoallergenic" only with evidence.', 'Gift hampers, refill reminders and subscriptions suit how parents and families actually buy baby products.'],
  'website-for-inverter-battery-dealers': ['Group inverters, UPS systems and batteries by backup need rather than by model number or VA rating.', 'A simple load calculator helps, as long as every result is labelled as an estimate and confirmed by you.', 'Spell out warranty terms from the manufacturer and call yourself authorised only for brands you truly represent.'],
  'image-compression-plugins-compared': ['WordPress creates image sizes and applies light compression, but does not bulk-optimise or convert old images.', 'ShortPixel, Imagify, EWWW and Smush differ mainly in where processing happens, credit limits and format delivery.', 'Use a single optimisation tool, test WebP or AVIF delivery, and let an existing image CDN do the job if you have one.'],
  'website-for-wedding-choreographers': ['Families want to know you can teach beginners and older relatives, how many sessions they need and whether you come home, use a studio or teach online.', 'Host performance videos on a video platform with click-to-play previews, and get each family\'s permission before publishing wedding footage.', 'Clear packages, an enquiry form that asks for the sangeet date and group size, and honest reviews turn Instagram interest into bookings.'],
  'website-for-pandit-puja-services': ['Organise service pages by occasion, explaining each ritual simply along with its duration, languages and whether it can be done online.', 'A short booking form, printable samagri lists and WhatsApp confirmations make home and online pujas easier for families.', 'Explain what affects dakshina and what is included without publishing fixed prices, and avoid guaranteed results or fear-based claims.'],
  'woocommerce-gift-cards': ['WooCommerce needs a plugin for gift cards and store credit; look for partial redemption, balance tracking and clear usage logs.', 'State expiry and usage terms consistently everywhere, have them reviewed by a lawyer, and ask your CA how GST and unused balances should be handled.', 'Prevent fraud with random codes, attempt limits, value caps, manual checks on unusual orders and no cash on delivery for gift cards.'],
  'website-for-tractor-farm-equipment-dealers': ['Group tractors and implements by horsepower and farm job, with a quick on-road price enquiry on every model page.', 'Word finance and subsidy information carefully: name only real lenders, mark EMIs as indicative and never promise subsidy amounts.', 'Make calls and WhatsApp the main actions, add Hindi or regional language pages, and give each branch its own Google Business Profile.'],
  'website-for-event-organisers-ticketing': ['Give every event its own page with date, venue, ticket tiers, line-up, entry rules and FAQs written as real text.', 'Choose between a ticketing platform, selling on your own WordPress site or a hybrid, and spell out refund, transfer and postponement terms.', 'Prepare for launch-day traffic with caching, capable hosting, lighter ticket pages and a fallback plan if checkout struggles.'],
  'wordpress-plugin-conflicts-troubleshooting': ['Back up, clear caches and reproduce the problem on staging before switching plugins on and off.', 'Use Health Check troubleshooting mode or disable plugins one at a time with a default theme to find the clashing pair.', 'Check error logs and the browser console, report details to the plugin developers, and replace abandoned or overlapping plugins.'],
  'website-for-gym-equipment-sellers': ['Give home buyers and commercial buyers (gyms, societies, offices) separate paths, with filters for the specs they actually compare.', 'Sell gym setup consultations and use a staged quote form that captures facility type, space, users, budget and timeline.', 'State warranty terms per part from each manufacturer, and give installation, service and AMC their own pages.'],
  'website-for-lab-scientific-equipment-suppliers': ['Build product pages around consistent specification tables, free datasheet downloads and a quote button that carries the model number.', 'List only brands you are authorised to sell, write your own descriptions, and state any calibration accreditation scope accurately.', 'Make institutional and tender buying easy with document-upload enquiry forms, proforma and PO handling, and fast replies.'],
  'rebranding-your-website': ['Decide the scope first: a new logo, a new name or a new domain each bring different amounts of work and SEO risk, and keeping your existing URLs is the single best way to protect rankings.', 'Map every URL that changes to its closest new page with a 301 redirect, and if the domain changes, verify the new domain in Search Console and use the Change of Address tool from the old property.', 'Update every mention of the old name (titles, schema, emails, invoices, listings), keep "formerly Old Name" visible, and follow a before, launch-day and after checklist while you monitor Search Console.'],
  'website-for-drone-services': ['Give aerial shoots, surveys, inspections and spraying their own pages, each showing real sample footage, maps or reports.', 'Explain the process step by step and say exactly what clients receive, in which formats and how soon.', 'State your DGCA registration and pilot certification honestly, and explain that airspace zones are checked before every job.'],
  'website-for-aquarium-shops': ['Treat livestock and dry goods as two different businesses on one site: keep permanent species pages with a simple stock status, and sell tanks, equipment and food through a normal online store.', 'Offer live fish for store pickup or your own local delivery only, set up as a separate shipping class, and state your arrival policy plainly; courier shipping of live animals is a special case to check first.', 'Setup and maintenance services, species and care guides, a complete Business Profile and WhatsApp for photo questions bring the local enquiries that a stock list alone never will.'],
  'website-for-musical-instrument-stores': ['Organise the catalogue by instrument family first, then brand, with filters for price, level and type.', 'Label items clearly as in stock, available to order or display pieces, rather than marking a whole distributor catalogue as in stock.', 'Use in-store demo videos, and give repairs, rentals and teacher partnerships their own pages to bring customers back.'],
  'website-for-domestic-help-agencies': ['Explain exactly which checks you do (ID, references, police verification status) instead of promising "100% verified" staff.', 'Publish a clear replacement policy and fee structure, including whether workers pay anything, and keep it consistent with your agreement.', 'Protect workers\' dignity and privacy: no ID documents, surnames or religion filters on profiles, and data stored securely.'],
  'seo-for-pdfs-brochures': ['Google can index PDFs with real, selectable text, but a web page is usually the better landing page for visitors.', 'Set a clear file name and document title, keep file sizes small, and include contact details and links inside the PDF.', 'Keep PDF URLs stable when updating, redirect old versions, and use an X-Robots-Tag header for PDFs that should not be indexed.'],
  'meta-catalog-instagram-shopping-woocommerce': ['A Meta catalogue powers catalogue ads and, where available, Instagram and Facebook shopping features; check what Meta currently offers in India.', 'Use one connection method, and make sure catalogue IDs match the content IDs your Pixel and Conversions API send.', 'Check syncing after bulk changes and sales, mark out-of-stock items correctly, and review Commerce Manager diagnostics weekly.'],
  'website-for-dog-trainers': ['Give each programme, from puppy foundation to behaviour consultations, its own page with honest expectations about progress.', 'Explain your methods and equipment plainly, and list only qualifications you actually hold, with the issuing organisation.', 'Start bookings with an assessment form, and share training videos only with the owner\'s written permission.'],
  'hindi-devanagari-fonts-website': ['Use Unicode text rather than legacy fonts like Kruti Dev or text inside images, so Hindi content is readable and searchable.', 'Choose a Devanagari font with clean conjuncts and matras, and give Hindi text a slightly larger size and more generous line height.', 'Load Devanagari fonts separately with unicode-range, avoid over-subsetting, and test on real budget phones before launch.'],
  'woocommerce-product-filters': ['Pick filters based on how buyers actually choose products, and back them with consistent global attributes.', 'Filtered pages bypass page caching, so use indexed filter tools, fewer options and object caching to keep them fast.', 'Keep filter URLs out of the index with noindex or canonicals, block crawl-heavy parameters carefully, and build real category pages for combinations worth ranking.'],
  'contact-page-that-gets-enquiries': ['Lead with one or two contact options your best customers actually use, such as click-to-call or WhatsApp, and keep the form short.', 'Show your address, landmark, map and hours, and tell visitors honestly when and how you will reply.', 'Place a few trust signals beside the form and track form submissions, call taps and WhatsApp clicks.'],
  'meet-the-team-page': ['Feature the people customers will meet or rely on, with consistent real photos instead of stock or AI-generated faces.', 'Write short bios that explain what each person does for customers, with qualifications stated exactly and only where current.', 'Get written consent from staff, avoid personal contact details, and update the page promptly when people join or leave.'],
  'google-business-profile-photos': ['Cover the full set: a square logo, a representative cover photo, exterior shots from where customers arrive, interior, team, at-work, and real products or finished work.', 'Follow the current guidance from Google on formats and sizes, keep photos real and lightly edited, and add a few new ones whenever something changes rather than uploading hundreds at once.', 'Geotags and keyword file names have no confirmed ranking benefit; what helps is honest, current photos that match your website and win the click, the call and the visit.'],
  'website-for-plumbers': ['Make calling effortless with a sticky call button, WhatsApp for photos of the problem and honest working hours.', 'Explain how visit charges, parts and night call-outs work instead of publishing prices you might not honour.', 'Real before-and-after job photos, steady Google reviews and a complete Google Business Profile win local searches.'],
  'website-for-carpenters': ['Organise your portfolio by project type and label each job with the material, finish and area.', 'Explain materials, finishes and the measurement-to-installation process so customers trust what they are paying for.', 'Use enquiry forms that ask for rough dimensions, photos and deadlines so you can reply with useful answers.'],
  'website-for-bike-rentals': ['Give each vehicle type real photos, rates by rental period and a clear list of what is included.', 'State documents, deposits, damage and fine responsibilities in plain language, and check local transport rules first.', 'Show availability, pickup points with maps and a booking confirmation that includes everything renters need.'],
  'website-for-stationery-shops': ['Class-wise school kits, list uploads and pickup slots move the back-to-school rush online.', 'Office buyers need a quote form, business pricing, easy reordering and GSTIN on invoices.', 'Organise products by category and brand, and make local delivery and WhatsApp ordering simple.'],
  'website-for-bookstores': ['Show stock honestly: in the shop, available to order, or second-hand, and keep counts in step.', 'Searching by ISBN, author and edition matters, along with school and exam book lists.', 'Staff picks, events and used-book buying are strengths an independent shop should put first.'],
  'website-for-plant-nurseries': ['Plant pages need real stock photos, pot sizes and clear light, water and care information.', 'Deliver large live plants locally, courier only what travels well, and publish an arrival policy.', 'Seasonal collections, garden setup services and corporate gifting bring repeat and bulk orders.'],
  'website-for-car-service-garages': ['Show the makes and services you cover without implying you are an authorised service centre.', 'Explain a clear estimate and approval process so customers never face extra work they did not agree to.', 'Make slot booking and pickup and drop easy, explain genuine and aftermarket parts honestly, and collect reviews.'],
  'website-for-loan-agents-dsa': ['Name lenders only with permission and accurately, and say clearly that rates and approval are decided by the lender.', 'Explain loan types and document checklists in plain language, and keep enquiry forms short with no PAN, Aadhaar or OTPs.', 'Avoid promises like guaranteed approval or lowest rates, and check with lenders what RBI rules and their policies allow you to advertise.'],
  'website-for-two-wheeler-dealers': ['Use on-road price enquiry forms rather than publishing prices you cannot keep up to date.', 'Model pages, test ride booking, finance and exchange forms and service booking turn browsers into showroom visits.', 'Authorised dealers should follow the manufacturer\'s brand guidelines on logos, images, offers and domain names.'],
  'wordpress-auto-updates': ['Leave minor core updates, translations and small, well-maintained plugins on auto-update, but update major core releases, page builders, WooCommerce and your theme by hand after testing.', 'Auto-updates only roll back fatal errors, so broken layouts, silent form failures and expired premium licences still need daily backups, uptime monitoring, a monitored admin email and a weekly check.', 'Use staging for stores, major versions and PHP changes, check exactly what your host\'s managed updates cover, and keep a recent backup, because restoring it is the most reliable rollback.'],
  'choose-wordpress-developer-pune': ['Judge Pune developers on live work, communication and process first; being local is a useful bonus, not the deciding factor.', 'A fair proposal lists pages, features, revisions, content responsibility, milestones, exclusions, GST and the support period, so compare scope rather than totals.', 'Make sure the domain, hosting, admin login, licences and Google accounts are in your name, and agree who maintains the site before launch.'],
  'website-content-calendar-small-business': ['Build a question bank from real customer questions so every blog post, FAQ and Google post answers something people actually ask.', 'Choose a publishing level you can keep up for a year, and mix blog posts, case studies and Google Business Profile posts that point back to your website.', 'Spend 20 minutes a month in Search Console spotting rising pages, low-click queries and missing topics to decide what to write next.'],
  'woocommerce-wishlist': ['A wishlist helps most on gifting, wedding, baby and high-consideration stores where buyers shortlist before buying.', 'Let guests save items without logging in, then offer an account or OTP login to keep the list across devices.', 'Send back-in-stock and price-drop reminders only with clear consent, and keep the plugin from breaking page caching.'],
  'wordpress-white-screen-of-death': ['A blank white page usually means PHP hit a fatal error; your content is almost always still safe.', 'Check the recovery mode email first, then log errors privately with WP_DEBUG_LOG to find the failing plugin or theme.', 'Rename plugin or theme folders by FTP to get back in, and restore a backup carefully on stores taking orders.'],
  'error-establishing-database-connection-fix': ['The error means WordPress cannot reach its database, usually through wrong wp-config.php details or a struggling server.', 'Rule out the host first, then match the database name, user, password and host against your hosting panel.', 'Use WordPress\'s repair tool for damaged tables, remove it straight after, and restore the database only as a last step.'],
  'website-for-footwear-stores': ['Give each brand its own size chart and honest fit notes, and put the size guide right next to the size buttons.', 'Track every size and colour as a WooCommerce variation with its own stock, and grey out sold-out sizes rather than hiding them.', 'Make size exchanges quick and visible, and use a store locator with branch pages so shoppers can try pairs on in person.'],
  'website-for-saree-ethnic-wear-stores': ['Photograph sarees in consistent neutral light with drape, border, pallu and weave shots, and check colours against the real fabric.', 'List fabric, weave, zari, blouse piece and care details on every product, and offer clear blouse stitching options with timelines.', 'Use handloom labels, Silk Mark and GI names only where genuinely held, and offer WhatsApp video calls for high-value purchases.'],
  'website-for-watch-stores': ['Build brand pages within your dealer agreement and claim authorised status only for brands and branches it actually covers.', 'Explain warranty terms, registration and repair services such as battery replacement clearly, with a repair request form.', 'Grade pre-owned watches with a published scale and real photos, and keep high-value pieces enquiry-led with fast, personal replies.'],
  'local-seo-pune-businesses': ['Distance shapes local results, so plan for the Pune and PCMC areas you genuinely serve rather than the whole city.', 'Build area pages only where you have real local jobs, reviews and details; swapped-name pages are doorway pages.', 'Choose Marathi, Hindi or English based on how your customers actually talk to you, and track which areas bring enquiries.'],
  'not-showing-on-google-maps': ['First work out whether it is a profile problem (unverified, suspended, duplicate) or a ranking problem.', 'Check the primary category, business name, map pin and service-area settings before anything else.', 'Google does not publish exact ranking weights; distance cannot be changed, but reviews, consistent details and your website can.'],
  'woocommerce-order-notifications-sms-whatsapp': ['Send a few useful updates (confirmed, COD confirmation, shipped with tracking, delivered) rather than every internal step.', 'Automated WhatsApp needs the Business API with approved templates, and business SMS in India generally needs DLT registration.', 'Get opt-in at checkout, keep offers out of order updates, and make sure transactional email actually reaches the inbox.'],
  'seo-pricing-models-india': ['SEO is usually priced as a monthly retainer, a one-off audit, a fixed project fee or a performance-based deal, and each suits different needs.', 'Whatever the model, insist on listed deliverables and monthly reports that show enquiries and sales from search, not just charts and scores.', 'Walk away from guaranteed #1 rankings, secret techniques, bulk backlink packages and providers who keep your accounts in their name.'],
  'website-payment-terms-milestones': ['Most website projects in India start with an advance, then tie each later payment to something you can see and check, such as an approved design or a working staging site.', 'Write down what each milestone means, how changes are quoted, what happens when either side is late, and what is refunded if the project stops early.', 'Ask for an invoice for every payment, confirm whether GST is added, and keep the domain, hosting and licences in your own name whatever the payment schedule.'],
  'hidden-website-costs': ['A website keeps costing money after launch: domain and hosting renewals, premium licences, business email and maintenance all recur.', 'Check renewal prices, keep licences in your business\'s name, and remember that SSL is often included free by good hosts.', 'List every running cost with how often it is billed, and budget for at least one way of bringing visitors, such as SEO or ads.'],
  'website-for-ice-cream-parlours': ['Run the flavour menu as an editable "flavour board" with today, coming back and seasonal sections, and reuse the same seasonal page every year so it keeps its search history.', 'Give party, bulk and event orders their own page with a quote form that asks for date, guests, flavours and delivery area, and keep your own ordering ahead of delivery app links.', 'Show your FSSAI details, describe frozen desserts and dietary options accurately, and keep hours and flavours consistent across your website, Google and delivery apps.'],
  'website-for-mehendi-artists': ['Organise the portfolio by style, such as bridal, Arabic and minimal, and show real brides with the developed stain, not just fresh paste.', 'Explain how travel charges, guest mehendi and group bookings work in writing, even if you quote the exact amount per booking.', 'Describe your henna paste accurately, say you avoid "black henna" only if true, offer patch tests and never promise the darkest colour.'],
  'website-for-chess-academies': ['Group programmes by what students can already do, not only by age, and explain how new students are assessed and placed.', 'List FIDE titles and ratings only if genuinely held, state which rating type it is, and add the FIDE ID so parents can verify it.', 'Present tournament results with the event, year, category and placing, and only for students who genuinely trained with you.'],
  'wordpress-xmlrpc-explained': ['XML-RPC is an older remote-access feature that most business sites no longer need; Jetpack, some apps and pingbacks are the main things that may still use it, and the REST API has replaced it for most jobs.', 'Floods of POST requests to xmlrpc.php in your access logs, slowdowns or resource-limit errors are signs that bots are using it for password guessing that can bypass login protection.', 'Block it at the server or firewall to save resources, or switch it off with a security plugin or code snippet, then test Jetpack, apps and connected services; restrict instead of blocking if something needs it.'],
  'wordpress-user-accounts-audit': ['List every account by role and remove former staff, old developers, agencies and test logins that no longer need access.', 'Give each person their own account with the lowest role they need, and attribute content to a current user when deleting.', 'Treat unknown administrators as a hack sign to investigate, and repeat the audit quarterly and whenever someone leaves.'],
  'stop-spam-user-registrations-wordpress': ['Most business sites can untick "Anyone can register", and the default role should always be Subscriber or Customer.', 'Protect every registration form with honeypots, low-friction challenges, rate limiting and, where it fits, email verification.', 'Back up before deleting spam accounts, keep customers with orders, and investigate any spam account with a higher role.'],
  'agency-wordpress-care-plans': ['A care plan bundles updates, backups, uptime and security monitoring, functional checks and an allowance of small edits into one monthly service.', 'Build tiers around scope and response times, from brochure sites to stores, and define exactly what counts as a small edit.', 'A white-label partner works through your agency with separate logins and an NDA, while you send the branded report and spell out exclusions.'],
  'agency-developer-handoff-checklist': ['Hand over one approved design file with mobile frames, interactive and form states, and easily forgotten templates like 404, search and archives.', 'Settle font, stock image and premium plugin licences, final copy, the sitemap and any redirect map before development starts.', 'Agree acceptance criteria, a browser and device list, access arrangements and a timeline with set review rounds upfront.'],
  'website-qa-checklist-before-launch-agencies': ['Run internal QA on staging after the developer finishes and before the client sees the build, using someone who did not build it.', 'Check design fidelity, responsive and cross-browser behaviour, forms, emails, analytics, speed, SEO basics such as noindex and redirects, and accessibility.', 'Log each issue with URL, device, screenshot, severity and type, send them in one batch and retest fixes before sharing the link.'],
  'reduce-server-response-time-ttfb': ['TTFB covers redirects, connection setup and the time WordPress takes to build the page, and it delays everything else, including LCP.', 'Check it logged out, in PageSpeed Insights and the Network tab of browser dev tools, and confirm pages are really served from the cache.', 'Fix it in order of impact: working page caching, better hosting near your visitors, slow plugins and queries, then dynamic and WooCommerce pages.'],
  'eliminate-render-blocking-resources': ['Render-blocking CSS and scripts in the page head make the browser wait before showing anything, delaying the first content and often LCP.', 'Use critical CSS for the first screen, defer most scripts, delay chat and marketing tags until interaction, and load fonts sensibly.', 'Use one optimisation plugin, change one setting at a time, and test menus, sliders, forms, checkout and tracking after every change.'],
  'reduce-unused-css-javascript-wordpress': ['The warning counts code not used during one page load, so it points to possible waste rather than code you can safely delete.', 'Remove plugins you don\'t need first, then load form, slider, shop and payment scripts only on the pages that use them.', 'Small savings, third-party code you rely on and pages with good real-user Core Web Vitals are usually not worth chasing.'],
  'follow-up-website-leads-fast': ['Send form alerts to a shared address and route them by service or branch, with entries saved as a backup.', 'Give every lead one named owner and follow a short, planned sequence of calls and messages before closing it politely.', 'Track status and the reason each lead was lost, and handle out-of-hours enquiries with clear hours, away messages and a morning queue.'],
  'website-calculators-lead-generation': ['Calculators suit businesses whose price depends on a few inputs visitors know, such as solar, interiors, loans and shipping.', 'Show assumptions, give ranges rather than exact figures, and test estimates against real past jobs before launch.', 'Showing an honest range and offering a detailed quote for contact details usually beats hiding the result behind a form.'],
  'website-quiz-lead-generation': ['A finder quiz gives visitors an answer, so the recommendation must be genuinely useful and honest, not an upsell.', 'Keep quizzes to a few easy, tappable questions, and cut any question whose answer does not change the result.', 'Show results first, offer extras with clear consent, and track drop-offs, results shown and sales or enquiries afterwards.'],
  'website-for-wedding-decorators': ['Organise your portfolio by function, décor element and venue, and publish client and venue photos only with permission.', 'Describe packages by scope and by what changes the quote, and explain in plain language how dates are held and advances work.', 'Give planners and venues their own page, and use a short enquiry form plus WhatsApp to capture date enquiries.'],
  'website-for-curtain-blinds-shops': ['Show real swatch photos in daylight and against the light, so customers can compare texture, colour and opacity.', 'Explain your measuring and installation process, motorised options and sample policy clearly.', 'Ask for window sizes and photos in a short form, and add a separate trade page for interior designers and builders.'],
  'website-for-upholstery-services': ['Before and after photos from the same angle, plus work-in-progress shots, are the strongest proof of upholstery quality.', 'Offer estimates from photos as a range, and explain the factors that change the final quote.', 'Spell out pickup, drop and timelines, and give cafés, hotels and offices a separate commercial page.'],
  'seo-for-new-websites': ['Get indexed first with Search Console, a sitemap and indexing requests for key pages, then move on.', 'New domains rank slowly because Google has little to go on, so build service pages before blogging and use your Business Profile for early visibility.', 'Earn early links from real relationships and judge progress by indexed pages, impressions and enquiries rather than one keyword.'],
  'google-discover-small-business': ['Discover is a personalised feed shown without a search, so its visitors are browsing rather than ready to hire.', 'Timely, helpful content with large original images, the max-image-preview:large setting and honest titles has the best chance.', 'Google doesn\'t guarantee Discover traffic, so check the Search Console report occasionally but treat it as a bonus.'],
  'seo-for-service-pages-without-blog': ['Searches that bring service work are usually won by service pages, area pages and your Google Business Profile, not blog posts.', 'Strong service pages with FAQs, plus case studies and project pages, give Google unique, relevant content without a blog.', 'Start a blog when customers research heavily, competitors rank with guides or someone can write useful articles consistently.'],
  'website-for-sports-turfs': ['Show live slot availability and keep one calendar so phone, counter and app bookings never clash.', 'State advance, cancellation and rain policies in plain language on the booking page, and apply them consistently.', 'Give corporate, league and tournament bookings their own page and enquiry form, separate from the hourly slot grid.'],
  'website-for-swimming-classes': ['Organise batches by age, ability and season so parents can quickly find the right one.', 'Describe safety, supervision and coach certifications honestly, listing only what is genuinely in place and held.', 'Publish photos of children only with written parental consent, and keep medical details off general enquiry forms.'],
  'website-for-custom-tshirt-printing': ['Explain each print method, such as screen printing, DTF and embroidery, so customers can choose the right one.', 'State minimum order quantities clearly and use a bulk quote form that asks for sizes, print positions and deadlines.', 'Make written proof approval a fixed step before printing, and give delivery timelines you can reliably meet.'],
  'wordpress-rest-api-explained': ['The REST API is built into WordPress and already powers the block editor, many plugins, WooCommerce features and app integrations.', 'Public content is readable through it by default, and the user listing can reveal login usernames, so restrict that endpoint rather than disabling the whole API.', 'Application passwords let apps connect without your main password; give each service its own, on a low-role user, and revoke ones you no longer use.'],
  'wordpress-hooks-actions-filters': ['Actions run your code at a specific moment, while filters change a value and must always return it.', 'Hooks let you customise WordPress, themes and plugins without editing their files, so your changes survive updates.', 'Keep custom code in a child theme, a small site plugin or a snippets plugin, and test any copied snippet on staging first.'],
  'wordpress-shortcodes-vs-blocks': ['Shortcodes are plain text, so removing the plugin or theme behind one leaves raw [brackets] on your pages.', 'Blocks store standard HTML in your content, so they usually survive plugin changes far better than shortcodes or page-builder widgets.', 'Migrate by listing every shortcode, swapping in plugin or core blocks, building custom blocks where needed and testing on staging.'],
  'website-for-computer-training-institutes': ['Give every course its own page with the syllabus by module, duration, teaching language, fees and the exact certificate students receive.', 'Describe certificates and affiliations precisely: your own completion certificate is fine, but claim partnerships or recognition only while you genuinely hold them.', 'Publish batch timings, verifiable student work and honest job support details, and make booking a free demo class the main action.'],
  'website-for-abacus-vedic-maths-classes': ['Explain abacus and Vedic maths on separate pages, and lay out every level with what is taught, approximate duration and how promotion works.', 'Avoid unprovable claims like boosting IQ, and report competitions precisely with the event name, organiser, year and level.', 'Franchise networks need a centre locator, a useful page for each centre and enquiries routed to the right franchisee.'],
  'website-for-pet-boarding-daycare': ['Show every area of the facility with your own photos and video, and invite owners to visit before they book.', 'Publish vaccination and health requirements, a typical daily routine, your night supervision and your real emergency and vet arrangements.', 'Make booking by date range easy, promise only the updates you can deliver, and explain what affects the price of a stay.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-optimization': ['Ask only for the fields you need, allow guest checkout and show delivery charges and dates before the last step.', 'Make UPI quick on mobile, label payment options clearly and confirm every checkout plugin supports the block or classic checkout you use.', 'Test with real low-value orders on real phones, including failed payments, and change one thing at a time.'],
  'woocommerce-sell-internationally': ['Decide whether to only display other currencies or actually charge in them, and keep the currency consistent through to invoices.', 'Plan international shipping zones, courier options and who pays customs duties, and state it clearly on product pages and at checkout.', 'Export points such as IEC, LUT and export invoices depend on your business, so confirm them with your CA before you start selling abroad.'],
  'woocommerce-pre-orders-backorders': ['Backorders are built into WooCommerce, while proper pre-orders usually need an extension or plugin.', 'Most Indian stores take full payment or an advance upfront, and should show honest expected dispatch dates everywhere.', 'Cap pre-orders at incoming stock and send matching preorder or backorder availability, with a date, to Google Merchant Center.'],
  'woocommerce-store-running-costs': ['A WooCommerce store has fixed running costs, like hosting, licences and maintenance, plus variable costs that rise with every order.', 'Payment gateway fees are usually a percentage per transaction and vary by payment method and provider, so build them into your prices.', 'Shipping, packaging, returns and COD return-to-origin charges can eat into margins, so work out the full cost of a typical order.'],
  'shared-vs-vps-vs-managed-wordpress-hosting': ['Shared hosting suits many small business sites, while VPS and cloud plans give dedicated resources but may need someone to manage the server.', 'Managed WordPress hosting looks after the server and WordPress environment, but plugins, theme and content are still your responsibility.', 'Upgrade when resource limits, slow server response or traffic peaks hurt the site, after ruling out heavy plugins and themes.'],
  'landing-page-cost-factors': ['Landing page quotes vary because scope varies: copywriting, custom or template design and the number of sections all change the work involved.', 'Forms, integrations, conversion tracking and speed work are often where quotes differ most, so ask what each one includes.', 'Send every developer the same brief covering offer, traffic source, sections, tracking and campaign changes so quotes are comparable.'],
  'wordpress-stuck-in-maintenance-mode': ['The maintenance message usually means an interrupted update left a .maintenance file in the main WordPress folder.', 'Deleting that file through your hosting file manager or FTP brings the site back, but check the update actually finished.', 'Prevent it by backing up first, updating one item at a time and keeping the tab open until updates complete.'],
  'increase-wordpress-upload-size-limit': ['WordPress takes its upload limit from PHP, using the smaller of upload_max_filesize and post_max_size, so raise both, ideally in your hosting control panel.', 'php.ini, .user.ini and .htaccess changes only work on some servers, wp-config.php cannot raise the limit, and Nginx, Cloudflare or multisite can add limits of their own.', 'Before raising the limit, check whether the file should be smaller: compressed images, embedded videos and SFTP uploads are often the better fix.'],
  'wordpress-pages-404-permalinks-fix': ['When pages 404 but the homepage works, re-saving permalinks in Settings is the first and often only fix needed.', 'A missing or damaged .htaccess file, common after migrations, stops the server passing page requests to WordPress.', 'Content types registered by a removed theme or plugin, slug clashes and bad redirects can also cause sudden 404s.'],
  'website-for-commercial-kitchen-equipment-suppliers': ['Give every product a spec-led page with dimensions, material, fuel type, capacity and a downloadable datasheet.', 'Show kitchen layout services and completed projects, naming clients only with their written permission.', 'Use detailed project quote forms and a clear AMC, service and spares section to win repeat business.'],
  'website-for-car-accessories-shops': ['Build shop-by-car pages for popular models and variants, showing photos from your own installs.', 'Make installation booking simple, with realistic time estimates and WhatsApp confirmation.', 'State coating, film and product warranties exactly as the brand does, and avoid absolute claims.'],
  'website-for-escape-room-gaming-zones': ['Give each room or game its own page with difficulty, group size, duration, age guidance and fear level.', 'Offer live slot booking with UPI or card payment and clear rescheduling and cancellation policies.', 'Describe birthday and corporate packages by what they include, and state safety rules you actually follow.'],
  'click-to-whatsapp-ads-vs-website': ['Click-to-WhatsApp ads suit simple, familiar offers and warm audiences, but only when someone can reply within minutes.', 'Without a landing page you lose website tracking, a reusable page that can rank later, and the chance to qualify leads before they message.', 'Combine both: put WhatsApp on the landing page, label ad chats by campaign, and compare routes on qualified enquiries and customers won.'],
  'video-testimonials-website': ['Ask customers soon after a good outcome, make it optional, and get written consent that says where the video will appear.', 'A phone, a quiet room and a few open questions are enough; edit out pauses but never cut or caption in a way that changes the meaning.', 'Host on YouTube with a click-to-play preview, add corrected captions and a short written quote, and place videos near the decisions they support.'],
  'qr-codes-for-business-website': ['Give every QR code one purpose and link it to a fast, mobile-friendly page that answers what the scanner wants, not your homepage.', 'Add UTM tags with a separate source for each placement, ideally through a short redirect on your own domain that you can change without reprinting.', 'Print your own domain beside each code and check displays for stuck-on fakes, so customers can spot QR scams before they tap.'],
  'website-for-ev-charging-stations': ['Give every public station its own page with accurate AC/DC type, rated kW, connectors, access hours and payment options, and explain that real charging speed depends on the vehicle.', 'Show live availability only when it comes from your charger management platform; otherwise link to your app and give a support number.', 'For installations, collect vehicle, property, parking and meter details up front, explain the site survey, and hedge tariffs and subsidies with "check current state rules".'],
  'website-for-trophy-awards-shops': ['Organise the catalogue by occasion, product, material and budget, and describe materials and finishes accurately.', 'Make written proof approval a visible step before engraving, using spreadsheets and proof sheets for bulk name lists.', 'State lead times honestly, separating in-stock engraving from custom-made medals, and publish last order dates for busy seasons.'],
  'website-for-organic-food-stores': ['Explain sourcing openly, including partner farms and any items bought in from other suppliers.', 'Claim NPOP, PGS-India or Jaivik Bharat organic status only where it is genuinely held, show certificate details per product, and check current FSSAI rules.', 'Set clear rules for subscriptions, delivery days, freshness and returns so expectations match what you can actually deliver.'],
  'white-label-woocommerce-development': ['A white-label partner handles store setup, payments and shipping for the client\'s market, product imports and custom features, while your agency keeps the client, the design and the brand.', 'The client opens payment gateway accounts in its own name and confirms tax settings with its accountant; the partner connects and configures what they decide.', 'Test every checkout path in test mode before the client review, then package ongoing store support as a commerce tier in your care plans.'],
  'offshore-wordpress-developer-time-zones': ['Write down the overlap hours, use them for calls, quick questions and live changes, and recheck them when clocks change in your country.', 'A short end-of-day update with staging links, numbered questions and blockers does most of the communication, as long as you answer before the developer\'s next day starts.', 'Agree response times by severity in writing, and prepare urgent-fix steps in advance: alerts to both sides, access ready and one emergency channel.'],
  'agency-overflow-wordpress-work': ['Overflow partners suit agencies whose busy periods come in waves; steady long-term workload may justify a hire instead.', 'Keep quality consistent by writing down coding standards, the page builder and plugin choices, and running partner builds through the same QA checklist as your own.', 'Protect client relationships with white-label basics and a written agreement reviewed by your own lawyer, and start with a small paid trial project.'],
  'wordpress-login-not-working-fix': ['Match the symptom first: a silently reloading login page usually means cookies, a cached login page or mismatched site URL settings, while lockouts and 404 login pages point to a security plugin.', 'Without dashboard access, a developer can correct the site URLs in wp-config.php or phpMyAdmin, or temporarily rename a security or caching plugin\'s folder, then re-enable it once you are in.', 'Reset forgotten passwords by email where possible, use phpMyAdmin or WP-CLI carefully with a backup, and treat unexplained password or admin changes as a sign of a hack.'],
  'wordpress-http-error-image-upload-fix': ['The "HTTP error" usually happens while WordPress resizes the image, so test with a small image first to tell size and memory problems apart from permission, security and plugin problems.', 'Resize large photos before uploading, check memory limits in Site Health, and test with GD instead of Imagick if the image library is struggling on shared hosting.', 'If every upload fails, check uploads folder permissions and disk space, then ask your host to adjust any ModSecurity rule blocking the upload rather than switching the firewall off.'],
  'wordpress-css-changes-not-showing': ['Use the browser\'s Inspect tool first: a missing rule means an old stylesheet is being served, while a crossed-out rule means another rule is overriding yours.', 'Clear layers in order: regenerate Elementor CSS, clear minified and combined files, then purge the page cache, hosting cache and CDN, and change the stylesheet version so visitors get the new file.', 'Overridden rules come down to loading order and specificity, so load child theme styles after the parent, target a class you added and keep !important for rare cases.'],
  'white-label-elementor-development': ['A white-label Elementor partner turns your agency\'s approved designs into a WordPress site behind your brand, while your agency keeps the design, the client and every conversation.', 'Set up global colours, global fonts, site settings and Theme Builder templates before building pages, then decide what the client may edit and restrict roles and templates to match.', 'Keep builds fast with a light theme, few add-ons and tested updates, and finish with branded training: a recorded walkthrough, a one-page editing guide and a clear route for questions.'],
  'wordpress-reseller-for-marketing-agencies': ['SEO and marketing agencies can resell WordPress builds, landing pages, SEO fixes and maintenance through a white-label partner, with the client dealing only with the agency.', 'Get the partner\'s written scope before quoting the client, and make the client contract mirror it, so you never promise more than the partner has agreed to deliver.', 'Margins usually slip on unscoped extras, open-ended revisions, content delays and rush jobs, so agree one change process and use it for every new request.'],
  'agency-development-retainer': ['A monthly retainer reserves a WordPress partner\'s time or an agreed scope for an agency\'s steady stream of small requests, while large builds are still quoted as separate projects.', 'Run requests through one queue with a standard template, one person setting priorities and estimates for bigger tasks, so a single request cannot quietly use up the month.', 'Agree in writing how unused time, overage and notice work, and review the retainer\'s size, request types, response times and quality every few months.'],
  'search-console-sitemap-couldnt-fetch': ['"Couldn\'t fetch" or a General HTTP error means Google didn\'t get a usable sitemap file back; it doesn\'t mean your pages have been removed from Google.', 'Open the exact submitted URL in a private window, check its status code, robots.txt and the URL Inspection live test before changing anything else.', 'If the sitemap loads cleanly, returns 200 and passes the live test, resubmit once and wait rather than resubmitting every day.'],
  'google-analytics-not-tracking-wordpress': ['Test GA4 with Realtime, DebugView and a clean browser, because standard reports take time to process and ad blockers can hide your own visits.', 'WordPress tracking failures usually come down to a missing tag, a doubled tag, the wrong measurement ID, a consent banner or a speed plugin delaying or breaking the script.', 'Use Tag Assistant to see every Google tag on the page and its ID, then confirm events arrive in DebugView and a real visit shows up in Realtime.'],
  'wordpress-site-not-showing-on-google': ['Start with a site: search and URL Inspection, whose Crawl allowed and Indexing allowed lines point to most site-wide blocks.', 'The usual culprits are the WordPress Discourage search engines box, an SEO plugin noindex setting or a Disallow rule left in robots.txt after launch.', 'If nothing is blocking the site, check Security and Manual Actions in Search Console, then help a new domain get discovered with a sitemap, indexing requests and real links.'],
  'website-for-personal-trainers': ['Say clearly who you train and where, whether in person, at home or online, and describe each package by sessions, support and validity rather than promised results.', 'List only certifications you genuinely hold, with the issuing body and year, and be clear about your scope on injuries and nutrition.', 'Use transformation photos and testimonials only with written consent, unedited and with honest context, and make the free consultation easy to book.'],
  'website-for-paint-dealers': ['Organise brand ranges by category, brand and job, use brand assets within each company\'s dealer guidelines, and claim authorised status only if appointed.', 'Point customers to brand colour tools and shade cards, explain that screen colours differ from paint on a wall, and encourage test patches.', 'Show the assumptions behind any quantity calculator, set clear terms for painter referrals, and give bulk buyers a proper enquiry form and per-store pages.'],
  'website-for-ayurvedic-product-brands': ['Confirm whether each product is treated as an Ayurvedic medicine, a cosmetic or a food before writing claims, as the rules differ for each.', 'Give full ingredient, usage and caution details, avoid cure or treatment claims, and have claims reviewed by a regulatory consultant.', 'Show only certifications you genuinely hold, with issuer, scope and validity, and grow repeat orders with fair subscriptions and honest reviews.'],
  'wordpress-403-forbidden-error-fix': ['Work out who is refusing before changing anything: the look of the error page, and whether it also happens on mobile data, point to the server, a security plugin, the host\'s firewall or a CDN.', 'Test .htaccess by renaming it and re-saving permalinks, keep folders at 755 and files at 644, make sure index.php exists and never use 777.', 'A 403 only when saving or submitting usually means a firewall rule such as ModSecurity; ask the host to allowlist that rule and send them the URL, time, your IP and a screenshot.'],
  'wordpress-500-internal-server-error-fix': ['A 500 error is deliberately vague, so read the server error log first: the time, severity, message and file path usually name the cause.', 'Turn on WP_DEBUG_LOG with WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY off, ideally writing to a path outside the public folder, then switch it off and delete the log once you have the error.', 'Common causes are a bad .htaccess line, a plugin or theme fatal error, PHP memory, a PHP version mismatch and hosting limits; work through them in order with a backup in hand.'],
  'wordpress-502-504-gateway-timeout-fix': ['A 502 means the front server got no valid reply from PHP and a 504 means it waited too long; either way the fix is usually behind the proxy, not in it.', 'Busy PHP workers are the most common cause, so get page caching working, block abusive bots and find heavy plugins, slow queries and slow outside services before upgrading.', 'Check the error page branding and your hosting resource graphs, then give your host exact times and URLs and ask about worker limits, PHP-FPM restarts and slow logs.'],
  'cloudflare-wordpress-setup-guide': ['Copy and check every DNS record before changing nameservers, keep mail records on DNS only, and set SSL/TLS to Full (strict), because Flexible causes redirect loops on WordPress.', 'Let Cloudflare cache static files by default; if you cache HTML, bypass wp-admin, wp-login.php, cart, checkout, my-account and logged-in visitors, and let one tool control each cache layer.', 'After switching, restore real visitor IPs, add light firewall rules, and test logins, forms, a test order, payment callbacks and email before calling the job done.'],
  'find-fix-broken-links-wordpress': ['Use Search Console\'s Not found (404) report to see missing URLs and a crawler to find the pages that link to them, focusing on URLs that had visits or links.', 'Fix your own links at the source, 301 redirect removed pages only to a genuine replacement, and let worthless or junk URLs return a 404 or 410 instead of sending everything to the homepage.', 'Avoid always-on link checkers that scan from your server; crawl monthly or quarterly, flatten redirect chains and decide on a redirect whenever you delete or rename a page.'],
  'wordpress-search-not-working-fix': ['Default WordPress search only looks at titles, content and excerpts with basic ordering, so custom fields, attributes and often SKUs are not found without help.', 'Check WooCommerce catalog visibility, out-of-stock hiding, the search results template\'s query and the form itself before blaming WordPress, and test with the ?s= address directly.', 'Keep search URLs and live search requests out of page and CDN caches, rebuild search indexes after imports, and use a relevance plugin or a dedicated search engine only when the catalogue needs it.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-not-working-fix': ['Start with the browser\'s Console and Network tabs: a 403, 500 or 504 on the checkout request, or a response full of PHP warnings, points straight at the cause.', 'Keep cart, checkout and account pages out of every cache layer and out of JavaScript delay or combine features, because stale pages carry expired nonces and lost sessions.', 'Find conflicts on staging with a default theme and only WooCommerce plus your gateway active, check block checkout support for every checkout plugin, and finish with a real low-value order.'],
  'woocommerce-payment-gateway-not-working-india': ['Paid orders stuck at Pending payment usually mean the return redirect failed and no working webhook was set up, so configure the webhook URL, events and secret your gateway\'s docs give.', 'Keep test and live keys and webhooks separate, set the store currency to Indian rupee, and check that firewalls, bot protection or staging passwords are not blocking the gateway.', 'Confirm a payment in the gateway dashboard before marking an order as Processing, and reconcile gateway exports against WooCommerce orders regularly.'],
  'wordpress-update-failed-fix': ['"Another update is currently in progress" is a core_updater.lock option that expires after about 15 minutes; delete only that row if it stays and no update is running.', 'Download and install failures usually come from the server: timeouts, blocked connections, expired premium licences, full disks, wrong permissions or file ownership.', 'When an update keeps failing, replace the plugin with a fresh zip or update core manually over SFTP without touching wp-content or wp-config.php, after a full backup.'],
  'wordpress-media-library-not-loading-fix': ['If list view works but the grid view spins or stays empty, the files are fine and a script conflict or a failed admin-ajax request is the likely cause, so check the browser console and Network tab.', 'For broken images after a move, open the image address directly: an old domain means a careful search-replace, a 404 means missing files and a 403 usually means permissions.', 'Check Settings, Media for leftover upload paths, treat CDN and offload plugins with care, and regenerate missing thumbnails from a backup-protected site.'],
  'indexed-though-blocked-by-robots-txt-fix': ['Robots.txt controls crawling, not indexing, so a blocked URL can still be indexed from links, and a noindex tag on a blocked page is never seen.', 'Common WordPress causes are SEO plugin robots.txt edits, a physical file overriding the virtual one, leftover staging rules, blocked wp-content or CSS and JavaScript, and broad parameter rules.', 'Fix the rule in one place, confirm the live file, use the robots.txt report and URL Inspection, then click Validate fix and allow days or weeks.'],
  'recaptcha-not-working-wordpress': ['"Invalid domain for site key" means the domain is missing from the key\'s list, and "Invalid key type" means a v2 key and a v3 integration, or vice versa, have been mixed.', 'Script delay, lazy-loading, caching and cookie consent tools often stop reCAPTCHA loading or leave tokens expired, so exclude its scripts and test in a private window.', 'Start v3 near the 0.5 threshold Google suggests, make sure submissions are verified on the server, and consider Turnstile or a honeypot as lower-friction alternatives.'],
  'wordpress-site-slow-on-mobile-fix': ['PageSpeed Insights tests mobile as a slower phone on a slower connection, so look at which metric fails on mobile and at the real-user field data rather than the score alone.', 'Heavy sliders, autoplay videos, oversized background images, third-party scripts, popups and extra fonts cost far more on a phone than on a desktop.', 'Test on an ordinary mid-range phone on mobile data, change one thing at a time, and expect field data to improve gradually over a few weeks.'],
  'woocommerce-orders-not-showing-fix': ['Decide first whether the order is hidden, never confirmed by the gateway, or fine with only the email missing, because each has a different fix.', 'Paid orders stuck at Pending payment usually mean the gateway confirmation never arrived, so check the gateway dashboard and webhooks before marking anything as Processing.', 'HPOS sync backlogs, plugins that still read the old order tables, stale caches and a stalled WP-Cron can all make orders look missing, so check these carefully and test on staging.'],
  'wordpress-admin-bar-dashboard-missing-fix': ['A missing front-end admin bar is often a profile setting, logging in on a different address, a cache serving the logged-out page, or a theme without wp_footer().', 'Missing menus and "Sorry, you are not allowed to access this page" usually mean your role or its capabilities changed, or a plugin is hiding menus.', 'After a migration that changed the table prefix, the role keys in user meta and options must match the new prefix; back up before editing the database.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-seo': ['Google treats each site in a network as a separate site, so every one needs its own SEO settings, sitemap and Search Console checks.', 'On a subdirectory network only the root robots.txt is read, so list or submit every site\'s sitemap and check each site\'s search engine visibility setting.', 'Avoid copied pages across sites, redirect old addresses after domain mapping, and use hreflang only when sites are true language versions of each other.'],
  'wordpress-hosting-price-india': ['Hosting price depends on the type of hosting, the server resources you get and how much of the work the host does for you.', 'Compare the total over three years, including renewal rates, GST, backups, email and any add-ons, not the introductory monthly price.', 'Check current pricing on each host\'s site, and weigh support quality and server location alongside cost.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-slow': ['Multisite networks slow down because every site shares one database, one set of network-activated plugins, per-site cron and one server.', 'A Redis object cache and page caching that properly supports Multisite usually make the biggest difference, followed by database clean-up on every site.', 'If one site dominates or sites need very different plugins and hosting, splitting the network can be the most effective speed fix.'],
};
